{"parentUuid":"78cc1ac0-67e0-407c-a64a-a02c5609da91","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChLWfaUTfK7qrnPHsAqJr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Status at about 21:00Z:\n\n- **#756 now carries both halves of VR-2.** Codex pushed the ACP extension (`_nexus/verify`) onto my branch while the review follow-ups for the MCP half were being applied. I merged the two; the combined branch type-checks and all ACP and service tests pass locally.\n- **First review (MCP half): accept with follow-ups.** The reviewer found no way to get VERIFIED for a weakened or broken change. Pinned-base, verifier-tampering and hostile-argument attacks all failed when run. The four follow-ups are fixed and pushed:\n  - SIGTERM or SIGINT to the server now stops the verifier and cleans up. The new test fails without the handler.\n  - A thrown error in the verify call returns a JSON-RPC error instead of an unhandled rejection.\n  - Read-only is honoured when it comes from the environment or config file, not only from flags.\n  - The guide now lists fully what the request guarantee does not cover.\n- **Second review is running** on Codex's ACP half and my follow-ups, again with a brief to break it. One point I asked it to check specifically: whether pinning the base per ACP session means a client can commit weakened tests and then open a new session. #756 does not merge before that verdict.\n- **#755 (docs)** is merged into the release branch as `c5c803b6`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3418,"cache_read_input_tokens":434315,"output_tokens":449,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3418,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"o