{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T01:35:44.954Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a83775bd5993166af</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01GBZZDseemGhaQ5u74D75ny</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a83775bd5993166af.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review PR #763 flight gaps\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES** at `748863d201a75f953121ff4703576c8481ae82aa`. The head did not move during the review. The worktree is removed.\n\n**Follow-ups**\n1. **Restart hid a trailing skip: partly fixed.**\n   - A gap marker is now written (`flight.ts` `markRecordingSkipped`, ~177), and `resumeChain` (~104) reads it back.\n   - I ran the exact sequence (`session_start, c1, [skip], restart, c3, end`). It now reports \"recording skipped…\" with the chain broken at position 3. The variants with a trailing skip and no restart, and with two skips, are also flagged.\n   - The hole remains when the marker cannot be written either. I made the append fail by putting a directory where the log file goes, then restored it and restarted. The result was \"chain intact (4 records), closed\" with `recordingSkipped: false`. That is the disk-failure case this follow-up was about.\n   - A failed marker also leaves no trace in memory: `chained=false` is set, but a restart rebuilds from disk.\n   - The marker is unchained by design. A forged marker appended to a healthy log reads as skipped and broken, so forging fails closed.\n   - Deleting the marker plus every later unchained record leaves a clean prefix. I ran this: marker deleted, restart, `c3` and `session_end` appended. It read \"intact, closed\". That matches the limit already documented for the chain (the file can be rewritten), but the marker adds no tamper evidence of its own.\n2. **Operator not told: fixed.** `process.emitWarning` fires once per recorder, with code `NEXUS_FLIGHT_RECORDING_DEGRADED`. I saw it in my probes. It is also emitted when a recorder resumes a degraded log.\n3. **A redaction throw degrades the whole session: not fixed, and not changed.** `chained=false` is still permanent after one cyclic value or BigInt. The result is now visible, but one bad input still ends chaining for the session.\n4. **Broken at export is only a note: partly fixed.**\n   - `audit_bundle.ts:1014` adds the finding `log_not_intact_at_export`. In `audit export`, then `audit verify`, a mid-log skip and a restart after a skip both return exit 1 `tampered` with that finding. A healthy bundle still returns exit 0.\n   - A skip on the first record is the gap. That log has no chained records, so its status is `unchained`, and verify returns exit 0 `intact_unsigned` with only a \"not chained when exported\" note. The marker-unwritable skip with a restart verifies exit 0 with no note.\n5. **Skip on the first record read as pre-chaining: partly fixed.** `describeIntegrity` and replay now say \"recording skipped\". `audit verify` still prints the old \"not chained… integrity cannot be shown\" note, and nothing in the bundle records the skip.\n\n**Blocker**\n- `cli/test/flight_sanitization.test.ts:157` fails at head and passes on `origin/release/v1.3.0` (3/3). Its \"classifier failure skips flight and audit persistence\" test asserts that no flightlog file exists. The new marker write creates one, so `AutonomousAgent` now leaves a durable log after a sanitizer failure. Either update the test and state this as intended, or skip the marker when no log exists yet. Merging as is breaks CI.\n\n**Non-blocking**\n- Make the gap durable even when the marker write fails, for example a sidecar or an in-record flag, or fail closed on resume when the last write failed. Without that, follow-up 1 does not hold for ENOSPC.\n- Make `audit verify` treat `unchained` plus a `recording_gap` record as a finding. This would also need the manifest to carry the skip.\n- The new finding kind extends an enum in the v1 schema. The ledger gains a new `schemaSha256` entry with `schema_version` still 1. This follows the earlier GV-2 additive precedent. A consumer that validates strictly against the old enum would reject the new kind. The bundle format itself is unchanged.\n- The new verifier now returns `tampered` for old bundles whose exported log was already broken, where it used to return `intact_unsigned`. That is intended, but `docs/AUDIT.md` should say it changes verdicts on existing bundles.\n- Marker content is fixed: `{recordingSkipped:true, reason}` only. No failed payload appears, and the test checks this for the classifier text.\n\n**Mutations** (each reverted)\n- Removing the marker write fails 2 tests in `hash_chain`. The audit files pass, so no audit test needs the marker.\n- Making verify ignore a broken-at-export log fails 1 test each in `audit_bundle`, `audit_command` and `audit_export`.\n\n**Tests at head** (fail counts: only `flight_sanitization` has any)\n\n| File | Pass |\n|---|---|\n| `hash_chain` | 28 |\n| `audit_bundle` | 24 |\n| `audit_command` | 7 |\n| `audit_export` | 18 |\n| `audit_schema` | 7 |\n| `flight_exclude` | 8 |\n| `flight_sanitization` | 2 of 3 |\n| `trace_transparency` | 14 |\n| `leak_guard` | 6 |\n| `protocol_parity` | 5 |\n| `protocol_schema` | 8 |\n\n**Tamper tests (D):** The audit test files that contain the tamper tests pass at head. I did not run a separate byte-flip probe.\n\n**Docs (G):** The `docs/AUDIT.md` and plan wording about retaining degraded status across a restart is accurate only when the marker was written. I did not check whether the plan states the marker-unwritable limit; read that paragraph before merging. I did not check an older verifier binary on a new bundle, the Node 22/24 matrix, or a signed bundle.\n\nMy probes are in `/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/p2/probe.mts` and `.../p2/export.mts`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>61820</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>679996</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
