{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T04:57:34.361Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac814f9f6c073b4b8</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01RYf9vt4wfBo1XpB2vQeekE</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/ac814f9f6c073b4b8.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review PR #770 kill tree fix\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS** at `20b50f9bbb63bf29c668d76504160db8971dad68`. At the end of my review the PR was still OPEN and the head hadn't moved.\n\n**Findings (ranked)**\n\n1. **Non-blocking, by reading. `signalTree` signals `child.pid` after the worker has been reaped** (`verify_service.ts:191, 205, 222`).\n   - The `finish` sweep (:240) always runs after the worker has exited and been reaped.\n   - The kill timers can also fire after `exit` but before `disconnect`. Both cases send SIGSTOP and SIGCONT to a pid that may have been reused. `list()` then uses that pid as a root, so the reused process's children can be stopped and killed.\n   - Linux reuses pids only after a wrap, so this is rare. In a container with a small `pid_max` and heavy churn it is not impossible.\n   - Fix: guard with `child.exitCode === null &amp;&amp; child.signalCode === null`. In the sweep, use only `survivors()` as roots and skip the worker.\n2. **Non-blocking, by reading. Pids found by the last listing are neither stopped nor signalled** (:212, :214).\n   - When the pass limit is hit, the final `targets` are never stopped or signalled. For a SIGKILL stage, those late forks escape.\n   - They are not left stopped, so this is not a stuck-T problem.\n   - Fix: `for (const pid of new Set([...stopped, ...targets])) send(pid, name)`.\n3. **Non-blocking, by reading. Two smaller points on `seen`.**\n   - `seen` is never pruned. It is bounded by one run, so this is minor.\n   - `startedAt` compares clock ticks of 10 ms, so a reused pid would need to start within the same tick. That is not realistic.\n\n**Q1 pid safety**\n- `startedAt` indexing is correct. I ran it on a process named `a) b 1 2 3` and it returned the right field.\n- A pid with undefined `startedAt` is never stored (`started !== undefined`, :197), so undefined is never compared.\n- Roots are the worker plus remembered survivors, and `descendants` follows children only. Pid 1 has ppid 0 and is filtered out, so init's other children are not pulled in. I see no path to the server or the client.\n- The only reuse hole is finding 1.\n\n**Q2 stuck-T**\n- The SIGTERM stage sends CONT to all stopped processes and the worker.\n- SIGKILL with `worker=false` kills the descendants and sends CONT to the worker.\n- SIGKILL with the worker kills everything.\n- If `ps` fails or times out, it returns an empty listing. Only the worker is stopped, and it is then continued or killed. `send` swallows errors.\n- If `child.pid` is undefined, the function returns before stopping anything.\n- Everything is synchronous, so nothing interleaves.\n- The only stuck case is the server dying mid-function.\n\n**Q3 graceful path:** the worker is stopped, then SIGTERM is queued, then CONT is sent, so the handler still runs. Both test files passed, and the scratch-removal assertions are:\n- `verify_service.test.ts:252`, after cancellation.\n- `verify_service.test.ts:288`, after the watchdog timeout, in the \"watchdog stops the worker\" test.\n- `mcp_verify.test.ts:112`, after cancellation, and :311 on SIGTERM to the server.\n\n**Q4 sweep**\n- It runs one `ps` plus a `/proc` read per remembered pid, roughly tens of milliseconds, on the event loop. The worst case is 5 s if `ps` hangs.\n- It also covers the watchdog, because the watchdog aborts the same controller (:313–315).\n- It only touches seen or descendant processes, so no legitimate leftovers are hit.\n- It is acceptable. It's the same code path as finding 1.\n\n**Q5 exit-time worst case**\n- One call makes up to 1 + 6 = 7 `ps` runs. At 5 s each, that is 35 s if `ps` hangs, and about 0.1 s normally.\n- Each concurrent run registers its own exit listener, so the bound is multiplied by the number of live runs.\n- Consider a total deadline on `onProcessExit`, or one pass only.\n\n**Q6 loops**\n\n| Run | Runs | Orphans | Stopped-forever |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| TRAP=1 | 120 | 0 | not measured |\n| TRAP=1 with my stopped check (copy at `…/scratchpad/loop_stop.mts`) | 60 | 0 | 0 |\n\nThe stopped check ran after settle and again 300 ms later.\n\n**Tests:** `verify_service.test.ts` 10/10, `mcp_verify.test.ts` 7/7.\n\n**Q7 other**\n- The comments explain why, which is good.\n- `loop.mts` and `loop_stop.mts` ran against a stale worktree path form, which I corrected, so there was no effect on results.\n- Candidate unit tests:\n  - `startedAt` parsing with a comm containing `)` and spaces.\n  - `descendants` with several roots, and with a root that has been reparented.\n  - A pid-reuse guard, once finding 1 is fixed.\n\n**Not checked:** Node 22, the Docker sandbox engine, a hanging `ps`, and pid wrap in practice. Findings 1 and 2 are by reading only.\n\nI left no sleep-marker processes and nothing in state T. The `rev770` worktree is removed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>45607</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>817546</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
