{"parentUuid":"e3808038-362f-4f5e-b194-ba0b55466a3f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMLVTYKNYijbqoidsn17","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Codex's docs commit confirms #772 fixes the filter exposure, and both PRs are still open. Since I wrote #772 and an earlier test of it was flawed, I'm starting an independent review of it now against all nine vectors.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1813,"cache_read_input_tokens":732023,"output_tokens":4088,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":583},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1813,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4088,"cache_read_input_tokens":732023,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1813},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":10,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChMLVTHwD6LiLucJDoQBc","requestRef":"70a38b52-0688-487a-8e32-d34c29143ca9","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"c5ce3b5a-f8d7-45ce-b30d-942930e96f95","timestamp":"2026-11-24T07:11:59.059Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"c5ce3b5a-f8d7-45ce-b30d-942930e96f95","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMLVTYKNYijbqoidsn17","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01N2R6mSEvdLAdtfaL95zLiY","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review PR #772 git programs check","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent security review of GitHub PR #772 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 772`, `gh pr diff 772`), \"fix(audit): do not hash a repository whose own Git configuration names a program\". Its base is another PR's branch (`codex/p10-03-audit-candidate-binding`, PR #771), not the release branch. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 772 --json headRefOid` (short `fa919263`), review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. The real change: a function `repositoryConfiguresPrograms(workspacePath)` and a constant `GIT_PROGRAM_KEYS` in `cli/src/audit_export.ts`, one call before `computeCandidateIdentitySync` in the candidate-binding block (search `workspace_configures_git_programs`), and a test \"a repository whose own Git configuration names a program is not hashed…\" at the end of `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts`.\n\nBackground: PR #771 makes `nexus audit export` compute the \"candidate identity\" of the workspace named by recorded state, which runs git there (`cli/src/core/candidate.ts`: `git rev-parse`, `git config --get core.filemode`, `git status --porcelain`, `git ls-files -v`, `git diff --name-status` with `-c core.fsmonitor=false -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null` on some). A review of #771 showed that programs configured by the repository itself then execute with the exporter's rights; nine vectors executed a planted command: `filter.<x>.clean` via `.gitattributes`; `filter.<x>.process`; `core.fsmonitor=<cmd>`; `include.path` to a file defining a filter; `includeIf` to such a file; `core.attributesFile` naming the filter; `.git/info/attributes` naming the filter; `.git` as a FILE pointing at a gitdir elsewhere whose config has `core.worktree` back at the workspace plus a filter; `core.worktree` set plus a filter. (Since then #771 added `-c core.fsmonitor=false` to `ls-files`.)\n\nThe fix under review lists the repository's own configuration with `git config --show-scope -z --get-regexp <GIT_PROGRAM_KEYS>` and treats any matching key whose scope is not `system`, `global` or `command` as a reason to refuse: the binding is `unavailable` with reason `workspace_configures_git_programs`. A listing that fails counts as a hit; a directory that is not a git work tree is not checked.\n\nYour job is to BREAK it. By running, in throwaway repos under a fresh `mktemp -d`, with harmless planted commands that write a marker file inside that directory:\n1. Re-run ALL NINE vectors above against the real export path (drive `exportAuditBundle` with a seeded subject exactly as the new test does — read the test and its helpers `savedSubject`, `setReceiptCandidateId`, `exportChecked`, `bundleTexts` in `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts`; write your probe as a separate test file copy or script in a scratch directory importing from the worktree). IMPORTANT for triggering filters: the tracked file must keep the same content and size and get a NEW modification time (`fs.utimesSync` to a later time), otherwise git does not re-hash and no filter runs; and do not run any git command yourself in the repo after planting (your own command would run the planted program). For each vector report: executed / not executed, and the binding status + reason.\n2. Bypasses of the key listing: keys the regex misses that can still run a program or redirect git during `status`/`ls-files`/`diff`/`rev-parse`/`config` (think: `core.fsmonitor` spelled with different case or as `core.fsMonitor`; `core.untrackedCache`+hooks; `core.hooksPath`; `core.alternateRefsCommand`; `core.askPass`/`credential.helper` (does status call it? no network here); `gpg.program`/`gpg.<fmt>.program`; `uploadpack.packObjectsHook`; `diff.<driver>.textconv` used by `git diff --name-status`? (name-status should not run textconv — confirm); `merge.<driver>.driver`; `pager.<cmd>`/`core.pager` with stdout not a tty; `alias.*`; `trace2.*`/`GIT_TRACE2` config `trace2.eventTarget` writing files (file write, not exec — note it); `extensions.*` such as `extensions.worktreeConfig` making `config.worktree` a scope named `worktree` (covered?); `safe.bareRepository`; `core.sparseCheckout`; `index.*`; submodules: `submodule.<name>.update=!cmd` (only on update), a registered submodule whose own `.git/modules/<name>/config` defines a filter (does `git status` in the superproject hash submodule files? it runs status in the submodule: does the submodule's config get listed by the check? try it); `core.fsmonitor` set via `includeIf \"gitdir:...\"`; an `[include]` with a relative path and with `~`; config in `$GIT_DIR/config.worktree`; `GIT_CONFIG_*`/`GIT_DIR` env are the exporter's own and out of scope.\n   Also: how does `--show-scope` label keys from an included file, from `config.worktree`, and from a gitdir reached through a `.git` file? Are any of them labelled `global`/`system`/`command` (which the check trusts)? Can a repository make its own key appear with scope `command` or `global` (e.g. via `include.path` pointing at... the scope of included files is the includer's scope — confirm)? What if the listing output is huge, or a key/value contains NUL-adjacent tricks or newlines in the subsection so the `scope NUL key LF value NUL` parsing desynchronises and a hostile record is read as scope `global` (try a subsection name containing a newline and the literal text `global`)?\n3. Does the check itself execute anything from the repository (`git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree` and `git config --get-regexp` with a hostile repo: fsmonitor/filters are not run by these, confirm with the marker; what about `core.fsmonitor` + `rev-parse`? `include` of a FIFO or a huge file making `git config` hang → there is a 10 s timeout: what does a timeout produce (status null → counted as hit?))?\n4. TOCTOU: the check runs, then the identity is computed. The workspace is attacker-influenced but is the attacker running concurrently? Note it as a limit if relevant; do not over-weigh.\n5. False refusals: a normal repo (no such keys) still gets `matched`/`mismatch`; an operator with a GLOBAL filter (set `HOME`-level `.gitconfig` in the throwaway HOME with a harmless filter) is still allowed and the comparison works; a repo with `git lfs install --local`-style keys is refused with the documented reason (that is the stated trade-off).\n6. Tests: run `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts` at head; disable the check (mutate, then `git checkout` — or better, copy the file aside and restore it) and confirm the new test fails with \"the configured command ran\" for the clean-filter case; confirm each of the five cases in the test really triggers its program when the check is disabled (add temporary logging in a COPY of the test in your scratch dir rather than editing the worktree's file if you can).\n7. Run at head, one file at a time: `audit_export`, `audit_command`, `audit_bundle`, `audit_schema`, `leak_guard`, `candidate_git_paths`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`.\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev772 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev772/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/rev772/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev772`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Every run uses a throwaway HOME (`mktemp -d`): `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test <file> 2>&1 | tail -30`, foreground, timeout at most 9 minutes (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus, and never touch the real ~/.gitconfig (git reads `$HOME/.gitconfig`, so with a throwaway HOME the global config is empty unless you write one there). No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops. Aim for about 45 minutes; an experiment that fails to converge twice is \"not confirmed\".\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 600 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; a table of every vector tried with executed / not executed and the binding result; bypasses found, ranked, blocking vs not; false-refusal results; mutation results; test counts; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1813,"cache_read_input_tokens":732023,"output_tokens":4088,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":583},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1813,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4088,"cache_read_input_tokens":732023,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1813},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":32991,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01N2R6mSEvdLAdtfaL95zLiY":{"description":"Review PR #772 git programs check","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent security review of GitHub PR #772 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 772`, `gh pr diff 772`), \"fix(audit): do not hash a repository whose own Git configuration names a program\". Its base is another PR's branch (`codex/p10-03-audit-candidate-binding`, PR #771), not the release branch. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 772 --json headRefOid` (short `fa919263`), review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. The real change: a function `repositoryConfiguresPrograms(workspacePath)` and a constant `GIT_PROGRAM_KEYS` in `cli/src/audit_export.ts`, one call before `computeCandidateIdentitySync` in the candidate-binding block (search `workspace_configures_git_programs`), and a test \"a repository whose own Git configuration names a program is not hashed…\" at the end of `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts`.\n\nBackground: PR #771 makes `nexus audit export` compute the \"candidate identity\" of the workspace named by recorded state, which runs git there (`cli/src/core/candidate.ts`: `git rev-parse`, `git config --get core.filemode`, `git status --porcelain`, `git ls-files -v`, `git diff --name-status` with `-c core.fsmonitor=false -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null` on some). A review of #771 showed that programs configured by the repository itself then execute with the exporter's rights; nine vectors executed a planted command: `filter.<x>.clean` via `.gitattributes`; `filter.<x>.process`; `core.fsmonitor=<cmd>`; `include.path` to a file defining a filter; `includeIf` to such a file; `core.attributesFile` naming the filter; `.git/info/attributes` naming the filter; `.git` as a FILE pointing at a gitdir elsewhere whose config has `core.worktree` back at the workspace plus a filter; `core.worktree` set plus a filter. (Since then #771 added `-c core.fsmonitor=false` to `ls-files`.)\n\nThe fix under review lists the repository's own configuration with `git config --show-scope -z --get-regexp <GIT_PROGRAM_KEYS>` and treats any matching key whose scope is not `system`, `global` or `command` as a reason to refuse: the binding is `unavailable` with reason `workspace_configures_git_programs`. A listing that fails counts as a hit; a directory that is not a git work tree is not checked.\n\nYour job is to BREAK it. By running, in throwaway repos under a fresh `mktemp -d`, with harmless planted commands that write a marker file inside that directory:\n1. Re-run ALL NINE vectors above against the real export path (drive `exportAuditBundle` with a seeded subject exactly as the new test does — read the test and its helpers `savedSubject`, `setReceiptCandidateId`, `exportChecked`, `bundleTexts` in `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts`; write your probe as a separate test file copy or script in a scratch directory importing from the worktree). IMPORTANT for triggering filters: the tracked file must keep the same content and size and get a NEW modification time (`fs.utimesSync` to a later time), otherwise git does not re-hash and no filter runs; and do not run any git command yourself in the repo after planting (your own command would run the planted program). For each vector report: executed / not executed, and the binding status + reason.\n2. Bypasses of the key listing: keys the regex misses that can still run a program or redirect git during `status`/`ls-files`/`diff`/`rev-parse`/`config` (think: `core.fsmonitor` spelled with different case or as `core.fsMonitor`; `core.untrackedCache`+hooks; `core.hooksPath`; `core.alternateRefsCommand`; `core.askPass`/`credential.helper` (does status call it? no network here); `gpg.program`/`gpg.<fmt>.program`; `uploadpack.packObjectsHook`; `diff.<driver>.textconv` used by `git diff --name-status`? (name-status should not run textconv — confirm); `merge.<driver>.driver`; `pager.<cmd>`/`core.pager` with stdout not a tty; `alias.*`; `trace2.*`/`GIT_TRACE2` config `trace2.eventTarget` writing files (file write, not exec — note it); `extensions.*` such as `extensions.worktreeConfig` making `config.worktree` a scope named `worktree` (covered?); `safe.bareRepository`; `core.sparseCheckout`; `index.*`; submodules: `submodule.<name>.update=!cmd` (only on update), a registered submodule whose own `.git/modules/<name>/config` defines a filter (does `git status` in the superproject hash submodule files? it runs status in the submodule: does the submodule's config get listed by the check? try it); `core.fsmonitor` set via `includeIf \"gitdir:...\"`; an `[include]` with a relative path and with `~`; config in `$GIT_DIR/config.worktree`; `GIT_CONFIG_*`/`GIT_DIR` env are the exporter's own and out of scope.\n   Also: how does `--show-scope` label keys from an included file, from `config.worktree`, and from a gitdir reached through a `.git` file? Are any of them labelled `global`/`system`/`command` (which the check trusts)? Can a repository make its own key appear with scope `command` or `global` (e.g. via `include.path` pointing at... the scope of included files is the includer's scope — confirm)? What if the listing output is huge, or a key/value contains NUL-adjacent tricks or newlines in the subsection so the `scope NUL key LF value NUL` parsing desynchronises and a hostile record is read as scope `global` (try a subsection name containing a newline and the literal text `global`)?\n3. Does the check itself execute anything from the repository (`git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree` and `git config --get-regexp` with a hostile repo: fsmonitor/filters are not run by these, confirm with the marker; what about `core.fsmonitor` + `rev-parse`? `include` of a FIFO or a huge file making `git config` hang → there is a 10 s timeout: what does a timeout produce (status null → counted as hit?))?\n4. TOCTOU: the check runs, then the identity is computed. The workspace is attacker-influenced but is the attacker running concurrently? Note it as a limit if relevant; do not over-weigh.\n5. False refusals: a normal repo (no such keys) still gets `matched`/`mismatch`; an operator with a GLOBAL filter (set `HOME`-level `.gitconfig` in the throwaway HOME with a harmless filter) is still allowed and the comparison works; a repo with `git lfs install --local`-style keys is refused with the documented reason (that is the stated trade-off).\n6. Tests: run `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts` at head; disable the check (mutate, then `git checkout` — or better, copy the file aside and restore it) and confirm the new test fails with \"the configured command ran\" for the clean-filter case; confirm each of the five cases in the test really triggers its program when the check is disabled (add temporary logging in a COPY of the test in your scratch dir rather than editing the worktree's file if you can).\n7. Run at head, one file at a time: `audit_export`, `audit_command`, `audit_bundle`, `audit_schema`, `leak_guard`, `candidate_git_paths`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`.\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev772 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev772/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/rev772/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev772`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Every run uses a throwaway HOME (`mktemp -d`): `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test <file> 2>&1 | tail -30`, foreground, timeout at most 9 minutes (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus, and never touch the real ~/.gitconfig (git reads `$HOME/.gitconfig`, so with a throwaway HOME the global config is empty unless you write one there). No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops. Aim for about 45 minutes; an experiment that fails to converge twice is \"not confirmed\".\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 600 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; a table of every vector tried with executed / not executed and the binding result; bypasses found, ranked, blocking vs not; false-refusal results; mutation results; test counts; what you did not check."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011ChMLVTHwD6LiLucJDoQBc","requestRef":"70a38b52-0688-487a-8e32-d34c29143ca9","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"d868b0ef-753b-4ae2-9ad4-4c547a3ff973","timestamp":"2026-11-24T07:12:32.176Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
