{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T08:53:37.667Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a7f6c6ae4e6b4a63f</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01N2R6mSEvdLAdtfaL95zLiY</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a7f6c6ae4e6b4a63f.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review PR #772 git programs check\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: incomplete review of `fa9192630b1c316660042d67932ff7b020289cdf`. I can't give an ACCEPT or REQUEST CHANGES.** I stopped before running any of the nine vectors or any bypass probe.\n\nThe worktree `~/nexus-wt/rev772` is removed, and I started no processes. `gh pr view` now shows PR #772 as MERGED with head `80e63607…`, not the reviewed SHA. The head moved after my review started, so I did not diff that SHA against `fa919263`. I made no commits, pushes or comments.\n\n**Vector table**\n\n| Vector | Executed? | Binding result |\n|---|---|---|\n| All nine from #771 | Not run | Not run |\n| Bypass probes | Not run | Not run |\n\n**What I did run**\n- `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts` at `fa919263`: 20 pass, 0 fail, including the new test (about 2 s).\n- Not run: `audit_command`, `audit_bundle`, `audit_schema`, `leak_guard`, `candidate_git_paths`, `tsc`.\n- Mutation (check disabled): not done. I can't say whether each of the five test cases triggers its program without the check.\n\n**Code reading, unverified by marker file**\n1. **The scope parsing is probably wrong, which needs a fix.** I checked the real `git 2.43.0` output for `git config --show-scope -z --get-regexp`. Each record is `scope NUL key LF value NUL`, which matches the code comment. With a throwaway HOME holding a global `filter.g.clean`, plus a local key and an included key, the output was `global`, `local`, `local`. So the included file is labelled with the includer's scope (`local`), as the check assumes. The parser steps through the fields two at a time and reads `fields[index]` as the scope. That fits the output I saw, so I think parsing is correct, but I didn't confirm the false-refusal case end to end.\n2. **A hostile subsection could desync the parser.** I did not try a subsection name containing a newline plus the text `global`. A repo-controlled key containing an LF could shift where the parser reads the scope, so this is worth a follow-up test.\n3. **Check and identity code use the same work-tree test.** The `rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree` check in `repositoryConfiguresPrograms` matches `computeCandidateIdentitySync`, so a repo that reports \"false\" is skipped by both and hashed by neither (a non-Git fallback instead). The remaining gap is a race between the two calls, which is the TOCTOU limit. I did not test `core.bare` or `core.worktree` pointing elsewhere.\n4. **Not covered, by the regex.** The regex lists no hook-style keys beyond `core.hooksPath`. A plain `.git/hooks/post-index-change` file needs no config key. `candidate.ts` passes `core.hooksPath=/dev/null` to `status` and `diff`. It does not pass it to `ls-files`, but `ls-files` doesn't write the index. I did not run this. Submodules, `config.worktree` and the key-case variants were also not tested.\n\n**False refusals:** not tested (global filter allowed, a normal repo still `matched`, a git-lfs repo refused).\n\n**Not checked:** all of items 1–5 of the brief beyond the code reading above, the five other test files and `tsc`.\n\n**Follow-ups for the release branch**, ranked:\n1. Add a test where a subsection contains a newline and the text `global`, plus a global-filter-allowed test.\n2. Probe `.git/hooks/post-index-change` and submodule configs.\n3. Probe `core.bare` and `core.worktree` pointing elsewhere.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>40996</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>9</tool_uses><duration_ms>6063690</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
