versioning rule for the ACP adapter schema (look at how v2 and v3 were added in `ACP_ADAPTER_VERSIONS.json` and `cli/test/acp_schema.test.ts`: earlier versions' files must stay byte-identical; add a new version if the result shape changes).\n\n3. Id-less `_nexus/receipt/get` must return TURN receipts only (plan decision 7: existing clients see no change). Today `latestReceipts` mixes verification receipts in, and a test asserts that. Keep verification receipts readable only by explicit id, bound to the session. Update the test: after a `_nexus/verify`, id-less get still returns the last turn receipt (or the existing \"not available\" error when there is none) and get-by-id returns the verification receipt.\n\n4. Interlock tests (a mutation showed they are untested): `_nexus/verify` while a prompt is running on that session is refused and nothing runs; `_nexus/verify` while the session is `stopping` is refused; `session/prompt` while a verification is running is refused; `_nexus/verify` in read-only mode is refused with no receipt; a cross-session receipt read uses a SECOND REAL ACTIVE session on the same connection and must fail. Confirm by mutation that removing the verify-during-prompt check fails a test (copy the source file aside and restore it from the copy — do not use `git checkout` to undo a mutation while you have uncommitted work).\n\n5. Shutdown bound: `ACP_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS` (10 s) equals the verify kill grace, so on a signal the process can exit before a running verification's worker has cleaned up. While a verification is running, the signal deadline must be at least the verify kill grace + 5 s (see what `McpServer.startStdio` in `cli/src/mcp.ts` does). Keep the existing stop semantics in `runAcpStdio`; only lengthen the bound when a verification is in flight. Add a test if cheap (there is a `shutdownGraceMs` option and existing signal tests in `cli/test/acp_review_fixes.test.ts`); otherwise say it is untested.\n\n6. `_meta` on `_nexus/verify`: make it consistent with how the adapter treats `_meta` on its other methods (check `cli/src/acp/validate.ts`) and state the behaviour in `docs/ACP.md`.\n\nDocs, honestly, in `docs/ACP.md` (the `_nexus/verify` section) and `docs/evidence/v1.3.0/VR2.md`: the client chooses the directory a session works in, so a verification covers that directory; the operator's verifier command and deliverable list are fixed at start-up and applied to the session's directory (read `cli/src/config.ts` near `normalizeTrustedDeliverableFiles` and the worker to describe exactly how deliverables resolve); the result and receipt name the workspace and base; the base is pinned per workspace for the life of the process, so commits made before the first session on that directory are the baseline and restarting `nexus acp` re-pins; the same-user limit described in `docs/MCP_GUIDE.md` applies. In the evidence doc replace any test count you cannot reproduce with counts from your own run.\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/vr2f/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. Never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference, no network.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; clear every timer and stop every child you start by its own pid (never `pkill`).\n- Run at the end, one at a time: `acp_stdio`, `acp_validate`, `acp_schema`, `acp_mapping`, `acp_review_fixes`, `acp_client_conformance`, `acp_jsonrpc`, `verify_service`, `leak_guard`, any new test file; `node cli/scripts/gen_acp_schema.mjs --check` (or its actual check invocation); `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts` from the repo root (a CI gate: listed critical functions must not grow in complexity). Run `acp_stdio` three times in a row to check for flakes.\n- The recorded real-client fixtures in `cli/test/fixtures/acp/clients/` must keep replaying (`acp_client_conformance`).\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the spec.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n\nReport (under 450 words): per item: file:line of the change, the tests added, and anything left open; exact test results per file; the mutation result; gates run; deviations and why.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/aae4846fd8cf474b8.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9ad2de88-875c-4856-9aee-f9a974c2cd19","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code/cli","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"540c0596-4d16-4593-842e-6214128bc558","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMUWVqBaQdhvtjpB9rzU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01284R4pgJy7JFABQT3TK9xb","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement GV-1 slices 3-4 and GV-5","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Finish units GV-1 (slices 3 and 4) and GV-5 (MCP and plugin allowlists) of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 plan. Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/gv1c` (branch `v1/gv1-slices-3-4`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not commit, push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where a detail is open choose the conservative (stricter, fail-closed) option and list it in your report.\n\nRead first, in this order: the unit rows in `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` (search \"GV-1\", \"GV-5\", gate \"G-3\", decision \"D16\"); the design plan `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/GV1_PLAN.md` (it defines the slices; slices 1 and 2 are merged); `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` on this branch, which marks precisely what is \"not yet\" or \"planned\" — that list is your work list; the merged implementation `cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts` (library) and `cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts` (enforcement) and their tests (`cli/test/managed_policy*.test.ts`, `cli/test/policy_runtime*.test.ts` or similarly named: find them).\n\nScope (confirm each against `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` and the plan; if the doc lists further \"not yet\"/\"planned\" items in the same area, include them or state why not):\nA. GV-5 allowlists: `mcp.allow`, `mcp.disable_workspace`, `plugins.allow`, `plugins.disable_workspace` are accepted by the schema and enforce nothing. Enforce them where MCP servers are configured/connected (`cli/src/mcp.ts`: `mcpConfigFiles`, the manager that loads servers) and where plugins are discovered/loaded (find the plugin loader). A server or plugin not on the allowlist is not started/loaded and is reported with the policy source; `disable_workspace` ignores workspace-level MCP/plugin definitions. A project/workspace policy can only tighten, never loosen, the system policy (the library already merges that way: reuse it). Fail closed on an unreadable policy.\nB. GV-1 slice 3: the TUI \"always approve\" answer under `approval.disable_auto` (documented as a gap: an \"always\" answer must not switch the session to auto when policy forbids it; see `cli/src/sidecar.ts` approval handling and the REPL in `cli/src/repl.ts`), and `sandbox.*` policy reaching `nexus verify --sandbox` (documented as \"does not reach … yet\").\nC. GV-1 slice 4: `nexus policy` commands (at least `nexus policy check` to validate and show the effective policy with the source of each value, and whatever else GV1_PLAN.md specifies for the CLI), and `nexus doctor` findings for policy (a configuration finding per GV1_PLAN.md; `nexus doctor --fix` must not modify a policy file).\nD. Anything `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` marks \"not yet\" for `egress.secret_destinations` or similar: implement if GV1_PLAN.md puts it in slices 3–4; otherwise leave it marked and say so.\nThen update `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` so every row says what is now true (no \"not yet\" for things you implemented; keep it for what you did not), the user manual/CLI reference if commands were added, and any ledger the repo requires for a new command or setting (`node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`, `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, the doc-command check `scripts/doc_commands.mjs` used by the package smoke test — find what applies and make it pass).\n\nConstraints:\n- Other agents hold file claims on `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts` for unrelated work. You may edit them, but keep each edit to a self-contained block (a new `program.command(\"policy\")` block; new doctor findings in their own function) and do not reformat or move existing code, so the merge is clean.\n- A CI gate fails if these functions grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting: `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl`, `driveOperation` (see `cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). If you need behaviour inside one of them, put the logic in a helper and call it without adding a branch there, and run the gate: from the repo root `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Tool descriptions offered to the model have a size budget (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`, `cli/test/lsp_shape.test.ts`): do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions.\n- Security posture: policy can only restrict; an invalid or unreadable system policy is exit 2 / refusal, never \"no policy\"; nothing a model, a tool result or a workspace file says may loosen it. Test the hostile cases: a workspace policy trying to add to an allowlist, a server name with odd characters or case differences matching an allowlist entry it should not, a symlinked workspace MCP config, an allowlist that is empty (allows nothing) versus absent (no restriction — check what the library defines and follow it).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/gv1c/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME is mandatory; never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus` (tests point the policy loader at temp paths the way the existing policy tests do). No live inference, no network.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo. If the TUI (Rust, `core/`) would need changes for item B, do NOT change Rust: implement the enforcement on the TypeScript sidecar side so the TUI cannot switch the mode, and report that the TUI text is unchanged.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; clear timers and stop children by their own pid (never `pkill`). Stress new async tests: run each new file 3 times.\n- Run at the end, one at a time: every new or changed test file; the existing managed-policy and policy-runtime test files; `mcp.test.ts`, `mcp_verify.test.ts` (about 3 minutes), `verify_command`, `verify_sandbox`, `doctor.test.ts` (11 tests of it fail on this host on the base too — compare against `origin/release/v1.3.0` if you see failures and report only differences), `approval_policy`, `sidecar`, `context_convergence`, `check_requirements`, `leak_guard`, `release_workflow_parity`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the spec; nothing speculative.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n- If the work is too large to finish in one go, finish complete, tested pieces in this order: A (allowlists), C (policy check command + doctor finding), B (TUI always gap, sandbox to verify), D; and report exactly what is done and what is not. Never leave a half-wired setting that the docs would describe as enforced.\n\nReport (under 550 words): per item A–D: what is implemented, files and key line references, tests added (names), what is left; exact test results per file; gates run and their results; behaviour changes a release note must mention; deviations from GV1_PLAN.md and why."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_read_input_tokens":755841,"output_tokens":12274,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_cre