osed) option and list it in your report.\n\nRead first, in this order: the unit rows in `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` (search \"GV-1\", \"GV-5\", gate \"G-3\", decision \"D16\"); the design plan `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/GV1_PLAN.md` (it defines the slices; slices 1 and 2 are merged); `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` on this branch, which marks precisely what is \"not yet\" or \"planned\" — that list is your work list; the merged implementation `cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts` (library) and `cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts` (enforcement) and their tests (`cli/test/managed_policy*.test.ts`, `cli/test/policy_runtime*.test.ts` or similarly named: find them).\n\nScope (confirm each against `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` and the plan; if the doc lists further \"not yet\"/\"planned\" items in the same area, include them or state why not):\nA. GV-5 allowlists: `mcp.allow`, `mcp.disable_workspace`, `plugins.allow`, `plugins.disable_workspace` are accepted by the schema and enforce nothing. Enforce them where MCP servers are configured/connected (`cli/src/mcp.ts`: `mcpConfigFiles`, the manager that loads servers) and where plugins are discovered/loaded (find the plugin loader). A server or plugin not on the allowlist is not started/loaded and is reported with the policy source; `disable_workspace` ignores workspace-level MCP/plugin definitions. A project/workspace policy can only tighten, never loosen, the system policy (the library already merges that way: reuse it). Fail closed on an unreadable policy.\nB. GV-1 slice 3: the TUI \"always approve\" answer under `approval.disable_auto` (documented as a gap: an \"always\" answer must not switch the session to auto when policy forbids it; see `cli/src/sidecar.ts` approval handling and the REPL in `cli/src/repl.ts`), and `sandbox.*` policy reaching `nexus verify --sandbox` (documented as \"does not reach … yet\").\nC. GV-1 slice 4: `nexus policy` commands (at least `nexus policy check` to validate and show the effective policy with the source of each value, and whatever else GV1_PLAN.md specifies for the CLI), and `nexus doctor` findings for policy (a configuration finding per GV1_PLAN.md; `nexus doctor --fix` must not modify a policy file).\nD. Anything `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` marks \"not yet\" for `egress.secret_destinations` or similar: implement if GV1_PLAN.md puts it in slices 3–4; otherwise leave it marked and say so.\nThen update `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` so every row says what is now true (no \"not yet\" for things you implemented; keep it for what you did not), the user manual/CLI reference if commands were added, and any ledger the repo requires for a new command or setting (`node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`, `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, the doc-command check `scripts/doc_commands.mjs` used by the package smoke test — find what applies and make it pass).\n\nConstraints:\n- Other agents hold file claims on `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts` for unrelated work. You may edit them, but keep each edit to a self-contained block (a new `program.command(\"policy\")` block; new doctor findings in their own function) and do not reformat or move existing code, so the merge is clean.\n- A CI gate fails if these functions grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting: `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl`, `driveOperation` (see `cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). If you need behaviour inside one of them, put the logic in a helper and call it without adding a branch there, and run the gate: from the repo root `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Tool descriptions offered to the model have a size budget (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`, `cli/test/lsp_shape.test.ts`): do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions.\n- Security posture: policy can only restrict; an invalid or unreadable system policy is exit 2 / refusal, never \"no policy\"; nothing a model, a tool result or a workspace file says may loosen it. Test the hostile cases: a workspace policy trying to add to an allowlist, a server name with odd characters or case differences matching an allowlist entry it should not, a symlinked workspace MCP config, an allowlist that is empty (allows nothing) versus absent (no restriction — check what the library defines and follow it).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/gv1c/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME is mandatory; never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus` (tests point the policy loader at temp paths the way the existing policy tests do). No live inference, no network.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo. If the TUI (Rust, `core/`) would need changes for item B, do NOT change Rust: implement the enforcement on the TypeScript sidecar side so the TUI cannot switch the mode, and report that the TUI text is unchanged.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; clear timers and stop children by their own pid (never `pkill`). Stress new async tests: run each new file 3 times.\n- Run at the end, one at a time: every new or changed test file; the existing managed-policy and policy-runtime test files; `mcp.test.ts`, `mcp_verify.test.ts` (about 3 minutes), `verify_command`, `verify_sandbox`, `doctor.test.ts` (11 tests of it fail on this host on the base too — compare against `origin/release/v1.3.0` if you see failures and report only differences), `approval_policy`, `sidecar`, `context_convergence`, `check_requirements`, `leak_guard`, `release_workflow_parity`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the spec; nothing speculative.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n- If the work is too large to finish in one go, finish complete, tested pieces in this order: A (allowlists), C (policy check command + doctor finding), B (TUI always gap, sandbox to verify), D; and report exactly what is done and what is not. Never leave a half-wired setting that the docs would describe as enforced.\n\nReport (under 550 words): per item A–D: what is implemented, files and key line references, tests added (names), what is left; exact test results per file; gates run and their results; behaviour changes a release note must mention; deviations from GV1_PLAN.md and why.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/ab08d468d59756d02.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cab20743-2814-4989-8f4d-71992ee52154","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code/cli","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"505c140d-c0d0-42d3-bb0e-1087109ab529","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMUWVqBaQdhvtjpB9rzU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WxAHM337b5m8R97TLcS9CA","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Plan BG-1, BG-2 and HK units","subagent_type":"architect","prompt":"Write implementation plans for five units of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release: BG-1, BG-2 (background/unattended runs) and HK-1, HK-2, HK-3 (hooks and plugins). Write two files and nothing else: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md` and `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/HK_PLAN.md`. Do not edit code. Work read-only against `git -C ~/nexus-code show origin/release/v1.3.0:<path>` / `git -C ~/nexus-code grep <pattern> origin/release/v1.3.0 -- <paths>` (fetch first). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided take the plan's stated default, or else the conservative option, and record it under \"Decisions taken\".\n\nSources: `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` on that branch (read it all: unit rows BG-1, BG-2, BG-3, HK-1, HK-2, HK-3; gates G-3, G-4, G-6; decisions D15, D18, D19, D21 with their defaults; the phase ordering; the peer-project notes at the top). BG-3 is deferred to v1.4 by the plan: do not plan it. Existing plans for style and depth: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/AC1_PLAN.md`, `VR2_PLAN.md`, `GV1_PLAN.md`. Existing building blocks to reuse (verify each by reading; cite file:line): the sidecar server and protocol (`cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/protocol.ts`), `ApplicationCore` (`cli/src/core/app.ts`), `nexus run --json` and its contract (`cli/src/run_contract.ts`, `docs/protocol/RUN_RESULT_VERSIONS.json`), durable state and resume (`cli/src/core/state.ts`, session/task state), operations and goals (`cli/src/core/operation*.ts`, `cli/src/goal*.ts`, PA-1 resource admission), worktrees (`cli/src/worktree.ts` / worktrunk), approvals and modes (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`, managed policy `cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts`: note `approval.strict`/`disable_auto`), verification (`cli/src/core/verify*.ts`, verification-path protection), the flight recorder and audit bundles, MCP and plugin loading (`cli/src/mcp.ts`, the plugin loader: find it), the execution spine and `dispatchToolCallImpl` (`cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/spine.ts`), the P10 budgets (invocation, inference submission, turn deadline), `nexus doctor`.\n\nHard constraints every plan must respect:\n- The approval gates, verification-path protection, managed policy and the VERIFIED outcome ladder are authorities no new surface may weaken. A hook's or plugin's \"allow\" must have no effect on a gate denial (HK-1 acceptance); an unattended run must never grant allow-always, must run in a fresh worktree, and a seeded failing verifier must end BLOCKED, not VERIFIED (BG-2/G-4).\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`): new behaviour hooks in through a helper call that adds no branch there, or outside those functions.\n- Model-facing tool descriptions have a size budget (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`): prefer no new model-facing tools.\n- Every new CLI command, setting, protocol message or file format needs: docs (G-6 wants a runnable example per new surface, run in CI), a schema/version ledger entry where the repo keeps one, tests that fail when the guard is removed, and the hostile cases for G-3 (client, hook, plugin, schedule, policy each trying to bypass a gate).\n- Tests run with a throwaway HOME, no network, no live inference (there is a scripted gateway fixture `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`); new test files register `cli/test/helpers/leak_guard.ts`; child processes are stopped by pid.\n- Linux x86_64 is the only release target.\n\nFor EACH unit give: the acceptance criterion verbatim from the plan; what exists that it builds on (file:line); the design (data model, commands/flags, protocol or file formats with exact shapes, state on disk and its permissions, lifecycle, failure and crash behaviour, limits and timeouts); the security analysis (who is untrusted, what they control, each gate they could try to bypass and why they cannot); the exact list of files to add/change; the tests by name with what each proves, including the hostile ones and the kill/restart ones; docs and ledgers; and 2–4 mergeable slices in order, each independently green, with rough size (S/M/L) and the files it touches, flagging slices that touch `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/run_turn.ts` or `cli/src/sidecar.ts` (hot files shared with other work). Keep each unit to what its acceptance needs: state explicitly what is out of scope for v1.3.0. Where two units share machinery (BG-1's detached runner and BG-2's unattended policy and schedule; HK-1's hook points and HK-2's plugin install/verify and HK-3's per-plugin trust), design it once and say which slice introduces it.\n\nSpecific questions the plans must answer:\n- BG-1 (\"kill the CLI; the run continues and resumes\"): what process owns a detached run (a supervisor? the sidecar re-parented? systemd-run --user? plain double-fork with a pidfile and a unix socket?), how a client attaches/detaches/lists/stops, where output and state live, how identity and liveness are established without pid-reuse mistakes (the repo just fixed several of those: record pid + kernel start time), what happens on machine reboot, how the P10 budgets and the turn deadline apply, and how it interacts with PF-3 (a unix-socket transport that is a separate unit: can BG-1 ship with attach-by-log-and-state only, leaving live attach to PF-3?).\n- BG-2 (\"seeded failing verifier ends BLOCKED; unattended approval denied\"): the unattended approval policy (default deny for anything that would prompt; no allow-always; `--yes`/auto refused or clamped per D19 and managed policy), fresh-worktree requirement, what \"schedule\" means in v1.3.0 (D21: event triggers and SSH execution — take the default; if scheduling proper is deferred, say exactly what small scheduling surface, if any, the acceptance needs), and the receipt/audit trail of an unattended run.\n- HK-1 (\"a hook's allow has no effect\"): which hook points exist (pre-tool, post-tool, on-stop? keep it minimal), how a hook is declared (operator config only? project config with trust?), what a hook may return (deny / annotate only — never allow), execution model (subprocess with a timeout, env, stdin JSON, sandboxed?), failure mode (hook error = deny or ignore? choose fail-closed for pre-tool), and how it attaches to dispatch without adding branches to `dispatchToolCallImpl`.\n- HK-2 (\"commit-pinned plugin verify\") and HK-3 (\"per-plugin trust\"): D18's default on in-process plugins; what a plugin is today in this repo (read the loader); install from a git URL pinned to a commit with a recorded digest, verify on load, refuse on mismatch; trust records per plugin (where stored, how approved, how revoked); how GV-5's `plugins.allow` / `plugins.disable_workspace` interact (another agent is implementing GV-5 enforcement now: say what you assume it provides).\n\nAlso record per plan: reference-coding note per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md` (what peer projects do — the plan's own notes at the top of PLAN.md cite some; do not spend long; \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" plus what you used is acceptable), risks, and open questions that could not be settled from the code.\n\nShell notes: zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output is filtered by `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` when a grep/sed pipeline looks odd). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus.\n\nReturn (under 600 words): the two file paths; for each unit the slice list with sizes and hot files; the decisions taken; the main risks; anything you could not determine. Be concrete enough that an implementer can start slice 1 of each unit from the plan alone."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_read_input_tokens":755841,"output_tokens":12274,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":12274,"cache_read_input_tokens":755841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":31820,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WxAHM337b5m8R97TLcS9CA":{"description":"Plan BG-1, BG-2 and HK units","subagent_type":"architect","prompt":"Write implementation plans for five units of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release: BG-1, BG-2 (background/unattended runs) and HK-1, HK-2, HK-3 (hooks and plugins). Write two files and nothing else: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md` and `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/HK_PLAN.md`. Do not edit code. Work read-only against `git -C ~/nexus-code show origin/release/v1.3.0:<path>` / `git -C ~/nexus-code grep <pattern> origin/release/v1.3.0 -- <paths>` (fetch first). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided take the plan's stated default, or else the conservative option, and record it under \"Decisions taken\".\n\nSources: `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` on that branch (read it all: unit rows BG-1, BG-2, BG-3, HK-1, HK-2, HK-3; gates G-3, G-4, G-6; decisions D15, D18, D19, D21 with their defaults; the phase ordering; the peer-project notes at the top). BG-3 is deferred to v1.4 by the plan: do not plan it. Existing plans for style and depth: `/h