{"parentUuid":"511cc77a-b99e-4a0a-ab8b-0b33617822ed","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ba971b77-d93c-42dd-b3f3-8cc7229f5e40","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WxAHM337b5m8R97TLcS9CA","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: ac19cad41192e3f0a (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'ac19cad41192e3f0a', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/ac19cad41192e3f0a.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"1ac2580e-7cbb-4c68-9d11-34318da5a862","timestamp":"2026-11-24T08:58:25.919Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"ac19cad41192e3f0a","description":"Plan BG-1, BG-2 and HK units","resolvedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","prompt":"Write implementation plans for five units of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release: BG-1, BG-2 (background/unattended runs) and HK-1, HK-2, HK-3 (hooks and plugins). Write two files and nothing else: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md` and `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/HK_PLAN.md`. Do not edit code. Work read-only against `git -C ~/nexus-code show origin/release/v1.3.0:<path>` / `git -C ~/nexus-code grep <pattern> origin/release/v1.3.0 -- <paths>` (fetch first). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided take the plan's stated default, or else the conservative option, and record it under \"Decisions taken\".\n\nSources: `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` on that branch (read it all: unit rows BG-1, BG-2, BG-3, HK-1, HK-2, HK-3; gates G-3, G-4, G-6; decisions D15, D18, D19, D21 with their defaults; the phase ordering; the peer-project notes at the top). BG-3 is deferred to v1.4 by the plan: do not plan it. Existing plans for style and depth: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/AC1_PLAN.md`, `VR2_PLAN.md`, `GV1_PLAN.md`. Existing building blocks to reuse (verify each by reading; cite file:line): the sidecar server and protocol (`cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/protocol.ts`), `ApplicationCore` (`cli/src/core/app.ts`), `nexus run --json` and its contract (`cli/src/run_contract.ts`, `docs/protocol/RUN_RESULT_VERSIONS.json`), durable state and resume (`cli/src/core/state.ts`, session/task state), operations and goals (`cli/src/core/operation*.ts`, `cli/src/goal*.ts`, PA-1 resource admission), worktrees (`cli/src/worktree.ts` / worktrunk), approvals and modes (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`, managed policy `cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts`: note `approval.strict`/`disable_auto`), verification (`cli/src/core/verify*.ts`, verification-path protection), the flight recorder and audit bundles, MCP and plugin loading (`cli/src/mcp.ts`, the plugin loader: find it), the execution spine and `dispatchToolCallImpl` (`cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/spine.ts`), the P10 budgets (invocation, inference submission, turn deadline), `nexus doctor`.\n\nHard constraints every plan must respect:\n- The approval gates, verification-path protection, managed policy and the VERIFIED outcome ladder are authorities no new surface may weaken. A hook's or plugin's \"allow\" must have no effect on a gate denial (HK-1 acceptance); an unattended run must never grant allow-always, must run in a fresh worktree, and a seeded failing verifier must end BLOCKED, not VERIFIED (BG-2/G-4).\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`): new behaviour hooks in through a helper call that adds no branch there, or outside those functions.\n- Model-facing tool descriptions have a size budget (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`): prefer no new model-facing tools.\n- Every new CLI command, setting, protocol message or file format needs: docs (G-6 wants a runnable example per new surface, run in CI), a schema/version ledger entry where the repo keeps one, tests that fail when the guard is removed, and the hostile cases for G-3 (client, hook, plugin, schedule, policy each trying to bypass a gate).\n- Tests run with a throwaway HOME, no network, no live inference (there is a scripted gateway fixture `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`); new test files register `cli/test/helpers/leak_guard.ts`; child processes are stopped by pid.\n- Linux x86_64 is the only release target.\n\nFor EACH unit give: the acceptance criterion verbatim from the plan; what exists that it builds on (file:line); the design (data model, commands/flags, protocol or file formats with exact shapes, state on disk and its permissions, lifecycle, failure and crash behaviour, limits and timeouts); the security analysis (who is untrusted, what they control, each gate they could try to bypass and why they cannot); the exact list of files to add/change; the tests by name with what each proves, including the hostile ones and the kill/restart ones; docs and ledgers; and 2–4 mergeable slices in order, each independently green, with rough size (S/M/L) and the files it touches, flagging slices that touch `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/run_turn.ts` or `cli/src/sidecar.ts` (hot files shared with other work). Keep each unit to what its acceptance needs: state explicitly what is out of scope for v1.3.0. Where two units share machinery (BG-1's detached runner and BG-2's unattended policy and schedule; HK-1's hook points and HK-2's plugin install/verify and HK-3's per-plugin trust), design it once and say which slice introduces it.\n\nSpecific questions the plans must answer:\n- BG-1 (\"kill the CLI; the run continues and resumes\"): what process owns a detached run (a supervisor? the sidecar re-parented? systemd-run --user? plain double-fork with a pidfile and a unix socket?), how a client attaches/detaches/lists/stops, where output and state live, how identity and liveness are established without pid-reuse mistakes (the repo just fixed several of those: record pid + kernel start time), what happens on machine reboot, how the P10 budgets and the turn deadline apply, and how it interacts with PF-3 (a unix-socket transport that is a separate unit: can BG-1 ship with attach-by-log-and-state only, leaving live attach to PF-3?).\n- BG-2 (\"seeded failing verifier ends BLOCKED; unattended approval denied\"): the unattended approval policy (default deny for anything that would prompt; no allow-always; `--yes`/auto refused or clamped per D19 and managed policy), fresh-worktree requirement, what \"schedule\" means in v1.3.0 (D21: event triggers and SSH execution — take the default; if scheduling proper is deferred, say exactly what small scheduling surface, if any, the acceptance needs), and the receipt/audit trail of an unattended run.\n- HK-1 (\"a hook's allow has no effect\"): which hook points exist (pre-tool, post-tool, on-stop? keep it minimal), how a hook is declared (operator config only? project config with trust?), what a hook may return (deny / annotate only — never allow), execution model (subprocess with a timeout, env, stdin JSON, sandboxed?), failure mode (hook error = deny or ignore? choose fail-closed for pre-tool), and how it attaches to dispatch without adding branches to `dispatchToolCallImpl`.\n- HK-2 (\"commit-pinned plugin verify\") and HK-3 (\"per-plugin trust\"): D18's default on in-process plugins; what a plugin is today in this repo (read the loader); install from a git URL pinned to a commit with a recorded digest, verify on load, refuse on mismatch; trust records per plugin (where stored, how approved, how revoked); how GV-5's `plugins.allow` / `plugins.disable_workspace` interact (another agent is implementing GV-5 enforcement now: say what you assume it provides).\n\nAlso record per plan: reference-coding note per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md` (what peer projects do — the plan's own notes at the top of PLAN.md cite some; do not spend long; \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" plus what you used is acceptable), risks, and open questions that could not be settled from the code.\n\nShell notes: zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output is filtered by `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` when a grep/sed pipeline looks odd). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus.\n\nReturn (under 600 words): the two file paths; for each unit the slice list with sizes and hot files; the decisions taken; the main risks; anything you could not determine. Be concrete enough that an implementer can start slice 1 of each unit from the plan alone.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/ac19cad41192e3f0a.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"511cc77a-b99e-4a0a-ab8b-0b33617822ed","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code/cli","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"1ac2580e-7cbb-4c68-9d11-34318da5a862","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMUWVqBaQdhvtjpB9rzU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FyFjrmv8VBGE8uDo2FKzzk","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Plan PF-3, PF-4, SB-1, MT-2, PA-2","subagent_type":"architect","prompt":"Write implementation plans for five units of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release: PF-3, PF-4, SB-1, MT-2, PA-2. Write one file and nothing else: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md` (one top-level section per unit). Do not edit code. Work read-only against `git -C ~/nexus-code show origin/release/v1.3.0:<path>` / `git -C ~/nexus-code grep <pattern> origin/release/v1.3.0 -- <paths>` (fetch first). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided take the plan's stated default, or else the conservative option, and record it under \"Decisions taken\".\n\nSources: `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` on that branch (read it all: the unit rows; gates G-1..G-7; decisions D13 (npm publish: default \"no publish; the entry point is documented but not published\"), D22 (profile names and defaults: default \"ship `:workspace`, `:read-only`, `:network`; interactive default stays today's engine defaults until an explicit profile is set; managed policy may force a stricter profile, never a looser one\") and any other decision these units cite; the phase ordering; the peer-project notes at the top, including the Codex re-read on permission profiles and the tool registry). Existing plans for style and depth: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/AC1_PLAN.md`, `VR2_PLAN.md`, `GV1_PLAN.md`.\n\nThe units, verbatim acceptance from the plan (re-read them there):\n- PF-3: transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener; several clients may attach and reattach. Acceptance: a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600.\n- PF-4: library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar. Acceptance: an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI.\n- SB-1: named permission profiles that map today's bwrap/docker flags, HOME mounts and network settings without loosening gates; profiles compose with approval mode and managed policy; `nexus doctor` and session posture print the effective profile; document residual holes honestly. Acceptance: CLI and doctor show the effective profile; a profile cannot disable verification-path protection, grant allow-always, or bypass G-3; existing sandbox tests stay green.\n- MT-2: tools are registered spec+handler pairs (a registry: spec and handler bound together); dispatch and G-3 suites green. It follows MT-1 (the `agent.ts` split, landed in v1.2.1): confirm what MT-1's acceptance was and whether anything of MT-1 is left, and say so.\n- PA-2: conflicting units fail verification on the merged tree (\"verified merge arbitration\" for parallel operation units): read the PA-1/PA-3/PA-4 docs under `docs/v1.3.0/` and `cli/src/core/operation*.ts`, `cli/src/batch.ts`, `cli/src/loop.ts`, worktree handling, to define what a \"unit\", a \"merge\" and \"verification on the merged tree\" are here.\n\nExisting building blocks to reuse (verify each by reading; cite file:line): the sidecar server and protocol (`cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/protocol.ts`, `docs/protocol/`), `ApplicationCore` (`cli/src/core/app.ts`), the ACP adapter's bounded line reader and request correlator (`cli/src/acp/jsonrpc.ts`), `cli/src/index.ts` (commander program; how it is structured and what importing it does today), `cli/package.json`, `scripts/package-linux.sh` and the package smoke tests, the sandbox (`cli/src/sandbox.ts`, bwrap and docker engines, `cli/test/verify_sandbox.test.ts`, `sandbox_*.test.ts`), `nexus doctor` (`cli/src/doctor.ts`), managed policy (`cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts`, `policy_runtime.ts`, `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md`: `sandbox.*` keys), the capability registry and definitions (`cli/src/core/capability_definitions/`, `capabilityRegistry`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/spine.ts`, `cli/src/tools.ts`), verification (`cli/src/core/verify*.ts`), operations (`cli/src/core/operation*.ts`).\n\nHard constraints every plan must respect:\n- The approval gates, verification-path protection, managed policy and the VERIFIED outcome ladder are authorities no new surface may weaken (gate G-3: a client, hook, plugin, schedule or policy must not bypass a gate). PF-3 in particular adds a listener: the plan must say who can connect (same uid only, by socket permissions and directory permissions), what the token protects against, what a second client may and may not do (may it answer an approval the first client was asked? decide conservatively), and why DNS-rebinding/CSRF cannot reach a localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener if one exists at all (the conservative decision may be: unix socket only in v1.3.0, no TCP listener; say so if you take it and check it against the acceptance wording).\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). MT-2 touches dispatch: plan it so those functions shrink or stay equal, and say how the baseline file is handled if a function is split (read `cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`: a renamed or missing function fails closed).\n- Model-facing tool descriptions and the tool list have a size budget and exact-shape tests (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`, `cli/test/lsp_shape.test.ts`, protocol parity tests): MT-2 must be behaviour-preserving for what the model sees; say which characterization tests pin that and which must be added first.\n- Every new CLI command, setting, protocol message or file format needs docs (gate G-6 wants a runnable example per new surface, run in CI), a schema/version ledger entry where the repo keeps one, tests that fail when the guard is removed, and hostile cases.\n- Tests run with a throwaway HOME, no network, no live inference (scripted gateway fixture `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`); new test files register `cli/test/helpers/leak_guard.ts`; child processes are stopped by pid; sockets and listeners are closed.\n- Linux x86_64 is the only release target. Node 22 and 24.\n- PF-4: importing the library entry must have no side effects (no commander parse, no process.exit, no stdout writes, no reading of the real HOME at import time); the package must still build the same tarball layout (`scripts/package-linux.sh`, reproducibility check: the package is built twice and compared byte for byte in CI); D13 default = not published.\n\nFor EACH unit give: the acceptance verbatim; what exists that it builds on (file:line); the design (data model, commands/flags, protocol or file formats with exact shapes, state on disk and permissions, lifecycle, failure behaviour, limits); the security analysis; the exact list of files to add/change; the tests by name with what each proves (including hostile cases and, for refactors, the characterization tests that must be green before and after); docs and ledgers; and 2–4 mergeable slices in order, each independently green, with rough size (S/M/L) and the files touched, flagging hot files shared with other work (`cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/run_turn.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts`). Keep each unit to what its acceptance needs and state what is out of scope for v1.3.0. Note dependencies between these units and on BG-1 (detached runs; planned separately: BG-1 may rely on PF-3's socket for live attach) and on GV-1/GV-5 (managed policy; being finished now).\n\nAlso record: a recommended build order across the five units with reasons (smallest and least entangled first; MT-2 is a wide refactor of hot files, so say when it is safest), reference-coding notes per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md` (brief; \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" plus what you used is acceptable), risks, and open questions that could not be settled from the code.\n\nShell notes: zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output is filtered by `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` when a grep/sed pipeline looks odd). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus.\n\nReturn (under 600 words): the file path; for each unit the slice list with sizes and hot files; the recommended build order; the decisions taken; the main risks; anything you could not determine. Be concrete enough that an implementer can start slice 1 of each unit from the plan alone."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_read_input_tokens":755841,"output_tokens":12274,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":12274,"cache_read_input_tokens":755841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":28487,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FyFjrmv8VBGE8uDo2FKzzk":{"description":"Plan PF-3, PF-4, SB-1, MT-2, PA-2","subagent_type":"architect","prompt":"Write implementation plans for five units of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release: PF-3, PF-4, SB-1, MT-2, PA-2. Write one file and nothing else: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md` (one top-level section per unit). Do not edit code. Work read-only against `git -C ~/nexus-code show origin/release/v1.3.0:<path>` / `git -C ~/nexus-code grep <pattern> origin/release/v1.3.0 -- <paths>` (fetch first). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided take the plan's stated default, or else the conservative option, and record it under \"Decisions taken\".\n\nSources: `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` on that branch (read it all: the unit rows; gates G-1..G-7; decisions D13 (npm publish: default \"no publish; the entry point is documented but not published\"), D22 (profile names and defaults: default \"ship `:workspace`, `:read-only`, `:network`; interactive default stays today's engine defaults until an explicit profile is set; managed policy may force a stricter profile, never a looser one\") and any other decision these units cite; the phase ordering; the peer-project notes at the top, including the Codex re-read on permission profiles and the tool registry). Existing plans for style and depth: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/AC1_PLAN.md`, `VR2_PLAN.md`, `GV1_PLAN.md`.\n\nThe units, verbatim acceptance from the plan (re-read them there):\n- PF-3: transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener; several clients may attach and reattach. Acceptance: a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600.\n- PF-4: library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar. Acceptance: an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI.\n- SB-1: named permission profiles that map today's bwrap/docker flags, HOME mounts and network settings without loosening gates; profiles compose with approval mode and managed policy; `nexus doctor` and session posture print the effective profile; document residual holes honestly. Acceptance: CLI and doctor show the effective profile; a profile cannot disable verification-path protection, grant allow-always, or bypass G-3; existing sandbox tests stay green.\n- MT-2: tools are registered spec+handler pairs (a registry: spec and handler bound together); dispatch and G-3 suites green. It follows MT-1 (the `agent.ts` split, landed in v1.2.1): confirm what MT-1's acceptance was and whether anything of MT-1 is left, and say so.\n- PA-2: conflicting units fail verification on the merged tree (\"verified merge arbitration\" for parallel operation units): read the PA-1/PA-3/PA-4 docs under `docs/v1.3.0/` and `cli/src/core/operation*.ts`, `cli/src/batch.ts`, `cli/src/loop.ts`, worktree handling, to define what a \"unit\", a \"merge\" and \"verification on the merged tree\" are here.\n\nExisting building blocks to reuse (verify each by reading; cite file:line): the sidecar server and protocol (`cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/protocol.ts`, `docs/protocol/`), `ApplicationCore` (`cli/src/core/app.ts`), the ACP adapter's bounded line reader and request correlator (`cli/src/acp/jsonrpc.ts`), `cli/src/index.ts` (commander program; how it is structured and what importing it does today), `cli/package.json`, `scripts/package-linux.sh` and the package smoke tests, the sandbox (`cli/src/sandbox.ts`, bwrap and docker engines, `cli/test/verify_sandbox.test.ts`, `sandbox_*.test.ts`), `nexus doctor` (`cli/src/doctor.ts`), managed policy (`cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts`, `policy_runtime.ts`, `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md`: `sandbox.*` keys), the capability registry and definitions (`cli/src/core/capability_definitions/`, `capabilityRegistry`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/spine.ts`, `cli/src/tools.ts`), verification (`cli/src/core/verify*.ts`), operations (`cli/src/core/operation*.ts`).\n\nHard constraints every plan must respect:\n- The approval gates, verification-path protection, managed policy and the VERIFIED outcome ladder are authorities no new surface may weaken (gate G-3: a client, hook, plugin, schedule or policy must not bypass a gate). PF-3 in particular adds a listener: the plan must say who can connect (same uid only, by socket permissions and directory permissions), what the token protects against, what a second client may and may not do (may it answer an approval the first client was asked? decide conservatively), and why DNS-rebinding/CSRF cannot reach a localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener if one exists at all (the conservative decision may be: unix socket only in v1.3.0, no TCP listener; say so if you take it and check it against the acceptance wording).\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). MT-2 touches dispatch: plan it so those functions shrink or stay equal, and say how the baseline file is handled if a function is split (read `cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`: a renamed or missing function fails closed).\n- Model-facing tool descriptions and the tool list have a size budget and exact-shape tests (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`, `cli/test/lsp_shape.test.ts`, protocol parity tests): MT-2 must be behaviour-preserving for what the model sees; say which characterization tests pin that and which must be added first.\n- Every new CLI command, setting, protocol message or file format needs docs (gate G-6 wants a runnable example per new surface, run in CI), a schema/version ledger entry where the repo keeps one, tests that fail when the guard is removed, and hostile cases.\n- Tests run with a throwaway HOME, no network, no live inference (scripted gateway fixture `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`); new test files register `cli/test/helpers/leak_guard.ts`; child processes are stopped by pid; sockets and listeners are closed.\n- Linux x86_64 is the only release target. Node 22 and 24.\n- PF-4: importing the library entry must have no side effects (no commander parse, no process.exit, no stdout writes, no reading of the real HOME at import time); the package must still build the same tarball layout (`scripts/package-linux.sh`, reproducibility check: the package is built twice and compared byte for byte in CI); D13 default = not published.\n\nFor EACH unit give: the acceptance verbatim; what exists that it builds on (file:line); the design (data model, commands/flags, protocol or file formats with exact shapes, state on disk and permissions, lifecycle, failure behaviour, limits); the security analysis; the exact list of files to add/change; the tests by name with what each proves (including hostile cases and, for refactors, the characterization tests that must be green before and after); docs and ledgers; and 2–4 mergeable slices in order, each independently green, with rough size (S/M/L) and the files touched, flagging hot files shared with other work (`cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/run_turn.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts`). Keep each unit to what its acceptance needs and state what is out of scope for v1.3.0. Note dependencies between these units and on BG-1 (detached runs; planned separately: BG-1 may rely on PF-3's socket for live attach) and on GV-1/GV-5 (managed policy; being finished now).\n\nAlso record: a recommended build order across the five units with reasons (smallest and least entangled first; MT-2 is a wide refactor of hot files, so say when it is safest), reference-coding notes per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md` (brief; \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" plus what you used is acceptable), risks, and open questions that could not be settled from the code.\n\nShell notes: zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output is filtered by `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` when a grep/sed pipeline looks odd). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus.\n\nReturn (under 600 words): the file path; for each unit the slice list with sizes and hot files; the recommended build order; the decisions taken; the main risks; anything you could not determine. Be concrete enough that an implementer can start slice 1 of each unit from the plan alone."}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011ChMUWVb3dmjZMBZhfM3DU","requestRef":"a7782de7-9d22-4b9b-9849-53f5fb0870ae","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"8b8da9e0-24ea-463d-ad4a-7d9676c51948","timestamp":"2026-11-24T08:58:51.008Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code/cli","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
