{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:16:25.209Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>add2d9de159235857</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01FucdE1HVy6uCDG67HjSqap</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/add2d9de159235857.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review PR #776 submodule check\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS** at 7ad1d864dd6601f255279558155e3466419df47e. At the end of the review the head had not moved and the PR was still OPEN.\n\n**1. Tests**\n- `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts` at head passes: 21 of 21, including the new submodule test.\n- I disabled both submodule conditions in a copy of the source (gitlink check and `modules/` check) and restored the original afterwards. The new test then fails with \"the submodule's configured command ran\".\n\n**2. Probes** (real export, the new test's fixture style, filter planted last)\n\n| Probe | Planted program ran? | Binding |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) plain nested repo, not registered as a submodule | no | `partial`, no reason, so the export went ahead and hashed the workspace |\n| (b) gitlink added with `update-index --cacheinfo 160000` | no | `unavailable`, `workspace_configures_git_programs` |\n| (c) gitlink removed with `git rm --cached`, `.git/modules/sub` left in place | no | `unavailable`, `workspace_configures_git_programs` |\n\nIn (a), `status --untracked-files=all` did not descend into the inner repo. The inner filter did not run even though I re-stamped the inner file's mtime.\n\n**3. Reading the function** (`cli/src/audit_export.ts:87-117`)\n- `git ls-files --stage` and `git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir` run no planted program. Neither reads the working tree, so no filter applies. The `ls-files` call also sets `core.fsmonitor=false`. Both calls run before any program-naming config has been ruled out for the config the check reads. I read this off the code and did not probe it beyond the tests above.\n- Error paths:\n  - A failed `ls-files` or `rev-parse` returns true (refuse), and so does a `readdirSync` error other than ENOENT.\n  - The only `return false` branch left is `:96`, for a directory that is not a Git work tree. That is correct, because no Git command runs there.\n  - A `maxBuffer` overflow gives status null. `null !== 0` makes `:108` return true, so a huge index fails closed. I did not test it.\n- Follow-up (nit, `:114`): an empty `modules/` directory is allowed. That is safe.\n- Follow-up (nit, `:107`): `ls-files` can hit the 10 s timeout on a very large index. The result is refusal (`unavailable`), not a wrong answer, but the reason will read `workspace_configures_git_programs`. This is a misleading label rather than a security issue. A distinct reason would be clearer.\n\n**4. False refusals**\n- Plain repositories with no submodule still compare. The existing tests in `audit_export.test.ts` that use the fake receipt id show `mismatch`, and they pass at head.\n- I did not run a dedicated test for this.\n\n**Not checked**\n- Node 24, because I ran only the default Node.\n- Worktrees: a linked worktree whose `modules/` lives in the common git dir. `--absolute-git-dir` there points at the worktree's own gitdir, so a submodule stored in the common dir might be missed. Worth a probe.\n- Sparse-checkout, `.gitmodules`-only entries, and `GIT_DIR` environment overrides.\n- The Windows path, since the test skips on non-Linux.\n\nThe probe file is deleted, and the `rev776` worktree is removed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>34360</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>9</tool_uses><duration_ms>215715</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
