n invalid operator hooks file denies every call. Project hooks run only after their exact file hash is approved.\n- **Plugins:** contribute hooks, trigger-loaded skills and MCP servers only — no in-process code, custom tools or verifiers. Git installs need a full commit id, and Nexus computes its own digest of the plugin's files.\n- **D18:** in-process JS plugins stay, but workspace ones need per-file approval.\n\n## Main risks\n- `UnattendedApprovalPolicy` duplicates approval logic. It has an exhaustive table test, but must track any later change to `ApprovalPolicy`.\n- Worktree-write jobs can only run commands you allowed in `command_rules.json`.\n- Under systemd, a runner crash also ends its workers, so a crash mid-tool always needs a human decision.\n- Hooks run after the interactive approval prompt.\n- `agent.ts`, `index.ts`, `doctor.ts`, `plugins.ts` and `mcp.ts` are being edited by other agents.\n- Edits by programs running as the same user are not detected.\n\n## Could not determine\n- Whether command `allow` rules match compound commands segment by segment, and how bwrap isolates pids. Both affect how the SI-7-against-runner test is written.\n- Whether `nexus audit export` covers operations.\n- Whether operation units can answer `NEEDS_INPUT` questions.\n- GV-5's final plugin and MCP API. I read it from an uncommitted worktree.\n- Whether the TUI can ask about project-hook approval without a protocol change.\n- Whether git hosts allow fetching an unadvertised commit by id at depth 1. The plan has a fallback.\n\nXERJ has only the opencode corpus loaded, so the goose, Gemini CLI and Codex parts rely on the plan's own peer notes.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>328813</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>107</tool_uses><duration_ms>2007105</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"af44c8ff-8f34-47a7-bcb3-6bc06cd48b67","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:31:52.601Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"task-notification","producer":"session-task",