pproval policy as a pure module with its exhaustive table test, the job receipt schema and ledger. Tests as the plan names them.\n- Slice 2 (runner, worker, `runner serve`, `job start|status|stop`): as the plan specifies, including the kernel start time on the operation lock and the `origin` field (operation schema bump per the repo's schema rules), and the acceptance test: start a job, kill the client CLI, the run continues; kill the worker, it resumes (bounded retries); a job runs in a fresh worktree; an action that would prompt is denied, never allowed-always.\n- Slice 3 (operator surface: `job list|logs|resume`, `runner status|install-unit`, doctor findings, docs and a runnable example for gate G-6) ONLY if slices 1 and 2 are complete, committed and green. If you run short, stop cleanly after a finished slice and say so; never leave a half-wired command.\n\nHard constraints:\n- No surface here may weaken an approval gate, verification-path protection, managed policy (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`: `approval.strict`, `disable_auto`, …) or the VERIFIED outcome ladder. An unattended job never runs in auto mode, never grants allow-always, and refuses `--yes`.\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). Do not add branches there; run the gate from the worktree root: `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts` pins a size budget).\n- Other agents hold file claims on `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts`. You may edit them only with small self-contained additions (one register call for the new commands; new doctor findings in their own function); put the command implementations in new files as the plan says. Do not reformat or move existing