ed policy, are). Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/bg1` (branch `v1/bg1-runner-lib`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where the plan leaves a detail open choose the conservative option and list it in your report.\n\nThe unit's acceptance, from `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`: \"kill the CLI; the run continues and resumes\". Gate G-4 for unattended runs: no allow-always, fresh worktree, kill-and-resume.\n\nDeliver the plan's slices in order, COMMITTING LOCALLY at the end of each slice (one commit per slice, message `feat(background): <what> (BG-1, slice N)` ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`), so that each slice is independently green:\n- Slice 1 (library, no hot files): process identity (pid + kernel start time + boot id), the strict JSON reader, the runner store and job record `nexus-job` v1 with its schema and ledger entry, the unattended approval policy as a pure module with its exhaustive table test, the job receipt schema and ledger. Tests as the plan names them.\n- Slice 2 (runner, worker, `runner serve`, `job start|status|stop`): as the plan specifies, including the kernel start time on the operation lock and the `origin` field (operation schema bump per the repo's schema rules), and the acceptance test: start a job, kill the client CLI, the run continues; kill the worker, it resumes (bounded retries); a job runs in a fresh worktree; an action that would prompt is denied, never allowed-always.\n- Slice 3 (operator surface: `job list|logs|resume`, `runner status|install-unit`, doctor findings, docs and a runnable example for gate G-6) ONLY if slices 1 and 2 are complete, committed and green. If you run short, stop cleanly after a finished slice and say so; never leave a half-wired command.\n\nHard constraints:\n- No surface here may weaken an approval gate, verification-path protection, managed policy (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`: `approval.strict`, `disable_auto`, …) or the VERIFIED outcome ladder. An unattended job never runs in auto mode, never grants allow-always, and refuses `--yes`.\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). Do not add branches there; run the gate from the worktree root: `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts` pins a size budget).\n- Other agents hold file claims on `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts`. You may edit them only with small self-contained additions (one register call for the new commands; new doctor findings in their own function); put the command implementations in new files as the plan says. Do not reformat or move existing code.\n- Process handling: signal a process only after checking pid + kernel start time (+ boot id) still match; never `pkill`; every runner/worker/tool process a test starts is stopped by its pid in a `finally`. State directories and files are created 0700/0600 and tests assert it.\n- Every new on-disk format or protocol message gets a JSON schema and a version ledger entry where the repo keeps them (`docs/protocol/`, see how `VERSIONS.json`, `RUN_RESULT_VERSIONS.json`, `AUDIT_*` ledgers and their tests work), and earlier frozen schema versions stay byte-identical. If you change the sidecar protocol schema, regenerate `cli/test/fixtures/protocol_v1_schema_samples.jsonl` with `node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_samples.mjs` (CI checks it).\n- Linux x86_64 only. Code must work on Node 22 and 24 (only 24 is installed here: avoid Node-24-only APIs).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg1/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME (and `NEXUS_STATE_DIR` where the plan says) is mandatory: never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference (use the scripted gateway `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`), no network.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first (the repo's `leak_guard.test.ts` fails a file that builds an agent, bridge or gateway without it); clear every timer and close every server. A single test file must finish well under 300 s in CI (the per-file limit): keep process-spawning tests few and fast, and prefer starting the CLI from a current `dist/` the way `cli/test/helpers/audit_fixture.ts` `cliEntry()` does.\n- Stress every new async or process test: run each new file 3 times in a row; fix flakes (tight deadlines are the usual cause; this host is under load from other work, so use generous waits and polling, not sleeps).\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo; do not change Rust.\n- Before each commit run, one at a time: every new test file; `leak_guard`; `protocol_parity`; `protocol_schema`; `check_requirements`; `release_workflow_parity`; `operation_schema`, `operation_lock`, `operation_entry_integrity`, `operation_runner_restart`, `goal_lifecycle` (if you touched operations); `context_convergence`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate. If the repo requires a ledger or doc entry for a new command (`scripts/doc_commands.mjs` is run by the package smoke test against the user manual), add it. `doctor.test.ts` fails 11 tests on this host on the base branch too: if you touch doctor, compare against `origin/release/v1.3.0` and report only differences.\n- When you need to undo an experiment, copy the file aside first and restore from the copy; 