4-lib-entry`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where the plan leaves a detail open choose the conservative option and list it in your report.\n\nThe unit, from `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`: \"Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar. Acceptance: an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI.\" Decision D13 at its default: not published.\n\nPlan decisions that bind you (re-read them in the plan): the package stays unpublished (`\"private\": true` if the plan says so — but first check that this does not change the packaged tarball or the third-party notices: the release package is built twice in CI and compared byte for byte, see `scripts/package-linux.sh` and the `linux-x86_64` job in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`; if `private` changes generated output, keep the build reproducible and say what changed); the library only drives the sidecar as a separate process and does not export `ApplicationCore` or anything that would let an embedder skip start-up checks; the client API cannot send allow-always, and an approval the embedder did not explicitly grant is denied.\n\nDeliver the plan's slices in order, COMMITTING LOCALLY at the end of each slice (message `feat(lib): <what> (PF-4, slice N)` ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`), each independently green:\n- Slice 1: `cli/src/lib.ts`, the sidecar client module, `exports`/`types` in `cli/package.json`, the type-declaration build the plan describes (it flags a risk: whether this repo's TypeScript version can emit declarations for the lib; try it first and use the plan's hand-written fallback if not — say which), the package smoke-test addition, tests, `docs/EMBEDDING.md`, and the lib API ledger.\n- Slice 2: the `examples/embed/` example and the test that runs it (the acceptance: an example script runs a verified task through the entry point), wired so CI runs it (find how examples/tests are discovered: `cli/scripts/test_shards.mjs`, `cli/package.json` scripts; a new test file under `cli/test/` is run automatically).\n\nRequirements to prove with tests:\n- Importing the library entry has no side effects: no commander parse, no `process.exit`, no writes to stdout or stderr, no read of the real HOME at import time, no listeners or timers left (assert in a child process that imports it and exits 0 with empty output).\n- The CLI still works exactly as before (`nexus --help`, `nexus --version`, an existing command) — `cli/src/index.ts` is a hot file other agents are editing: change it as little as the plan requires (ideally not at all) and never reformat it.\n- The example really spawns the sidecar, runs a task against the scripted gateway fixture (`cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`; no live inference), and gets a verification verdict; the embedder's default is deny for approvals; allow-always cannot be expressed through the client API.\n- The built package layout: run what `scripts/smoke-package.sh` and the package build need only as far as is possible without the network; if you cannot run the full `scripts/package-linux.sh` here (it builds in a container), say so and reason from the script about whether `exports`, `types`, `private` and the new files change the tarball contents, `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt` or the SBOM, and whether the reproducibility comparison still holds.\n\nHard constraints:\n- No surface here may weaken an approval gate, verification-path protection, managed policy or the VERIFIED outcome ladder.\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in complexity (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`); you should not need to touch them. Run the gate from the worktree root: `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- `cli/package.json` / `package-lock.json`: add no dependency. If the lockfile changes at all, `node scripts/check_deprecated_lock.mjs cli/package-lock.json` must pass and the lockfile's `engines`/metadata must stay consistent with `package.json`.\n- `cli/test/release_workflow_parity.test.ts` pins the CI workflow structure and `cli/package.json` scripts (`test`, `test:cli`): if you add a script, do not change those two.\n- Linux x86_64 only; Node 22 and 24 (only 24 is installed here: avoid Node-24-only APIs).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/pf4/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME is mandatory: never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference, no network.\n- The build: `npm --prefix cli run build` (tsc) must succeed; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink to another checkout: do not run `npm install`/`npm ci` and do not let the build write into `node_modules`. If you build `dist/`, that is fine (it is untracked build output inside this worktree).\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; stop every child by its own pid in a `finally` (never `pkill`); a test file must finish well under 300 s. Stress each new file: 3 runs in a row; this host is under load, so use generous waits and polling.\n- Before each commit run, one at a time: every new test file; `leak_guard`; `release_workflow_parity`; `check_requirements`; `sidecar`; `protocol_parity`; `context_convergence`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; `node scripts/check_deprecated_lock.mjs cli/package-lock.json`; the complexity gate. If the repo requires a ledger or doc entry (`scripts/doc_commands.mjs` checks documented commands against the CLI), keep it passing.\n- When you need to undo an experiment, copy the file aside first and restore from the copy; do not use `git checkout <file>` while you have uncommitted work in it.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the plan; nothing speculative.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n\nReport (under 500 words): per slice: done / not done, commit hash, files added and changed, tests added with exact pass counts and the 3-run result, gates run; which way type declarations are produced; what `private`/`exports` do to the packaged tarball, notices and SBOM and how you know; how the acceptance is demonstrated; deviations from the plan and why; what is left; anything a release note must mention.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a59c1e14e05cf48ec.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"89c4936a-ac3a-457f-8c38-54f67a93c844","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"45c76679-2def-423b-8538-f180d75f832a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMXTLvR2sVe8YTQhdrzE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018mN8utniiDRY642coiRLxo","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement SB-1 permission profiles","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Implement unit SB-1 (named permission profiles) of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 plan, following the written design exactly: the \"SB-1\" section (and the shared notes and \"Decisions taken\") of `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md`. Read the whole SB-1 section and the decisions first; the other units in that file are NOT your job. Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/sb1` (branch `v1/sb1-profiles`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where the plan leaves a detail open choose the conservative (stricter) option and list it in your report.\n\nThe unit, from `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` (re-read the row and decision D22 there): named permission profiles (`:workspace`, `:read-only`, `:network` per D22's default) that map today's bwrap/docker flags, HOME mounts and network settings without loosening gates; profiles compose with approval mode and managed policy; `nexus doctor` and the session posture print the effective profile; residual holes are documented honestly. Acceptance: \"CLI and doctor show the effective profile; a profile cannot disable verification-path protection, grant allow-always, or bypass G-3; existing sandbox tests stay green.\" D22 default: the interactive default stays today's engine defaults until an explicit profile is set; managed policy may force a stricter profile, never a looser one. The plan's decision: profiles are selected through `--sandbox [engine]:profile`, with no new flag and no new managed-policy key, and the doctor schema stays at version 2.\n\nDeliver the plan's slices in order, COMMITTING LOCALLY at the end of each slice (message `feat(sandbox): <what> (SB-1, slice N)` ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`), each independently green:\n- Slice 1 (pure module, no existing source touched): `sandbox_profile.ts` as the plan defines it (parsing `[engine]:profile`, the profile table mapping to today's flags, composition rules: a profile can only select among existing confinement settings; with managed policy the stricter of the two wins), with a table test covering every profile × engine × policy combination and the hostile inputs (unknown profile, empty, case and whitespace variants, a profile string from a project file trying to loosen).\n- Slice 2 (wiring): the plan lists `config.ts`, `types.ts` (both claimed by other agents: keep each edit to one small self-contained block, no reformatting), `sandbox_network.ts`, `sandbox.ts`. Behaviour with no profile given must be byte-for-byte what it is today (D22): prove it with the existing sandbox tests unchanged and a characterization test of the generated bwrap/docker arguments before and after.\n- Slice 3 (showing the profile): doctor and the session posture. The plan flags hot files: `doctor.ts` (claimed; add the finding in its own function), `sidecar.ts`, `dispatch_tool_call.ts` (\"one property\" — `dispatchToolCallImpl` is under a complexity gate with NO headroom: adding any branch there fails CI; if the property cannot be added without a branch, put it elsewhere and say so), and `live_view.rs` (Rust TUI, claimed): do NOT change Rust; show the profile through the existing posture/protocol fields the TUI already renders if possible, otherwise leave the TUI unchanged and report it. Do slice 3 ONLY if slices 1 and 2 are complete, committed and green. If you run short, stop cleanly after a finished slice.\n\nRequirements to prove with tests (the acceptance and gate G-3):\n- A profile cannot disable verification-path protection, cannot grant allow-always or switch the approval mode, and cannot loosen what managed policy fixes (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`, `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md`: `sandbox.*`). Where policy and profile disagree, the stricter applies and the output says which source decided.\n- `:read-only` really is read-only for the command sandbox (a write inside the workspace fails) and `:network` is the only profile with network; `:workspace` has no network. Use the existing sandbox test helpers (`cli/test/verify_sandbox.test.ts`, `cli/test/sandbox_*.test.ts`; tests skip when bwrap is not installed — it is installed here; do not require docker).\n- A profile named by anything other than the operator (a project config file, a workspace `.env`, model output, a tool argument) is ignored or refused: check how `--sandbox` is resolved today in `cli/src/config.ts` (which sources may set it, `PROJECT_ENV_KEYS`) and keep project sources from selecting a looser profile than the operator's.\n- `nexus doctor` (text and `--json`) and the session posture show the effective profile and its source; the doctor JSON schema version stays 2 per the plan (additive field: check how `doctor.schemaVersion` consumers and `scripts/smoke-package.sh` read it).\n- Docs: the residual holes, stated plainly (broad host reads under `:workspace`, what `:network` exposes, ignored trees such as `node_modules`, the same-user limit), in the sandbox documentation the plan names, plus a runnable example for gate G-6 if the plan specifies one.\n\nHard constraints:\n- No surface here may weaken an approval gate, verification-path protection, managed policy or the VERIFIED outcome ladder.\n- Complexity gate: `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl`, `driveOperation` must not grow (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). Run from the worktree root: `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`).\n- If you change the sidecar protocol schema, follow the repo's ledger rules and regenerate `cli/test/fixtures/protocol_v1_schema_samples.jsonl` with `node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_samples.mjs` (CI checks it); prefer not changing the protocol.\n- Linux x86_64 only; Node 22 and 24 (only 24 is installed here).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/sb1/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME is mandatory: never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference, no network.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo; do not change Rust.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; stop every child by its own pid in a `finally` (never `pkill`); a test file must finish well under 300 s. Stress each new file: 3 runs in a row; this host is under load, so use generous waits.\n- Before each commit run, one at a time: every new test file; every existing `cli/test/sandbox*.test.ts` and `verify_sandbox`; `mcp_verify` (about 3 minutes) and `verify_service` (they run sandboxed verifiers); `approval_policy`; `leak_guard`; `context_convergence`; `check_requirements`; `protocol_parity`; `sidecar`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate. `doctor.test.ts` fails 11 tests on this host on the base branch too: if you touch doctor, compare against `origin/release/v1.3.0` and report only differences.\n- For each guard, show by mutation that a test fails without it (copy the source file aside and restore from the copy; do not use `git checkout <file>` while you have uncommitted work in it) and read the failure message to check it fails for the right reason.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the plan; nothing speculative.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n\nReport (under 550 words): per slice: done / not done, commit hash, files added and changed, tests added with exact pass counts and the 3-run result, gates run; how each acceptance clause is demonstrated and by which test and mutation; the exact mapping of each profile to flags; what happens with no profile (unchanged, and how you know); deviations from the plan and why; what is left; anything a release note must mention."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":256,"cache_read_input_tokens":823260,"output_tokens":6137,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":64},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":256,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6137,"cache_read_input_tokens":823260,"cache_creation_input_tokens":256,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":256},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":26790,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018mN8utniiDRY642coiRLxo":{"description":"Implement SB-1 permission profiles","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Implement unit SB-1 (named permission profiles) of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 plan, following the written design exactly: the \"SB-1\" section (and the shared notes and \"Decisions taken\") of `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md`. Read the whole SB-1 section and the decisions first; the other units in that file a