{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"2bd08633eecfb3ecc8da10165c4200e19a44316e3c1ba20cd1976d46d2e2bca5","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CmKcQ5hZxR7NbBh9S46Lse","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review PR #782 HK-1 hooks","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent security review of GitHub PR #782 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 782`, `gh pr diff 782`), \"feat(hooks): pre-tool and post-tool command hooks that can deny, never allow (HK-1)\", base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 782 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. Time-box: about 50 minutes; prioritise sections 1–4 and give a verdict on what you covered, listing the rest.\n\nContext: Nexus Code is a coding agent (TypeScript CLI in `cli/src`). Tool calls pass approval gates in `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, then the execution spine (`cli/src/core/spine.ts`: stages including `contextAdmission`, an authority stage that enforces read-only mode / authority grants / the workspace boundary, execution, `evidenceEmission`). Gate G-3 of the release: no client, hook, plugin, schedule or policy may bypass a gate. This PR adds command hooks: `cli/src/core/{hooks,hook_runner,content_trust,strict_json,process_identity}.ts`, `cli/src/{hooks_cli,doctor_hooks}.ts`, one block in `cli/src/agent.ts`, changes in `spine.ts`, `flight.ts`, `verify_service.ts` (helpers moved out), schema `docs/protocol/hooks-v1.schema.json`, docs `docs/HOOKS.md`, examples `examples/hooks/`. The design plan is `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/HK_PLAN.md`. The claimed rule: a hook can deny or annotate; its \"allow\" and any `updatedInput` are ignored; `ask` denies; a failing/timing-out pre-tool hook denies; an invalid operator hooks file denies every call; a project hooks file runs only after its exact content hash was approved by the operator. The PR body states one known wrinkle: for a write in read-only mode the hook runs before the spine's authority stage, cannot allow it, but a hook that denies replaces the gate's reason with HOOK_DENIED.\n\nTry to break it, by running wherever practical (write probes as extra untracked test files under `cli/test/` modelled on `cli/test/hooks_gate_bypass.test.ts`, `hooks_deny.test.ts`, `hooks_project_trust.test.ts`, `hook_runner.test.ts` and their helper `cli/test/helpers/hooks_fixture.ts`; delete them afterwards). Run tests with `TMPDIR=/dev/shm` if disk fsync makes them slow.\n1. Can a hook make something happen that would otherwise be denied, or change what runs? Try every output shape a hook could emit: allow/approve/permissionDecision variants, `updatedInput`, `continue: true`, extra fields (`suppressOutput`, `systemMessage`, `additionalContext`, `hookSpecificOutput`), malformed JSON, huge output, exit codes 0/1/2/other, output on stderr only, a hook that both denies and allows in a chain, a second hook after a deny. Does any annotation a hook returns reach the MODEL's context (prompt injection from a project hook: what text of a hook's output is shown to the model, is it bounded and labelled as untrusted, and can it instruct the model?) or reach the tool's arguments? Does a `post-tool` hook get to alter the tool result or the receipt/verification evidence (it must not be able to turn a failed verifier into a pass or hide output from the flight log)?\n2. Does adding hooks weaken an existing gate's outcome ordering? For each gate (headless approval, operator refusal, protected verification config, forbidden command rule, managed-policy forbid/require-human, delegation, read-only mode, workspace boundary, authority grants) confirm the receipt with an \"allow\" hook equals the receipt without hooks, and say what changes with a DENY hook (the stated wrinkle: reason replaced by HOOK_DENIED for spine-stage gates — is anything else lost, e.g. `denialReason` values that other code keys on such as `DECISION_REQUIRED_NO_OPERATOR`, `sandboxDenial` events, the VERIFICATION_CONFIG protection attempt record in the flight log, P10 budget counters — is a hook-denied call still counted against the tool-invocation budget, or can a hook make calls free)?\n3. Trust and configuration: who can make a hook run? Operator file `~/.config/nexus/hooks.json` (permissions/ownership checked? a symlink? what if the workspace is $HOME so the agent can write it); project hooks file (where, approved by content hash via `nexus hooks approve` — can the MODEL run that command through `run_command`/the shell tool, approving its own hook? is there a guard like the one that keeps a model from running `nexus trust`? check how approval state is stored — under the throwaway HOME's state dir — and whether the agent's tools can write that store directly); the approved file changing between approval check and execution (TOCTOU: is the content that was hashed the content that runs, including the script the hooks file POINTS at — approving the JSON does not pin the script it names; is that stated?); managed policy (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`) — can policy forbid project hooks, and is there any interaction with `plugins.disable_workspace`/`mcp.disable_workspace` semantics merged in PR #778? Subagents and operation units: do hooks apply to tool calls made by subagents (`runSubagent`) and operation workers, or can a model route around a deny hook by delegating?\n4. The runner: environment passed to the hook (no gateway key, no secrets — list what is passed), cwd, stdin content (does it include file contents or secrets from tool arguments unredacted? the PR says post-tool is redacted: and pre-tool?), timeout and kill (process tree killed by pid with start-time check; a hook that double-forks; a hook that ignores SIGTERM), output caps, concurrency (\"hooks are serialised per agent\": can a slow hook stall the turn deadline / cancellation — does operator cancel or the turn deadline kill a running hook?), and resource leaks (run `cli/test/leak_guard.test.ts`).\n5. Fail-closed claims: invalid operator file denies every call (also for read-only tools? is the agent then usable at all, and does `nexus doctor` say why); a pre-tool hook error denies; a post-tool hook error — what happens (should not undo a completed action nor crash the turn)?\n6. `verify_service.ts` refactor (\"helpers moved out to core/process_identity.ts\"): behaviour-preserving? Run `cli/test/verify_service.test.ts` and `cli/test/mcp_verify.test.ts` (about 3 minutes).\n7. Mutations (copy the source aside, restore from the copy; never `git checkout` to undo): make an allow output admit; make a hook error continue; skip the content-hash check for project hooks; pass the full environment. Each must fail a test; report which.\n8. Gates and suites at head, one file at a time: every `cli/test/hook*.test.ts`, `hooks_*.test.ts`, `content_trust`, `strict_json`, `doctor_hooks`; `approval_policy`; `mcp_tool_through_spine`; `sandbox_denial_events`; `hash_chain`; `leak_guard`; `protocol_parity`; `protocol_schema`; `check_requirements`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; from the worktree root `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`, `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`. This host may be under load: if a timing test fails, rerun it once alone and compare with `origin/release/v1.3.0` before reporting.\n9. Docs (`docs/HOOKS.md`): do they state the limits plainly (unsandboxed, the read-only wrinkle, the script behind an approved file not being pinned if that is so, project hooks not being a control over the agent)?\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev782 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev782/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev782/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev782/core/target/release/`. At the end delete probe files and run `git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev782`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev782/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one at a time (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). A throwaway HOME is mandatory. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 700 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; findings ranked with file:line, each marked confirmed-by-running or by-reading, blocking vs non-blocking (a way for a model or a workspace file to get a hook approved or run without the operator, or to route around a deny hook, or hook text steering the model unlabelled, is blocking); mutation results; test counts; gates; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XHSLh9e2pm1vpkEhSFkkKp","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"a253385a6f6d4f944","summary":"Finish #778 review, now merged","message":"Please finish your review of PR #778 and report. Note it was merged into release/v1.3.0 at 10:43Z (merge commit 580635d1), so findings become follow-ups against the release branch; your throwaway worktree ~/nexus-wt/rev778 may still exist with a mutation in progress: first restore any source file you had mutated from your copy, then finish only what is cheap (at most ~15 minutes more), then delete probe files and remove the worktree. Report under 500 words: verdict at the SHA you reviewed; bypasses found (ranked, file:line, confirmed-by-running or by-reading, blocking or not); mutation results so far; test counts; what you did not get to."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
