{"parentUuid":"88e1bd42-17d8-4593-9377-4cbbfe5d94ba","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMvykDQcpH3P72SJBu3Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1262,"cache_read_input_tokens":899392,"output_tokens":6310,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":161},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1262,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6310,"cache_read_input_tokens":899392,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1262,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1262},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1828,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011ChMvyjzFrjt7KHyoBQ9q8","requestRef":"9b4f2237-37ac-4b49-a3c3-5e7d8c5e8464","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"a2db47b6-cdf3-489d-a9d6-1ce9644ed9e3","timestamp":"2026-11-24T14:44:07.576Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"a2db47b6-cdf3-489d-a9d6-1ce9644ed9e3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMvykDQcpH3P72SJBu3Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WqpxMRfMwwtctq4h4s9TTJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review PR #779 PF-4 library entry","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent pre-merge review of GitHub PR #779 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 779`, `gh pr diff 779`), \"feat(library): expose sidecar embedding API\", base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 779 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. Time-box: about 40 minutes.\n\nContext: plan unit PF-4 of `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`: \"Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar. Acceptance: an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI.\" Decision D13 default: not published. The design is the PF-4 section of `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md` (read it and its \"Decisions taken\"): the library only drives the sidecar as a SEPARATE PROCESS and must not export `ApplicationCore` or anything that lets an embedder skip start-up checks; the client API cannot send allow-always; an approval the embedder did not explicitly grant is denied. Files: `cli/package.json`, `cli/src/lib.ts`, `cli/src/sidecar_client.ts`, `cli/tsconfig.lib.json`, tests `cli/test/{lib_entry,sidecar_client,embed_example}.test.ts`, `docs/EMBEDDING.md`, `docs/protocol/LIB_API_VERSIONS.json`, `examples/embed/*`, `scripts/smoke-package.sh`.\n\nAnswer with file:line evidence, running things where practical:\n1. Import side effects: in a child process, `import` the library entry (from source via tsx and, if you can build it, from `dist`): no commander parse, no `process.exit`, no stdout/stderr output, no read of the real HOME at import time, no listeners/timers left (the process exits by itself with code 0 and empty output). Does importing `lib.ts` transitively import `cli/src/index.ts` or anything with top-level side effects (trace the import graph: `node --import tsx -e` with `--trace-warnings` is not enough; read the imports)?\n2. What the library exports: list every export. Does any let an embedder run a turn in-process, skip the managed-policy start-up load (`requireManagedPolicyAtStartup` in `cli/src/index.ts`), skip the workspace-trust check, select auto approval, or grant allow-always? How does the client start the sidecar (which binary/entry, which argv, which env is passed through — does it pass the embedder's whole environment including secrets to the sidecar, and is that what the CLI would do anyway), and can the embedder pass arbitrary extra argv such as `--yes` / `--mode auto` (if the API has an `args` escape hatch, is auto approval refused at the sidecar as it is for `nexus acp`? try it).\n3. Approvals through the client: default deny when the embedder registers no handler; what the handler may return (allow once / deny only?); can a handler's return value of an unexpected shape (truthy object, `{always:true}`, a string) be taken as allow or allow-always (try each); a late reply after timeout or after the turn ended; concurrent approvals. The last commit is \"fix(library): approve configured verifier in embedder\" — read it: does the embedder now auto-approve a VERIFIER without the operator (verifier approval is a security decision: a discovered verifier runs project code)? Under what configuration, and is it limited to a verifier the embedder explicitly configured? Say plainly whether an embedder-supplied or project-supplied verifier can now run without any human approval and whether that matches how the CLI behaves with `--authoritative-verifier`.\n4. Robustness of the client: bounded line reading from the sidecar (a huge line), a sidecar that exits or hangs (timeouts, kill by pid, no orphan), protocol version check against `PROTOCOL_VERSION`, stdout purity assumptions. Run `cli/test/leak_guard.test.ts`.\n5. Packaging: `cli/package.json` changes (`exports`, `types`, `private`, `files`, scripts, name): do they change what `scripts/package-linux.sh` puts in the tarball, `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt` or the SBOM (the release package is built twice in CI and compared byte for byte)? Did `test`/`test:cli` scripts stay as `cli/test/release_workflow_parity.test.ts` pins them (run that test)? Was a dependency added (lockfile diff)? Does `tsconfig.lib.json` emit type declarations, and is that emit part of `npm run build` (does the build still succeed: run `npm --prefix cli run build` in the worktree; `cli/node_modules` there is a copy, do not run npm install)? What does the `scripts/smoke-package.sh` addition check?\n6. The acceptance: read `cli/test/embed_example.test.ts` and `examples/embed/embed.mjs`: does the example really spawn the sidecar, run a task against the scripted gateway fixture (no live inference) and get a verification verdict (which outcome: VERIFIED? with which verifier, approved how — tie this to question 3)? Would the test fail if the verdict were not established?\n7. Mutations (copy the source aside, restore from the copy; never `git checkout` to undo): make the default approval answer allow; let `{always:true}` through; remove the import-side-effect guard if there is one. Each must fail a test; report which.\n8. Run at head, one file at a time: `lib_entry`, `sidecar_client`, `embed_example`, `leak_guard`, `release_workflow_parity`, `check_requirements`, `sidecar` (slow; this host may be under load: on a timing failure rerun once alone and compare with `origin/release/v1.3.0`), `protocol_parity`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; from the worktree root `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, `node scripts/check_deprecated_lock.mjs cli/package-lock.json`, `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n9. Docs (`docs/EMBEDDING.md`): claim only what code and tests show, and state the limits (not published; the sidecar is the trust boundary; what the embedder is responsible for)?\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev779 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev779/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev779/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev779/core/target/release/`. At the end delete probe files and run `git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev779`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev779/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one at a time (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). A throwaway HOME is mandatory. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 650 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; the export list; findings ranked with file:line, each marked confirmed-by-running or by-reading, blocking vs non-blocking (any way for an embedder-side default or a project file to get a verifier or a tool call approved without an explicit grant, or to reach auto approval, is blocking); mutation results; test counts; gates; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1262,"cache_read_input_tokens":899392,"output_tokens":63