 gate (cyclomatic complexity 17, nesting 3: it must not grow): put the logic in helpers and call them without adding a branch there. Do slice 2 ONLY if slice 1 is complete, committed and green.\n\nRequirements to prove with tests (the acceptance and G-3):\n- Two units that each pass alone and conflict when combined (a semantic conflict that merges textually clean but fails the verifier on the merged tree; and a textual conflict): the first integrates with a verified record; the second is refused, with a decision that names both units and the failing check; nothing is integrated silently and the merged tree is left as it was before the refused merge.\n- A unit that edits another integrated unit's test files (or the verifier's own configuration, see how `cli/src/core/verify*.ts` and `security.ts` classify verification files — the plan could not determine which function to reuse: find it) is refused.\n- The re-run on the pre-merge tree distinguishes \"the new unit broke it\" from \"it was already broken\" (an already-failing check does not get blamed on the new unit, and is reported).\n- The cap of 16 distinct checks: what happens at 17 (refuse or report, never silently skip a check and call the result verified).\n- Verifier runs happen with the same confinement the rest of verification uses (read how `verifyChange`/the verify service sandbox a verifier: the plan's risk note says PA-2's checks \"run unsandboxed on the host\" — do NOT ship that if the existing verification path can be reused to run them sandboxed; if it truly cannot, stop and report rather than adding an unsandboxed execution path).\n- No model text, tool result or workspace content can mark a merge verified or skip the check; the record is written by the runner only.\n- State written before this change still loads (schema backward compatibility), and a state at the new version is refused by... check how older code treats a newer `schema_version` and follow the repo's rule.\n\nHard constraints:\n- No surface here may weaken an approval gate, verification-path protection, managed policy or the VERIFIED outcome ladder.\n- Complexity gate: `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl`, `driveOperation` must not grow (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). Run from the worktree root: `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Git handling: use the repo's hardened git wrapper (find it: `cli/src/worktrunk/git.ts` or the `git()` helper the verify code uses) with its pinned flags; never run plain `git` with a repository's own filters/hooks enabled in a directory a unit controlled.\n- Do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`).\n- Every new on-disk format or schema version gets its ledger entry where the repo keeps them and earlier frozen versions stay byte-identical; if the sidecar protocol schema changes, regenerate `docs/protocol/sidecar-v1.schema.json` (`node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_schema.mjs`) and `cli/test/fixtures/protocol_v1_schema_samples.jsonl` (`node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_samples.mjs`); CI checks both.\n- Linux x86_64 only; Node 22 and 24 (only 24 is installed here).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa2/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME is mandatory: never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference, no network.\n- First create the links the worktree needs if missing: `ln -s ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules cli/node_modules` and `mkdir -p core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} core/target/release/` (they exist already if `ls cli/node_modules` works). Do not run npm install or cargo; do not change Rust.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; stop every child by its own pid in a `finally` (never `pkill`); a test file must finish well under 300 s. Stress each new file: 3 runs in a row; the host may be under load, so use generous waits and polling.\n- Before each commit run, one at a time: every new test file; every `cli/test/operation*.test.ts`; `goal_lifecycle`, `goal_ledger_propagation`; `verify_command`, `verify_hostile`; `leak_guard`; `protocol_parity`; `protocol_schema`; `check_requirements`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate.\n- For each guard, show by mutation that a test fails without it (copy the source file aside and restore from the copy; do not use `git checkout <file>` while you have uncommitted work in it) and read the failure message to check it fails for the right reason.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the plan; nothing speculative. Update `docs/v1.3.0/` with a short PA-2 document stating what is and is not covered.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n\nReport (under 550 words): per slice: done / not done, commit hash, files added and changed, tests added with exact pass counts and the 3-run result, gates run; how the acceptance is demonstrated and by which tests and mutations; how verifier runs are confined; the schema version chosen and how it must be reconciled with PR #780; deviations from the plan and why; what is left; anything a release note must mention.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a2b26453fbd1df4f6.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1b1aae07-8c87-458e-9834-939ea06e55df","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-11-24T14:45:06.718Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a253385a6f6d4f944</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01XHSLh9e2pm1vpkEhSFkkKp</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a253385a6f6d4f944.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review PR #778 policy enforcement\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS** at `afb4ed64f62d4b597eb75286e3fb92209ad367fb`. The head did not move. The PR has since merged. I found no loosening bypass of the stated invariant, but there is one real gap: a name-only `mcp.allow` entry is not a trust boundary against a workspace file.\n\n**Bypasses, ranked**\n1. **Non-blocking, but should be documented or tightened.** A bare-name `mcp.allow` entry admits any definition with that name (`permitsMcpServer`, `core/managed_policy.ts:820-839`; `McpConnection.policyDescriptor`, `mcp.ts:129-135`).\n   - Confirmed by running: with `allow=[\"files\"]` and a trusted workspace, a `.mcp.json` defining `files` with an attacker `command` started and ran that program.\n   - The same holds for a WebSocket, HTTP or SSE URL under an allowed name. `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` says \"only a name entry admits\" a WebSocket server, but it never says a name entry pins nothing and that the workspace can redefine the name.\n   - Fix: say so in the docs, and recommend `mcp.disable_workspace=true` or `command:`/`url:` entries. Optionally reject a name-only match when `fromWorkspace` is true.\n2. **Non-blocking.** A hard link to a workspace file, placed in the user config directory, counts as non-workspace under `disable_workspace` (`isWorkspaceFile`, `workspace_trust.ts`).\n   - Confirmed by running: the server started.\n   - A repository cannot create that link, and `lstat` link-count detection is not feasible. It is a documentation-level limit.\n3. **Non-blocking.** `nexus policy check &lt;file&gt;` echoes parts of an arbitrary readable file in the parse error (`policy_cli.ts:159-169`).\n   - Confirmed by running: the first token of line 1 appeared as an \"invalid key\", and the value in `allow = sk-...` appeared as an \"unsupported value\".\n   - Unreadable files and `/dev/zero` give clean errors, and `--json` stayed valid JSON with exit 2.\n   - Fix: stop quoting the offending token, or truncate or redact it.\n4. **Doc gap.** The `sha256:` pin covers only the entry file, not the modules it imports. The hash-then-import race is documented.\n\n**Covered and held**\n- MCP name tricks: case, trailing space, Cyrillic look-alike and zero-width space were all refused (confirmed by running).\n- MCP config symlinked into the workspace and a symlinked parent directory were both treated as workspace under `disable_workspace`. A workspace symlink pointing out of the workspace was also treated as workspace.\n- With the workspace set to `$HOME`, the user config is labelled workspace. `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` documents this.\n- `addServer` and `addConnection` refuse an unlisted server; nothing was spawned.\n- ACP: `mcpServers` is validated and ignored (`acp/validate.ts:152`), confirmed by reading.\n- The policy decision is made per call from the frozen process policy.\n- A plugin symlinked from the user plugin directory into the workspace was not imported under `plugins.disable_workspace`. A `sha256:` mismatch refused.\n- A policy that cannot be read admits nothing, confirmed by reading `refusedByPolicy`.\n- Always-approve: `resolveApproval` clamps through `mayRememberAlways`. The `agent.setApprovalMode` clamp covers `/mode auto`, so `setApprovalMode(\"auto\")` cannot enter auto. Only `alwaysApprove` (set at `sidecar.ts:902`) approves silently, and it is now gated.\n- `nexus verify`: `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK` is read in `sandbox.ts` (lines 200, 720 and 892), not in `verifyFlagRefusal`. I did not trace whether the env var reaches a verify run, so I could not rule out a `--sandbox-network` bypass via that variable.\n- `doctor --fix` with a broken user `policy.toml` left the file byte-identical.\n\n**Mutations (each restored from a copy)**\n- Remove the `loadAndConnect` gate: allowlists test 10 failures.\n- Remove the `addConnection` gate: 1 failure.\n- Remove the `addServer` gate: 1 failure.\n- Remove the plugin gate: 5 failures.\n- Make `verifyFlagRefusal` return nothing: 10 failures.\n- Make \"always\" stick: `managed_policy_always` had 1 failure.\n\nAll were caught. I did not mutate the REPL path.\n\n**Tests and gates, all on the PR head**\n| Test file | Pass | Fail |\n|---|---|---|\n| `managed_policy_allowlists` | 18 | 0 |\n| `managed_policy_verify` | 12 | 0 |\n| `policy_cli` | 23 | 0 |\n| `managed_policy_enforcement` | 113 | 0 |\n| `managed_policy_schema` | 57 | 0 |\n| `managed_policy_always` | 6 | 0 |\n| `mcp` | 4 | 0 |\n| `mcp_tool_through_spine` | 3 | 0 |\n| `approval_policy` | 16 | 0 |\n| `leak_guard` | 6 | 0 |\n| `sidecar` | 51 | 7 |\n| `sidecar` on base (`45520edcf`, second parent of the PR head) | 48 | 6 |\n\n- The base `sidecar` run has 6 failures against 7 on the PR. The failing names I compared differ in titles only, mostly timeouts, so this does not show a regression. I did not run the failing sidecar tests individually, so the extra failure on the PR is unattributed.\n- `tsc --noEmit`, `check_critical_complexity`, `check_invariants` (18 rows) and `check_requirements` all pass.\n\n**Not done**\n- `context_convergence`.\n- A REPL mutation, a direct REPL or sidecar probe for `strict`, and a probe with no policy present beyond the existing suites.\n- Whether `NEXUS_SANDBOX_NETWORK` reaches `nexus verify` (noted above).\n- `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` and `--yes` at startup, and subagent or goal-runner mode inheritance. I only confirmed by reading that `setApprovalMode` clamps.\n- A mid-session policy change on disk, beyond reading that the decision uses the frozen policy.\n\nThe probe file is deleted, `git status` was clean, and the `rev778` worktree is removed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>87531</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>2</tool_uses><duration_ms>25710</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","reason":"absorbed_mid_turn","commandUuid":"bd7b8f95-cb0c-47de-8af4-1b860fd3945f","deliveryId":"ed801ea9-ecfe-4b91-8fe6-6ad9bc59ba9d"}
