{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"3f9520580fee103d995dda3f1cd1bba8fad2344d1e655e9bd294adbe24b88490","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WqpxMRfMwwtctq4h4s9TTJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review PR #779 PF-4 library entry","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent pre-merge review of GitHub PR #779 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 779`, `gh pr diff 779`), \"feat(library): expose sidecar embedding API\", base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 779 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. Time-box: about 40 minutes.\n\nContext: plan unit PF-4 of `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`: \"Library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar. Acceptance: an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI.\" Decision D13 default: not published. The design is the PF-4 section of `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md` (read it and its \"Decisions taken\"): the library only drives the sidecar as a SEPARATE PROCESS and must not export `ApplicationCore` or anything that lets an embedder skip start-up checks; the client API cannot send allow-always; an approval the embedder did not explicitly grant is denied. Files: `cli/package.json`, `cli/src/lib.ts`, `cli/src/sidecar_client.ts`, `cli/tsconfig.lib.json`, tests `cli/test/{lib_entry,sidecar_client,embed_example}.test.ts`, `docs/EMBEDDING.md`, `docs/protocol/LIB_API_VERSIONS.json`, `examples/embed/*`, `scripts/smoke-package.sh`.\n\nAnswer with file:line evidence, running things where practical:\n1. Import side effects: in a child process, `import` the library entry (from source via tsx and, if you can build it, from `dist`): no commander parse, no `process.exit`, no stdout/stderr output, no read of the real HOME at import time, no listeners/timers left (the process exits by itself with code 0 and empty output). Does importing `lib.ts` transitively import `cli/src/index.ts` or anything with top-level side effects (trace the import graph: `node --import tsx -e` with `--trace-warnings` is not enough; read the imports)?\n2. What the library exports: list every export. Does any let an embedder run a turn in-process, skip the managed-policy start-up load (`requireManagedPolicyAtStartup` in `cli/src/index.ts`), skip the workspace-trust check, select auto approval, or grant allow-always? How does the client start the sidecar (which binary/entry, which argv, which env is passed through — does it pass the embedder's whole environment including secrets to the sidecar, and is that what the CLI would do anyway), and can the embedder pass arbitrary extra argv such as `--yes` / `--mode auto` (if the API has an `args` escape hatch, is auto approval refused at the sidecar as it is for `nexus acp`? try it).\n3. Approvals through the client: default deny when the embedder registers no handler; what the handler may return (allow once / deny only?); can a handler's return value of an unexpected shape (truthy object, `{always:true}`, a string) be taken as allow or allow-always (try each); a late reply after timeout or after the turn ended; concurrent approvals. The last commit is \"fix(library): approve configured verifier in embedder\" — read it: does the embedder now auto-approve a VERIFIER without the operator (verifier approval is a security decision: a discovered verifier runs project code)? Under what configuration, and is it limited to a verifier the embedder explicitly configured? Say plainly whether an embedder-supplied or project-supplied verifier can now run without any human approval and whether that matches how the CLI behaves with `--authoritative-verifier`.\n4. Robustness of the client: bounded line reading from the sidecar (a huge line), a sidecar that exits or hangs (timeouts, kill by pid, no orphan), protocol version check against `PROTOCOL_VERSION`, stdout purity assumptions. Run `cli/test/leak_guard.test.ts`.\n5. Packaging: `cli/package.json` changes (`exports`, `types`, `private`, `files`, scripts, name): do they change what `scripts/package-linux.sh` puts in the tarball, `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt` or the SBOM (the release package is built twice in CI and compared byte for byte)? Did `test`/`test:cli` scripts stay as `cli/test/release_workflow_parity.test.ts` pins them (run that test)? Was a dependency added (lockfile diff)? Does `tsconfig.lib.json` emit type declarations, and is that emit part of `npm run build` (does the build still succeed: run `npm --prefix cli run build` in the worktree; `cli/node_modules` there is a copy, do not run npm install)? What does the `scripts/smoke-package.sh` addition check?\n6. The acceptance: read `cli/test/embed_example.test.ts` and `examples/embed/embed.mjs`: does the example really spawn the sidecar, run a task against the scripted gateway fixture (no live inference) and get a verification verdict (which outcome: VERIFIED? with which verifier, approved how — tie this to question 3)? Would the test fail if the verdict were not established?\n7. Mutations (copy the source aside, restore from the copy; never `git checkout` to undo): make the default approval answer allow; let `{always:true}` through; remove the import-side-effect guard if there is one. Each must fail a test; report which.\n8. Run at head, one file at a time: `lib_entry`, `sidecar_client`, `embed_example`, `leak_guard`, `release_workflow_parity`, `check_requirements`, `sidecar` (slow; this host may be under load: on a timing failure rerun once alone and compare with `origin/release/v1.3.0`), `protocol_parity`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; from the worktree root `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, `node scripts/check_deprecated_lock.mjs cli/package-lock.json`, `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n9. Docs (`docs/EMBEDDING.md`): claim only what code and tests show, and state the limits (not published; the sidecar is the trust boundary; what the embedder is responsible for)?\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev779 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev779/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev779/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev779/core/target/release/`. At the end delete probe files and run `git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev779`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev779/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one at a time (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). A throwaway HOME is mandatory. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 650 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; the export list; findings ranked with file:line, each marked confirmed-by-running or by-reading, blocking vs non-blocking (any way for an embedder-side default or a project file to get a verifier or a tool call approved without an explicit grant, or to reach auto approval, is blocking); mutation results; test counts; gates; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WRG8TN3cD7p8FoW8eYwqst","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement PA-2 verified merges","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Implement unit PA-2 (verified merge arbitration for parallel operation units) of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 plan, following the written design exactly: the \"PA-2\" section (and the shared notes and \"Decisions taken\") of `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md`. Read the whole PA-2 section and the decisions first; the other units in that file are NOT your job. Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/pa2` (branch `v1/pa2-verified-merges`, at the current tip of `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where the plan leaves a detail open choose the conservative option and list it in your report.\n\nThe unit, from `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` (re-read the PA-2 row, the PA-1/PA-3/PA-4 documents under `docs/v1.3.0/`, and gate G-3): \"conflicting units fail verification on the merged tree\". Plan decisions that bind you: after each merge of a unit's work, re-run every verifier already integrated plus the operator's verifier, capped at 16 distinct checks; a failing check is re-run on the pre-merge tree to decide whether the new unit caused it; a unit that edits another integrated unit's test files is refused; the operation schema goes to v5 (check first what version it is NOW: other work merged since the plan was written, including PA-3 changes in PR #758 and a goal token cap in PR #775, and BG-1 in open PR #780 also plans an operation schema bump with an `origin` field: read `cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts` on this branch and `gh pr diff 780 -- cli/src/core/operation_schema.ts` to see what it does, pick the next free version on THIS branch, and say in your report how the two will have to be reconciled); \"neither is integrated silently\" means the first unit lands with a verified record and the second is refused with a decision naming both units.\n\nDeliver the plan's slices in order, COMMITTING LOCALLY at the end of each slice (message `feat(operation): <what> (PA-2, slice N)` ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`), each independently green:\n- Slice 1 (`operation_git.ts` and its tests; no hot file): the merge-and-verify primitive as the plan defines it.\n- Slice 2 (runner, schema bump, operation view): `cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts` and `operation_schema.ts`. `driveOperation` in `operation_runner.ts` is under a CI complexity gate (cyclomatic complexity 17, nesting 3: it must not grow): put the logic in helpers and call them without adding a branch there. Do slice 2 ONLY if slice 1 is complete, committed and green.\n\nRequirements to prove with tests (the acceptance and G-3):\n- Two units that each pass alone and conflict when combined (a semantic conflict that merges textually clean but fails the verifier on the merged tree; and a textual conflict): the first integrates with a verified record; the second is refused, with a decision that names both units and the failing check; nothing is integrated silently and the merged tree is left as it was before the refused merge.\n- A unit that edits another integrated unit's test files (or the verifier's own configuration, see how `cli/src/core/verify*.ts` and `security.ts` classify verification files — the plan could not determine which function to reuse: find it) is refused.\n- The re-run on the pre-merge tree distinguishes \"the new unit broke it\" from \"it was already broken\" (an already-failing check does not get blamed on the new unit, and is reported).\n- The cap of 16 distinct checks: what happens at 17 (refuse or report, never silently skip a check and call the result verified).\n- Verifier runs happen with the same confinement the rest of verification uses (read how `verifyChange`/the verify service sandbox a verifier: the plan's risk note says PA-2's checks \"run unsandboxed on the host\" — do NOT ship that if the existing verification path can be reused to run them sandboxed; if it truly cannot, stop and report rather than adding an unsandboxed execution path).\n- No model text, tool result or workspace content can mark a merge verified or skip the check; the record is written by the runner only.\n- State written before this change still loads (schema backward compatibility), and a state at the new version is refused by... check how older code treats a newer `schema_version` and follow the repo's rule.\n\nHard constraints:\n- No surface here may weaken an approval gate, verification-path protection, managed policy or the VERIFIED outcome ladder.\n- Complexity gate: `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl`, `driveOperation` must not grow (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). Run from the worktree root: `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Git handling: use the repo's hardened git wrapper (find it: `cli/src/worktrunk/git.ts` or the `git()` helper the verify code uses) with its pinned flags; never run plain `git` with a repository's own filters/hooks enabled in a directory a unit controlled.\n- Do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`).\n- Every new on-disk format or schema version gets its ledger entry where the repo keeps them and earlier frozen versions stay byte-identical; if the sidecar protocol schema changes, regenerate `docs/protocol/sidecar-v1.schema.json` (`node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_schema.mjs`) and `cli/test/fixtures/protocol_v1_schema_samples.jsonl` (`node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_samples.mjs`); CI checks both.\n- Linux x86_64 only; Node 22 and 24 (only 24 is installed here).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/pa2/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME is mandatory: never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference, no network.\n- First create the links the worktree needs if missing: `ln -s ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules cli/node_modules` and `mkdir -p core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} core/target/release/` (they exist already if `ls cli/node_modules` works). Do not run npm install or cargo; do not change Rust.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; stop every child by its own pid in a `finally` (never `pkill`); a test file must finish well under 300 s. Stress each new file: 3 runs in a row; the host may be under load, so use generous waits and polling.\n- Before each commit run, one at a time: every new test file; every `cli/test/operation*.test.ts`; `goal_lifecycle`, `goal_ledger_propagation`; `verify_command`, `verify_hostile`; `leak_guard`; `protocol_parity`; `protocol_schema`; `check_requirements`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate.\n- For each guard, show by mutation that a test fails without it (copy the source file aside and restore from the copy; do not use `git checkout <file>` while you have uncommitted work in it) and read the failure message to check it fails for the right reason.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the plan; nothing speculative. Update `docs/v1.3.0/` with a short PA-2 document stating what is and is not covered.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n\nReport (under 550 words): per slice: done / not done, commit hash, files added and changed, tests added with exact pass counts and the 3-run result, gates run; how the acceptance is demonstrated and by which tests and mutations; how verifier runs are confined; the schema version chosen and how it must be reconciled with PR #780; deviations from the plan and why; what is left; anything a release note must mention."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
