{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"fe7034a65c48a8c9d8b6d405af99170fbe51d28f3928807c412c5a0b4956b53a","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GFYkvESNCgFeANyzBNkjDK","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review PR #783 policy follow-ups","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Short, time-boxed independent review (aim for 20 minutes) of GitHub PR #783 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 783`, `gh pr diff 783`), \"fix(policy): a name in mcp.allow no longer admits a workspace-defined server; policy check stops echoing file text\", base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 783 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. The change: a few lines in `permitsMcpServer` in `cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts`, a helper `withoutFileText` in `cli/src/policy_cli.ts`, one test each in `cli/test/managed_policy_merge.test.ts` and `cli/test/policy_cli.test.ts`, and a paragraph in `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md`.\n\nContext: managed policy `mcp.allow` is an operator allowlist of MCP servers; entries are a bare server name, `command:/absolute/path`, or `url:https://origin`. A previous review found that with a name-only entry, a workspace `.mcp.json` could define a server of that name with its own program and it would start. The fix: a server whose descriptor has `fromWorkspace: true` is no longer matched by a name entry, only by `command:`/`url:`.\n\nCheck, by running where quick:\n1. Is `fromWorkspace` set correctly on every path that reaches `permitsMcpServer` (`cli/src/mcp.ts`: `loadAndConnect`, `addServer`, `addConnection`, `policyDescriptor(fromWorkspace)`, `isWorkspaceFile` in `cli/src/workspace_trust.ts`)? Can a workspace-defined server arrive with `fromWorkspace: false` — e.g. `/mcp add` typed in a session whose text came from the model, a config merged from user + workspace files where the workspace file OVERRIDES fields of a user-defined server of the same name (name from the user file, command from the workspace file: which `fromWorkspace` does the merged entry get? read how configs are merged), a plugin-contributed server, an include/extends mechanism?\n2. The new rule's effect on a workspace server that has a bare (non-absolute) command such as `npx`: `policyDescriptor` gives it no `command`, so it can be admitted by nothing now — confirm it is refused with the new message and that this is what the docs say.\n3. `withoutFileText`: the regex cuts quoted tokens of 9+ characters unless they match `^[a-z][a-z_.]{0,63}$`. Can file content still be read out through errors: tokens shorter than 9 characters (acceptable), a secret made only of lowercase letters/dots/underscores (it would be echoed in full: note it), unquoted echoes elsewhere in the error (`line N:` only?), the `--json` output, a file with one very long line (is the whole line ever echoed)? Run `nexus policy check` logic via `checkPolicyFile` on a few crafted files in a temp dir.\n4. Run at head: `managed_policy_merge`, `policy_cli`, `managed_policy_allowlists`, `managed_policy_enforcement`, `mcp`, `leak_guard`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`. Mutate (copy the file aside, restore from the copy; never `git checkout` to undo): remove `&& !server.fromWorkspace` → the new merge test must fail.\n5. Does the doc paragraph claim only what the code does?\n\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev783 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev783/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev783/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev783/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev783/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one at a time. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No network. Stop any process by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. At the end delete probe files and run `git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev783`. Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` if it looks filtered). If you run out of time, report what you have.\n\nReport under 400 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; findings with file:line, confirmed-by-running or by-reading, blocking vs not (a route by which a workspace-defined server still starts under a name-only allowlist is blocking); test counts; mutation result; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
