`interactive` predicate, with no environment variable.\n- **Tests:** `hooks_self_approval` \"protected paths…\" and \"…approves it by run_command does not end with an active hook\" (real CLI through `run_command`); `hooks_cli` \"approve is refused without a terminal…\" and \"on a terminal --sha256 says which content is meant…\"; two assertions added to `managed_policy_enforcement`.\n- **Mutations:** removing either protected name, or the `interactive()` check, fails these tests.\n\n**What the TTY guard cannot stop:** `nexus trust` has no TTY guard and there is no agent marker on child processes, so nothing existed to reuse. An unsandboxed command in auto mode can fake a terminal with `script` or write the store directly. This is stated in `docs/HOOKS.md`.\n\n## Blocker 2: workspace = HOME\n- **Fix** (`cli/src/core/hooks.ts:403`, applied at `:548`): an operator hooks file inside the workspace is invalid, so every call is denied with \"Start Nexus in a project directory\". The owner and mode check was already there.\n- **Test:** \"with the home directory as the workspace…\". The file tools are refused, a planted file is not run, and the same file is accepted from a project directory.\n- **Mutation:** accepting the inside-workspace file fails it.\n\n## Blocker 3: script pinning\n- **Fix** (`cli/src/core/hooks.ts:311`, `:382`): the approval records the SHA-256 of the program and of every argument that is a workspace file, re-checked before each run. A change makes the hooks inactive and the notice names the file.\n- **Refusals:** a relative path leaving the workspace and a symlink out of it are refused.\n- **My choice:** project hooks must use array commands; a shell string cannot be pinned, so it is refused.\n- **Trusted as installed:** programs outside the workspace (documented).\n- **Shown in:** `nexus hooks list` and `approve`.\n- **Tests:** \"an approval covers the script the hooks run…\" (overwritten with the agent's `write_file`); `hooks_cli` \"a script the approved hooks run is covered…\" and the unpinnable cases.\n- **Mutations:** skipping the file comparison, allowing shell strings, or allowing a link out each fail them.\n\n## Schema\n`ExtensionTrustEntry` gains optional `files`. The version stays 1, the schema is regenerated, a second ledger entry is added and `--check` passes. Older approvals of hooks that run workspace files ask again.\n\n## Smaller items (all done)\n- **Denial framing** (`hooks.ts:640`): a fixed sentence plus the reason as one JSON string, capped; the \"IGNORE PREVIOUS INSTRUCTIONS\" test passes.\n- **Docs:** the four corrections are in `docs/HOOKS.md`.\n- **Subagent test:** added and passing.\n\n## Counts\n- `hooks_self_approval` 6 and `hooks_cli` 10, each 3 runs in a row.\n- The other hook files, `leak_guard`, `approval_policy`, `check_requirements`, the `managed_policy_*` files, `policy_cli`, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `verification_guard`, `spine` and `examples` all pass with 0 failures.\n- `tsc`, the complexity gate, `check_invariants` and `check_doc_versions` are clean.\n\n## Open\n- The unsandboxed fake-terminal and direct-store-write route above.\n- Files a pinned script sources are not covered.\n- An operator hook that names a workspace-relative program is not pinned; the docs advise against it.\n- Notices for unapproved project hooks still appear on the first tool call, not at session start.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>518685</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>23</tool_uses><duration_ms>815749</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>\n</system-reminder>"}]}}
