{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"b4ec6ebcd0c65942fa95f2008b6f58c8b70b07d8b9c1bc290c7f940afee61bef","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A7sn2TiPRcY8mAvmARirez","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/nexus-code && gh pr comment 782 --body \"**Independent re-review at \\`258f72ffc59376ed675e7d9e971ef88e9d8a5083\\` — REQUEST CHANGES. Blockers 1 and 2 are closed; blocker 3 is closed only for plain file arguments. I am fixing the rest on this branch.**\n\n- **Self-approval: closed (run).** 31 path spellings through \\`write_file\\`, \\`edit_file\\`, \\`patch_file\\` and \\`delete_file\\` (124 attempts: \\`..\\`, symlinked parents, a symlink to the store, case variants, workspaces of the repo, the home directory, \\`~/.config\\`, \\`~/.config/nexus\\` and the parent of home) never changed the store or the operator file. \\`nexus hooks approve\\` writes nothing with either side piped or with \\`--sha256\\` alone. The docs state the unsandboxed-shell limit honestly. Not run: whether \\`--sandbox\\` masks the store.\n- **Workspace = home: closed (run)**, including \\`~/.config\\`, the parent of home and symlinks to each.\n- **Script pinning: partly closed (run).** Overwriting \\`./guard.sh\\`, \\`bash ./guard.sh\\` or \\`node scripts/guard.js\\` stops the hook and names the file; symlinks out, swapped parents, late-created scripts, \\`../\\`, shell strings and oversized scripts are refused. **Blocking: four forms that name a workspace file are not pinned**, and after the agent replaced the file the hook ran the new content with no notice: a directory argument (\\`[\\\"node\\\",\\\"hookdir\\\"]\\`), \\`[\\\"sh\\\",\\\"-c\\\",\\\"./guard.sh x\\\"]\\`, \\`[\\\"bash\\\",\\\"-c\\\",\\\"exec ./guard.sh\\\"]\\`, \\`[\\\"node\\\",\\\"--require=./pre.js\\\",…]\\`. Cause: only arguments that are themselves existing regular files are pinned (\\`core/hooks.ts\\`, \\`coveredProjectFiles\\`). The doc's wording implies more than is done.\n- **Denial framing and old trust records: closed (run).**\n\n**Not blocking:** the pinned files are hashed once per call and the interpreter re-reads the script later (a window only a concurrent unsandboxed process can use); the state is re-hashed on every call (up to 32 files of 1 MiB); two mutations survive (dropping the stdout-terminal check; dropping the 'approved file no longer named' comparison).\n\n**Tests at this head, 0 failures:** eleven hook files (88 tests), \\`content_trust\\` 7, \\`strict_json\\` 9, \\`doctor_hooks\\` 5, \\`security\\` 14, \\`security_gates\\` 10, \\`workspace_trust\\` 5, \\`managed_policy_enforcement\\` 113, \\`approval_policy\\` 16, \\`leak_guard\\` 6, \\`check_requirements\\` 28; \\`tsc\\`, the complexity gate and \\`check_invariants\\` pass.\" | tail -1","description":"Post the re-review verdict on PR 782"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D4LJ7MK3DMf7upBPWxzeUx","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"a317b41792fa50650","summary":"Close remaining script-pinning gaps in hooks","message":"Re-review of your fix (now at 258f72ff in /home/mike/nexus-wt/hk1, release branch merged in on top of 3f769c90b; start with `git status` and `git log --oneline -4`; same rules; commit locally, do not push; message `fix(hooks): <what>` + the Co-Authored-By line). Blockers 1 and 2 are closed. Blocker 3 is closed only for plain file arguments. Still BLOCKING, each confirmed by running: after approval, the agent replaced a workspace file with `write_file` and the hook ran the new content with no notice, for these project-hook commands:\n - a directory argument: `[\"node\",\"hookdir\"]`\n - `[\"sh\",\"-c\",\"./guard.sh x\"]` and `[\"bash\",\"-c\",\"exec ./guard.sh\"]`\n - `[\"node\",\"--require=./pre.js\", ...]`\nCause: `coveredProjectFiles` (cli/src/core/hooks.ts, the `for (const arg of args)` loop) pins only arguments that are themselves existing regular files.\n\nFix by making the rule one that cannot be argued around: a project hook command is accepted only if every argument is either (a) pinned (an existing regular file inside the workspace, hashed) or (b) provably not a reference into the workspace. Concretely, refuse as invalid (unpinnable, with a message naming the argument and saying what to do: put the logic in one script file and name it directly):\n - any argument that resolves inside the workspace to a directory, or to anything that is not a regular file;\n - any argument of the form `--opt=VALUE` / `-oVALUE`-style where VALUE (after the `=`) resolves to an existing path inside the workspace: pin it if a regular file, else refuse (do pin the `=value` case rather than refuse, so `--require=./pre.js` is covered and named in `list`);\n - inline-code forms for project hooks: when command[0]'s basename is a known shell or interpreter (sh, bash, zsh, dash, ksh, fish, node, nodejs, bun, deno, python, python3, perl, ruby, php, pwsh, env, busybox, and versioned names like python3.12) refuse `-c`, `-e`, `--eval`, `-p`, `--print`, `-m`, `-r`/`--require`/`--import`/`--loader` given as a separate flag whose NEXT argument is not a pinned file (pin it if it is), and refuse `env` as command[0] altogether for project hooks (it hides the real program) — decide the exact list by what is simple and sound, and state the rule in the docs as: \"a project hook names a program and files; inline code is not accepted\";\n - any remaining argument that contains a `/` and resolves (relative to the workspace) to an existing path inside the workspace that is not pinned.\n Also consider the default-module resolution case the directory form stood for (`node hookdir` → index.js): the directory refusal covers it. And bare names with no slash that are existing workspace files (`[\"node\",\"guard.js\"]`) must already be pinned: confirm with a test.\nBe honest in docs/HOOKS.md about what remains: this is a refusal list for known interpreters, a program outside that list that takes inline code, files a pinned script itself loads, and the window between the hash check and the interpreter reading the file are not covered; correct the sentence at HOOKS.md ~86-93 that implies every reference is covered. Operator hooks are unaffected by these refusals.\n\nAlso fix the two surviving mutations: add a test with a terminal on stdin only (and one on stdout only) so that dropping the stdout check fails; add a test for the \"approved record names a file the hooks no longer name\" comparison (or remove that comparison if it protects nothing — say which and why). Cheap extra if simple: cache `projectHooksState` hashing by file stamp (dev, ino, size, mtimeNs) so each call does not re-hash up to 32 MiB; skip it if it adds risk, and say so.\n\nTests: one per refused form above (each of the four reviewer attacks end to end: approve is refused, or if the file was approved under the old code path the hooks are inactive), the `--require=./pre.js` pinned-and-overwritten case, and that ordinary forms still work (`[\"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"bash\",\"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"node\",\"scripts/guard.js\",\"--flag\"]`, `[\"/usr/bin/true\"]`, the shipped examples under examples/hooks — run `hooks_examples`). Mutation for each refusal. Then all hook test files, `leak_guard`, `security`, `managed_policy_enforcement`, `tsc --noEmit`, the complexity gate, `check_invariants`, `check_doc_versions`, the hooks schema `--check`. Report under 350 words: the final rule as implemented (file:line), tests and mutations, what is not covered, commit hash."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
