inst an older tip; re-verify every file:line you use), plus `/home/mike/nexus-wt/pf3/AGENTS.md`, `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` (PF-3 and gates G-3, G-6), `docs/PROTOCOL.md`, `docs/protocol/README.md`. Acceptance verbatim: \"a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600\". Scope decision from the plan: unix domain socket only, no TCP/HTTP/WebSocket listener.\n\nSecurity is the point of this unit; the plan's threat list is the checklist. In particular: socket and token live in a private directory created 0700 and lstat-checked (reuse the pattern of `core/client_lease.ts`), socket file 0600, token file 0600 and generated with crypto randomness; constant-time token comparison; a wrong or missing token is refused before any command is parsed and the refusal reveals nothing; connection and line size caps (reuse `BoundedLineReader`); only the controller may send commands that change state or answer approvals; observers are read-only; NO client may grant allow-always or switch to auto over the socket (today any stdin writer can: do not extend that to a socket; refuse `always` and auto for socket clients and say so in the protocol notes); managed policy and approval modes are unchanged; stale socket handling (never unlink a path that is not a socket owned by this uid; a live listener's socket is not replaced); peer uid check via SO_PEERCRED if Node exposes it (otherwise document that the 0700 directory is the boundary); cleanup on shutdown, with no leaked listeners (tests register servers with `trackResource`). Do not touch the five critical functions in `cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json` beyond not growing them. Keep `sidecar.ts` changes minimal: put the transport in new files under `cli/src/` and `cli/src/core/`, attach through the existing `write` callback seam.\n\nDeliver, in this order, each as its own local commit (do NOT push, no PR; I will review first): (1) the transport core with unit tests, (2) wiring into the sidecar (an opt-in flag such as `--listen` with the socket path printed on stdout in a machine-readable line, default off; stdio behaviour byte-identical when the flag is absent: prove with the existing `sidecar.test.ts` and protocol parity/conformance tests), protocol schema/samples/ledger updates (additive; follow `docs/protocol/README.md`; regenerate with `gen_protocol_schema.mjs` and `gen_protocol_samples.mjs`), (3) docs (`docs/PROTOCOL.md` section replacing the \"No socket\" statement, a runnable example under `examples/` run by a CI test like `cli/test/examples.test.ts` does, CHANGELOG Unreleased entry) and the hostile tests listed below. Hostile tests, each of which must fail when its guard is removed (verify by mutating a copy and restoring from it; never `git checkout` to undo): wrong token, empty token, token of different length, token sent after a first command, second controller refused while one is attached (and reattach after the first disconnects), observer sending a state-changing command, `always`/auto over the socket, oversized line, socket file mode, directory mode, a pre-existing symlink or regular file at the socket path, another uid's directory (simulate by mode/ownership checks you can do without root, or document), cleanup after shutdown, stdio mode unchanged.\n\nRules: new test files start with `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\";`. Run one test file at a time: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/pf3/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (throwaway HOME is mandatory; never read the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus). No network and no inference runs. Stop processes only by their own pid, never `pkill -f`. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops), output passes through `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` if it looks cut); a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; Rust binaries are in `core/target/release/`. Before each commit: `git fetch origin` and `git log origin/release/v1.3.0..HEAD` for foreign commits; merge origin/release/v1.3.0 in (never rebase). At the end run: `npx tsc --noEmit -p .` in `cli/`, the complexity gate, `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`, `leak_guard`, `sidecar`, `protocol_parity`, `protocol_schema`, `examples`, your new files three times each. The host is loaded; timing-sensitive existing tests may flake, rerun alone before concluding.\n\nReport under 450 words: files and commits (hashes), design decisions beyond the plan, tests with counts and the mutation that fails each, what the socket cannot protect against (same-uid processes, an unsandboxed model command that reads the token file: say so plainly), protocol/ledger changes, anything left open.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/af7235dbcad738773.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b2f8f20b-0238-4138-9b71-0039f9e600d1","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
