{"parentUuid":"2a44487e-2b6e-41cc-bfda-de88e8942f74","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"73d4db91-5979-4604-9d87-ffa0509a3208","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Wy3xCb6dn6kD99grEhRs5","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: a6ad467289d879869 (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'a6ad467289d879869', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a6ad467289d879869.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"7d82a946-ca23-4533-b471-e8fd36316a3f","timestamp":"2026-11-24T20:18:19.003Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"a6ad467289d879869","description":"Security review PR #797 PF-3","resolvedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","prompt":"Independent security review of GitHub PR #797 (PF-3: unix-socket transport for the Nexus sidecar) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 797 --json headRefOid` (expected 512d0f9d…), review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or merged. Read the PR body. Acceptance: \"a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600\". Files: `cli/src/session_socket.ts`, `cli/src/core/socket_files.ts`, the `--listen` wiring in `cli/src/sidecar.ts`, protocol changes (`protocol.ts`, `acp/mapping.ts`, schema, `VERSIONS.json`), docs/PROTOCOL.md \"Socket transport\", `examples/attach/`, tests `session_socket.test.ts`, `sidecar_socket.test.ts`.\n\nAttack it by RUNNING things, with a throwaway HOME and temp dirs. Checklist: (1) token: wrong, empty, different length, prefix of the real one, right token after a failed attempt, token reuse after the controller disconnects; timing-safe compare really used; does the refusal reveal anything (error text, timing, whether a session exists)? Can a client send commands before authenticating, or smuggle a command in the same line/packet as the attach? Is the token ever written to logs, flight records, telemetry, `session.state`, error messages, or the `listening` line? (2) filesystem: socket file mode, directory mode, descriptor file mode, creation race (umask, mode set after bind?), a pre-existing symlink/regular file/directory at the socket or dir path, a directory owned by another uid or with group/world bits, `/tmp` placement and predictable names, stale-socket sweep (can it remove a live socket, a non-socket, another uid's file, or follow a symlink?), cleanup on exit/SIGTERM/SIGINT/crash, libuv unlinking a replaced path. (3) roles: second controller refused; observer sending prompt/approve/interrupt/any command; controller reattach; a client that connects and never authenticates (idle timeout? connection cap? slow-loris), huge line, many connections, a slow observer (does it block the sidecar or the controller; unbounded buffering/memory?). (4) authority: over the socket NO client may grant allow-always, switch to auto, use slash commands, shutdown, select model/provider; try each, including `approve` with `always`, mode changes, case/whitespace/unicode variants of command names, duplicate keys, and numeric/string type confusion. Is `--listen` + `-y`/`--mode auto` refused at startup under all spellings and via NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE/config? Does managed policy still apply? (5) approvals: replay of a pending approval to a newly attached client: can an observer or a late attacher answer someone else's approval; can approval ids be guessed; two clients answering at once. (6) stdio unchanged when `--listen` is absent: run sidecar.test.ts and protocol parity/conformance. (7) the doc claim that `/run/user/<uid>` is hidden from sandboxed commands: verify against `cli/src/sandbox.ts` and say where the socket directory lives and whether a sandboxed (bwrap) model command can reach the socket or the token file in the default and `--sandbox` configurations, and with no sandbox. State the answer plainly: it decides whether an unsandboxed or sandboxed model command can attach as controller. (8) error codes and schema/ledger changes sane; `examples/attach` is safe to copy.\n\nThen run at head, one at a time: `session_socket`, `sidecar_socket`, `sidecar`, `protocol_parity`, `protocol_schema`, `examples`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy; never `git checkout`): remove the token check, the controller-only check, the `always` refusal, the 0600 chmod, the dir-mode check, the stale-sweep uid check: each must fail a test (a hang counts as caught; note it).\n\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev797 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev797/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev797/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev797/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev797/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never read or write the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. No network, no inference. Stop processes by their own pid, never `pkill -f`; leave no listeners behind. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. At the end remove probe files and `git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev797`. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` if filtered); a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\n\nReport under 500 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; findings with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (any route by which a non-operator client or a model command gains controller rights, allow-always, auto, or reads the token, is blocking); test counts; mutation results; what you did not check.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a6ad467289d879869.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2a44487e-2b6e-41cc-bfda-de88e8942f74","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
