{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T20:44:37.887Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a6ad467289d879869</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_014Wy3xCb6dn6kD99grEhRs5</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a6ad467289d879869.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Security review PR #797 PF-3\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES at 512d0f9d20774067d540a49e56679e5fc60ac40c.** One blocker (sandbox containment). The PR head has since moved to aa365cf3e, a one-line test change in `cli/test/protocol_doc.test.ts`. The PR is still OPEN. I did not review or run aa365cf3e.\n\n**Blocker (run)**\n- `cli/src/sandbox.ts:805` and docs/PROTOCOL.md:72. A model command can reach the socket and read the token in these cases:\n  - **No sandbox, which is the default.** `config.sandbox` is undefined unless `--sandbox` is passed. A command run this way read the descriptor and attached as controller. A controller can answer approvals and prompt, so an approved command could then approve the later approvals for its own turn.\n  - **`--sandbox` (bwrap) with the default directory.** The general branch masks `/run/user/&lt;uid&gt;` (a tmpfs, only if that path exists) and `/tmp` (a fresh tmpfs). With `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/&lt;uid&gt;` or unset (the `/tmp/nexus-sockets-&lt;uid&gt;` fallback), the sandboxed command saw no descriptor. The read-only host branch uses `--tmpfs /`, so it hides the directory too (read only, not run).\n  - **`--sandbox` with `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` anywhere else.** A path under `/var/tmp` or inside the workspace let the sandboxed command read the token and attach. The doc's claim is true only for the two standard locations.\n  - **Fix:** pass the real socket directory into the sandbox args as a `--tmpfs` mask, whatever it is. State the unsandboxed limit plainly in the doc.\n- In review mode the model cannot reach the descriptor on its own. `read_file` is jailed to the workspace, and `cat &lt;descriptor&gt;` through `run_command` needed approval and showed the path.\n\n**Suggestions (non-blocking)**\n- `session_socket.ts:64`: the controller may send `checkpoint.choice` with `allow_sensitive` and `answer` with `approve`. These are one-shot, like approve-once, but the PR text doesn't list them. Document them or drop them from the socket.\n- `sidecar.ts:1663` (read): `--listen` refuses auto only at startup. If the stdio parent later approves with `always` or runs `/mode auto`, a socket controller's prompts run ungated. Refuse socket prompts while the mode is auto.\n- `session_socket.ts:159`: eight idle unauthenticated connections block a legitimate attach for up to 5 s. Only same-uid processes can connect, so this is low.\n- A stale-sweep orphan `.json` stays when libuv has already unlinked the socket. It is harmless.\n\n**Verified clean (run unless marked read)**\n- **Token:** wrong, empty, short, long, prefix, uppercase, trailing newline, number, array, null and object tokens all got the identical `UNAUTHORIZED` refusal. A command first, a command in the same packet as the attach, and a wrong token followed by a prompt started no turns. A right token after failures worked. Timing was flat (about 0.5 ms for every case). `timingSafeEqual` is used (read). The token never appeared in stdout, stderr, stdio events, observer output, or files under HOME and the workspace.\n- **Modes:** socket 0600, directory 0700, descriptor 0600, including under umask 0. The sidecar refused to start for group-readable and symlinked directories, and for a pre-existing symlink, regular file or directory at the socket path. The `/tmp` fallback is read only. Sweep left a live socket, regular file, directory and symlink alone. SIGTERM, SIGINT and stdin close all cleaned up. After SIGKILL the next start swept the leftovers.\n- **Roles:** a second controller got BUSY. An observer got `NOT_CONTROLLER` for every command. Oversize input, drip-feed and idle connections were all closed. A stalled observer was dropped at 8 MiB with a 17 MB RSS delta, and the controller kept working.\n- **Authority:** over the socket, slash, shutdown, `model.*`, `provider.*` and approve-with-`always` all returned `NOT_PERMITTED`. Case, zero-width and fullwidth variants of command names got `BAD_REQUEST`, and duplicate keys resolved identically on both parses. The mode stayed `review`. `--listen` with `-y`, `--yes`, `--mode auto`, `--mode=auto`, ` AUTO `, `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` or a `config.env` setting exited 1 with nothing bound. Combined with `--read-only` or `--approve` it runs, in review or read-only. Managed-policy handling is unchanged (read).\n- **Approvals:** an observer's approve did nothing. Duplicate answers got `NO_PENDING_APPROVAL`. A reattached controller saw the same approval id again. Ids are sequential (`approval-N`) but only the controller can answer.\n- **Tests at the exact SHA, Node 24:**\n\n| Test | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `session_socket` | 28/28 |\n| `sidecar_socket` | 8/8 |\n| `sidecar` | 65/65 |\n| `protocol_parity` | 5/5 |\n| `protocol_schema` | 8/8 |\n| `examples` | 7/7 |\n| `leak_guard` | 6/6 |\n| `check_requirements` | 28/28 |\n\n  `tsc --noEmit`, the complexity gate and `check_invariants.mjs` also passed.\n\n**Mutations (restored from copies; the worktree matched the copies)**\n- Caught: token check (3 failures), controller-only check (2), `always` refusal (2), `chmod` (28; the post-bind 0600 assertion fails closed, so the whole suite fails), dir-mode check (1 in `session_socket`).\n- Survived: the uid check in `prepareSocketPath`, because a non-root user cannot create another uid's socket here (the PR admits this). Removing the redundant `isSymbolicLink` check also survived.\n- No hangs.\n\n**Not checked:** a real other-uid file, Node 22, the `examples/attach` code beyond its passing test, the Rust parity beyond `protocol_parity`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>116389</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>44</tool_uses><duration_ms>1579168</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
