{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"dbc178e7b903953ce96d9a7e73885769f8237e14ba4761166554726e2e54001f","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012YTVtshEb2qGKiZuUyrY8F","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/nexus-code && git fetch -q origin v1/pf3-control-socket && git log --format='%h %an %s' 512d0f9d..origin/v1/pf3-control-socket | cut -c1-120; cat > /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev797.md <<'EOF'\n**Independent security review at `512d0f9d20774067d540a49e56679e5fc60ac40c` — REQUEST CHANGES (one blocker: sandbox containment). I am fixing it on this branch. The head has since moved by a one-line test change that this review did not cover.**\n\n**Blocking (confirmed by running):** `cli/src/sandbox.ts:805` and `docs/PROTOCOL.md:72`. A model command can reach the socket and read the token in these cases, and attach as controller (which answers approvals and prompts, so an approved command could approve later approvals of its own turn):\n- no sandbox, which is the default: a command read the descriptor and attached;\n- `--sandbox` with `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` anywhere other than `/run/user/<uid>` or unset (a path under `/var/tmp`, or inside the workspace): the sandboxed command read the token. The sandbox masks `/run/user/<uid>` and `/tmp` only, so the document's claim holds for those two locations alone.\nWith `--sandbox` and the default location the command saw no descriptor; in review mode the model cannot reach the descriptor by itself (`read_file` is jailed to the workspace, `cat <descriptor>` through `run_command` needs approval and shows the path).\nFix: pass the real socket directory to the sandbox as a mask whatever it is; state the unsandboxed limit plainly in the document.\n\n**Suggestions:** `session_socket.ts:64` — the controller may send `checkpoint.choice` with `allow_sensitive` and `answer` with `approve` (one-shot, like approve-once) and the PR text does not list them: document or drop. `sidecar.ts:1663` — `--listen` refuses auto only at startup; if the stdio parent later approves with `always` or runs `/mode auto`, a socket controller's prompts run ungated: refuse socket prompts while the mode is auto. Eight idle unauthenticated connections block an attach for up to 5 s (same-uid only; low). A stale-sweep orphan `.json` can remain (harmless).\n\n**Held up (run):** wrong, empty, short, long, prefix, uppercase, newline, number, array, null and object tokens all got the same `UNAUTHORIZED`; a command first, or in the same packet as the attach, started nothing; timing flat; `timingSafeEqual`; the token appeared in no output or file. Socket 0600, directory 0700, descriptor 0600 even under umask 0; startup refused for group-readable or symlinked directories and a pre-existing symlink, file or directory at the socket path; the sweep left a live socket, a file, a directory and a symlink alone; cleanup on SIGTERM, SIGINT and stdin close, and the next start sweeps after SIGKILL. Second controller BUSY; observers `NOT_CONTROLLER` for every command; oversize, drip-feed and idle connections closed; a stalled observer dropped at 8 MiB without harming the controller. Slash, shutdown, `model.*`, `provider.*` and approve-with-`always` all `NOT_PERMITTED`; name variants `BAD_REQUEST`; `--listen` with every spelling of auto (flags, env, config) refused with nothing bound. An observer's approve did nothing; duplicates got `NO_PENDING_APPROVAL`.\n\n**Tests at that head, Node 24:** `session_socket` 28, `sidecar_socket` 8, `sidecar` 65, `protocol_parity` 5, `protocol_schema` 8, `examples` 7, `leak_guard` 6, `check_requirements` 28; `tsc`, the complexity gate, `check_invariants` pass. **Mutations:** token check, controller-only check, `always` refusal, `chmod`, directory-mode check all caught; the other-uid check and a redundant symlink check survive (cannot be tested without root).\n**Not checked:** a real other-uid file; Node 22.\nEOF\ngh pr comment 797 --body-file /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev797.md | tail -1","description":"See who moved PR 797's head and post the review"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KWbJxzWNYzaA5rezkkiYtA","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"af7235dbcad738773","summary":"Fix PF-3 review findings","message":"PF-3 review came back REQUEST CHANGES (PR #797, worktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/pf3, branch v1/pf3-control-socket; it is pushed, so start with `git fetch origin && git status && git log --oneline -5 origin/v1/pf3-control-socket`: someone pushed one commit changing cli/test/protocol_doc.test.ts after your last push; merge it, never rebase; this time you may push at the end with a plain `git push origin v1/pf3-control-socket`, never force). Same rules as before. Fix:\n1. BLOCKER (confirmed by running): cli/src/sandbox.ts:~805 masks only `/run/user/<uid>` and `/tmp`; with --sandbox and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR elsewhere (e.g. /var/tmp or inside the workspace) a sandboxed command can read the token descriptor and attach as controller. Fix: the actual socket directory (wherever it resolves, realpath) is added to the sandbox as a `--tmpfs` mask (or --ro-bind /dev/null style hide) in every bwrap branch (read-only branch and general branch), and also refuse starting `--listen` when the socket directory lies inside the workspace (the workspace is writable/readable by the model). Add tests: with the sandbox args builder, a socket directory under /var/tmp and under a home path appears as a mask; real bwrap run if bwrap is available in the existing sandbox tests (follow how they are skipped otherwise): a sandboxed command cannot see the descriptor; mutation: remove the mask → test fails. Docs/PROTOCOL.md: replace the claim at ~:72 with what is true: masked under --sandbox for any location; NOT hidden when there is no sandbox (the default) — an unsandboxed command run in auto or approved in review mode acts with the operator's rights and can read the descriptor and attach; the docs must say so plainly and recommend --sandbox for untrusted work.\n2. `sidecar.ts` ~1663: `--listen` refuses auto only at startup. Refuse socket `prompt` (and checkpoint/answer commands) with NOT_PERMITTED while the session mode is `auto` or alwaysApprove is set (stdio parent may switch later), and document. Test + mutation.\n3. session_socket.ts:~64: the controller may send `checkpoint.choice` with allow_sensitive and `answer` with `approve`: decide: keep only what is strictly needed for a controller to drive a turn; if `allow_sensitive` is not needed over the socket, drop it (NOT_PERMITTED). Document the final list of controller commands in PROTOCOL.md.\n4. Idle unauthenticated connection cap: lower the pre-auth idle timeout (e.g. 2 s) and cap pre-auth connections at 4 so idle sockets cannot starve a real attach for 5 s; test.\nThen re-run: tsc, complexity gate, check_invariants, check_doc_versions, session_socket, sidecar_socket, sidecar, protocol_parity, protocol_schema, protocol_doc, examples, leak_guard, the sandbox test files you touched (`ls cli/test | grep -i sandbox`), each new/changed file three times; commit as `fix(pf3): <what>` + `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`; push. Report under 300 words: what changed (file:line), tests and mutations, commit hash, anything open."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
