{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T21:08:47.765Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a497a5c16f766f926</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01KH8auHoAoyAkpRwLuCW2Va</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a497a5c16f766f926.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Implement BG-2 schedules\" is waiting on its own background work</summary>\n<note>This agent stopped with background work of its own still running. It may resume on its own when that work completes or reports, and the same task-id notifies again if it does; the result below may be interim.</note>\n<result>BG-2 is done in `/home/mike/nexus-wt/bg2` on `v1/bg2-schedules`, as three local commits (not pushed, no PR). Every new test file passed three runs in a row, and the BG-1 files, `fault_kill_mid_tool_call`, `examples`, `protocol_schema`, `leak_guard`, `run_json_contract` and `security` passed once. `tsc`, the complexity gate (the five baseline functions did not grow), `check_invariants` and `check_doc_versions` are clean. No runner or worker processes are left behind.\n\n**Commits**\n- `dc6792984`: schedule store, cron/every/once parser and due-time computation, plus the protected path.\n- `54253348e`: runner integration, `nexus schedule add|list|status|remove|pause|resume|run-now` (`-j/--json`), and the shared `core/job_submission.ts`. `index.ts` gets one import and one register line.\n- `9f4cf34ea`: acceptance, hostile and G-4 tests, schedule schema and ledger, `docs/SCHEDULES.md`, `examples/schedules`, manual, CHANGELOG and STATE_COMPATIBILITY.\n\n**Policies**\n- **Time:** UTC only, so no daylight-saving shift; a leap day is real; no leap seconds. `every` keeps its phase from creation.\n- **Missed runs:** all are counted and reported as one gap, never replayed. The default is one catch-up job (`run-once`); `--on-missed report` runs none. The newest slot counts as on time if it is within 30 seconds.\n- **Overlap:** a slot due while the schedule's previous job runs starts nothing and is recorded as an `overlap` gap.\n- **Clock stepped back:** if the clock is more than 2 minutes behind where the runner last saw it, the step is counted and the next slot recomputed. A slot that already has a job is never started again.\n- **Caps:** 64 schedules, the last 50 gaps and 20 missed receipts kept, 200,000 slots per scan, and the runner's existing queue of 32.\n- **Trust:** `add` and every fire need a workspace in the operator's trust list.\n\n**Tests (pass counts)**\n| File | Tests |\n|---|---|\n| `schedule` | 14 |\n| `schedule_store` | 10 |\n| `schedule_schema` | 2 |\n| `schedule_engine` | 13 |\n| `schedule_hostile` | 7 |\n| `schedule_cli` | 6 |\n| `schedule_failing_verifier`, `schedule_approval_denied`, `schedule_missed`, `schedule_examples` | 1 each |\n| `schedule_unattended` | 2 |\n\n**Mutations.** Each of 18 mutations made its tests fail: protected path removed, unknown keys allowed, policy enum unchecked, file mode, symlink and owner checks, directory check, schedule cap, trust list, overlap check, burst advance, clock-step handling, slot dedupe, cron range and field count, workspace link check, missed info dropped from the receipt, and `UnattendedApprovalPolicy` letting exec and egress through. The last one is the allow-always guard. The mutation runner is `/dev/shm/bg2mut/run.py`; sources were restored from copies.\n\n**What a schedule cannot protect against.** Another program running as your user, including an agent's `run_command` with full access, can still write a record into the schedule directory. The file tools refuse it, but shell commands are only advisory-checked. A planted record is still bounded: it can only name one of the two job levels, the workspace must be in your trust list at each run, and the unattended rules still apply. The host clock is trusted; a wrong clock is reported, not fixed.\n\n**Schema and ledger**\n- The receipt's `missed` (reserved as always null) is now null or `{count, first, last, truncated?}`, and there is a new optional `schedule_occurrence`.\n- I appended entries to the job-receipt and job-status ledgers and kept both at version 1, because nothing shipped carried the old shape.\n- The new `schedule-v1.schema.json` and `SCHEDULE_VERSIONS.json` cover the schedule record, its `latest` pointer and the missed-run receipt.\n\n**BG-1 observations (not fixed here)**\n1. An unattended job's `--verifier` goes through the approval policy for exec, and command rules do not apply to it. A verifier like `node --test` is never run, so `task_completed` always fails. The job ends BLOCKED through stagnation with reason `EXECUTION_FAILED`, and the receipt never names the verifier, so the \"seeded failing verifier\" case is BLOCKED but not for the verifier's own failure. `git status --short` does run.\n2. As far as I could see, the file-tool protected paths do not cover `runner/inbox` or `runner/jobs`; I only protected the schedule files.\n\n**Open**\n- Tool `ExecutionReceipt` has no `unattended` stamp (plan BG-2.3). G-4's flag is met by the job receipt and the operation origin, which carries `scheduleId`.\n- No `doctor` `schedules.*` findings.\n- No `maxRounds` option.\n- `docs/releases/v1.3.0-READINESS.md` was not updated.\n- The example test is `schedule_examples.test.ts`, as BG-1 did with `runner_examples.test.ts`, not `examples.test.ts`. It needs a fresh `cli/dist` (`npm --prefix cli run build`).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>390044</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>132</tool_uses><duration_ms>2993830</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
