{"parentUuid":"faed0a04-cb64-44c8-81b2-456510f58778","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNXMafWHureg3psftcD1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GdSmk9FxZ9Jsw6pJ6iRqsm","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Re-review PR #799 BG-2 fixes","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent RE-review of GitHub PR #799 (BG-2 unattended schedules) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 799 --json headRefOid` (expected to start `c5182bbc`), review exactly that, and say whether it moved or merged. The first review is the PR comment \"Independent review at `68bc626f…` — REQUEST CHANGES\" (`gh pr view 799 --comments`): read it. The fix commit `c5182bbc7` (`git show --stat`, then diff; 21 files) answers it. The core change is security-relevant: an UNATTENDED job may now start the operator's `--verifier` command without approval (`cli/src/core/completion_oracle.ts` `isUnattendedOperatorVerifier`, called from `ensureVerifierApprovedImpl`; `job_submission.ts` `verifierProblem`; `job_receipt.ts` verifier `status`/`detail`; `spine_handlers.ts` denial text; `security.ts` `runnerRoot()` protected; `schedule.ts` cron floor and `MAX_SLOT_SCAN`; `schedule_cli.ts` `run-now` lock; docs). Try to break it by RUNNING things (throwaway HOME, temp dirs, no network, no live inference; scripted gateway from `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts`):\n\n1. Authority of the new allow: can anything other than the operator's own `--verifier` string run without approval in an unattended job? Try: a model-declared verifier or requirement verifier whose command is identical modulo whitespace/quotes/case/trailing `;`, `&&` chains, a command that merely starts with the verifier string (`node --test; curl x`), unicode lookalikes, env prefixes (`FOO=1 node --test`), a verifier that is a path inside the workspace the model can overwrite (`./verify.sh`: the model edits it, the operator verifier then runs the model's script unattended with the sandbox but no approval: is that acceptable? Compare with the interactive path and with what the sandbox confines: say plainly what a hostile repo/model can do through an operator verifier like `npm test`, and whether the docs say it), the verifier string tampered in a schedule record on disk (is it re-validated at every fire, bounded, no newline/NUL, tripwire and `command_rules.json` applied: try a command that `command_rules.json` forbids; try a record with an enormous verifier; try after changing the rules file), a job submitted by `nexus job` (not a schedule) with `--verifier`, and managed policy `require_human` for commands (must still deny). Is the verifier run inside the sandbox in a read-write job and in a read-only job (the builder says a read-only run does not run its verifier at all: confirm and confirm the receipt says so)? Does the allow apply on any surface other than `unattended` (try the interactive, headless `run`, ACP, sidecar surfaces: must be unchanged)?\n2. Acceptance: seeded FAILING verifier → BLOCKED, reason `VERIFIER_FAILED`, receipt names the verifier and says it failed; seeded PASSING verifier → COMPLETE/VERIFIED; no verifier → receipt says so; unattended approval still denied; missed runs still reported (`schedule_missed`, with `ce242d020`'s fix); kill-and-resume pass.\n3. Receipt/schema: `status`/`detail` additions follow the ledger rules (version stays 1 with entries); `protocol_schema`, `run_json_contract`, schema generators `--check` pass; old receipts still parse.\n4. The rest of the first review's non-blocking items: cron floor (`* * * * *`, `*/2 * * * *`, `0,3 * * * *` rejected; `*/5` accepted; hourly accepted), `MAX_SLOT_SCAN` 20,000 (does a legitimate yearly or leap-day schedule still compute its next slot; is a once far in the future ok?), `run-now` race (two concurrent `run-now` produce one job), `runnerRoot()` protection (agent `write_file`/`edit_file`/`delete_file` against runner inbox/jobs/schedules by absolute path, relative from a workspace that is HOME, symlink; and the runner itself can still write its own files).\n5. History hygiene: the builder says the branch was rebuilt from a patch on the remote tip; confirm the PR contains no dropped commits of the other author (`git log origin/release/v1.3.0..<head>`), and no leftover duplicate schedule code.\n\nThen run at head, one at a time: every `cli/test/schedule*.test.ts`, `unattended_verifier`, the BG-1 files (`ls cli/test | grep -iE \"runner|job\"`), `fault_kill_mid_tool_call`, tests mentioning completion_oracle (`grep -l completion_oracle cli/test/*.ts`), `approval_policy`, `security`, `protocol_schema`, `run_json_contract`, `examples`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; complexity gate; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`. Example tests need a fresh `cli/dist`: run `npm --prefix cli run build` inside the review worktree first. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): the surface check, the exact-match check, `require_human`, the verifier validation at fire time, the runner-root protection, the cron floor: each must fail a test.\n\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev799b <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev799b/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev799b/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev799b/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev799b/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never read/write the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. Stop processes by their own pid, never `pkill -f`; leave no runner or worker processes. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. Remove probes and the worktree at the end. Shell is zsh