{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"a12516acb822010f380f694fcf4bf6150ce22308c8fb5a3cf0b01e69bf90608f","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RHUhvUYEonVEQJfzxwVorY","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement MT-2 slice 2 registry","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Implement MT-2 slice 2 (the tool registry) in the worktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/mt2r (branch `v1/mt2-registry`, based on `origin/release/v1.3.0`; Nexus Code, TypeScript CLI in `cli/`). Read first, fully: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md` section \"MT-2: tool registry and router\" (design, security analysis, files, tests, slices; written against an older tip, so re-verify every file:line), `/home/mike/nexus-wt/mt2r/AGENTS.md`, `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` MT-2 row and gates G-3/G-6, and the slice-1 characterization from PR #784 already on the release branch: `cli/test/tool_surface_characterization.test.ts`, `tool_dispatch_characterization.test.ts`, `mt1_core_import.test.ts`, fixtures `cli/test/fixtures/model_tools_v1.json` and `tool_bindings_v1.json`. Acceptance: \"focused dispatch and G-3 suites green; adding a tool is a registered spec+handler pair; no new grant path\".\n\nTHE RULE FOR THIS SLICE: the slice-1 tests and fixtures must pass UNEDITED (no fixture regeneration, no test edits; if a characterization test fails after your change, your change is wrong unless it exposes a flaw in the test: then stop and report instead of editing). This slice is behaviour-preserving.\n\nDo only the registry (plan section \"Design\", the `ToolRegistry`/`RegisteredTool`/`ToolBinding` part and the one-execution-path part), NOT the dispatch decomposition and NOT the baseline move (those are slice 4: `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runTurnImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl`, `driveOperation` must not grow in complexity or nesting: `cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json` and `node` complexity gate; do NOT edit the baseline). Concretely: (1) new `cli/src/core/tool_registry.ts` with `register` that fails closed (duplicate name; a spec that already carries a `handler` AND a binding; an extension capability whose effect is not exec|egress, per `core/extension_capabilities.ts`; a non-extension spec without a binding), `resolve`, `modelTools(ctx)` reproducing EXACTLY today's `AutonomousAgent.effectiveTools` order and filters (agent.ts ~1335-1365), and a `capabilities()` adapter so existing `getByToolName` callers keep working; the registry has NO API that lowers an effect, marks a tool approval-free, or overrides `requiresDecision` (add a test that asserts the absence of such an API surface by inspecting the exported members, and a test that an extension cannot register `read_workspace` or `internal`). (2) Make the handler resolution table (plan \"Handlers come from five places\") go through `registry.resolve(name).binding` in `ExecutionSpine.execute` (`core/spine.ts` ~562-584) while keeping the existing fallbacks until `tool_dispatch_characterization` proves equivalence; remove a fallback only where the characterization table proves nothing relies on it, and say which in the report. (3) Derive `HARNESS_TOOLS`, parser `KNOWN_TOOLS`, the serve-mcp list and the ACP kind table from the registry's baseline set so they are identical to today (the slice-1 derived-list tests pin them). (4) `docs/TOOL_REGISTRY.md`: how to add a tool as a registered spec+handler pair; a unit test that adding a tool through the registry makes it appear in the model-visible list, the dispatch route and the derived lists with no other edit (use a test-only registry instance, do not change the shipped tool set).\n\nKeep `agent.ts` edits minimal (it is a hot file others edit: `git fetch origin` and merge origin/release/v1.3.0 often, never rebase). If the registry cannot be introduced without growing a critical function, stop and report which and by how much.\n\nVerify at HEAD and at BASE (`git stash`-free: use a second `git worktree add --detach /home/mike/nexus-wt/mt2r-base origin/release/v1.3.0` with `cp -a`/symlinked node_modules and the Rust binaries, and remove it at the end): the slice-1 files, `capabilities`, `lsp_shape`, `tool_groups`, `role_deny_lists`, `acp_mapping`, `tool_grammar*`, `command_rules_dispatch`, `managed_policy_dispatch`, `vllm_nullable_dispatch`, `approval_policy`, `injection_gate`, `security_gates`, `sandbox_denial_events`, `agt111_subagent_parity`, `mcp_tool_through_spine`, `hooks_*` (HK-1 attaches in the spine), `sidecar`, `acp_stdio`, `protocol_parity`, `critical_complexity`: failing sets must be identical or better than base (the host is loaded; rerun timing failures alone before concluding). `context_convergence` is very slow: run it once in the background with `timeout 900` and report. Then `npx tsc --noEmit -p .` in `cli/`, the complexity gate (must pass with the baseline unedited), `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): registry accepts a duplicate; accepts an extension with effect read_workspace; accepts a spec with no binding; `modelTools` order swapped; a binding resolved to the wrong kind: each must fail a test.\n\nRules: new test files start with `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\";`. One test file at a time: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/mt2r/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (throwaway HOME mandatory; never read the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus; no network; no live inference). Stop processes only by their own pid, never `pkill -f`. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` if cut); a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; Rust binaries are in `core/target/release/`. Commit locally in 2-3 coherent commits (`feat(tools): ...`, `docs(tools): ...`, each ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`), check `git log origin/release/v1.3.0..HEAD` for foreign commits first; do NOT push, no PR.\n\nReport under 450 words: commits (hashes), what resolves through the registry now and which fallbacks remain (and why), the head-vs-base table of failing sets, complexity gate output, tests with counts and the mutation that fails each, anything the characterization flagged as a defect that the registry made worse or better (do not fix), and what slice 3/4 still need."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MruFyB4ikcgiWrDrKoFA7e","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Hostile re-check of merged HK-1 hooks","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent hostile check of the hooks feature (HK-1) as it stands on `origin/release/v1.3.0` in ~/nexus-code (fetch first; use the tip commit). PR #782 merged on 2026-11-24 18:03Z after three of my reviews and a fourth round by another author; I have no independent review of the FINAL merged code. Read `docs/HOOKS.md`, `cli/src/core/hooks.ts`, `hook_runner.ts`, `content_trust.ts`, `strict_json.ts`, `process_identity.ts`, `cli/src/hooks_cli.ts`, `doctor_hooks.ts`, the hooks block in `cli/src/agent.ts`, `cli/src/security.ts` protected paths, and the PR comments on #782 (`gh pr view 782 --comments`) to learn the four earlier blocker classes (agent self-approval of project hooks; workspace=HOME writing an active operator hooks file; approval not pinning scripts a hook names; nested launchers like `nice bash -c` / unpinned loader flags) and what the last rounds changed (queue `hookStillCurrent` re-resolve; WRAPPERS/INTERPRETERS applied at every argv index). Attack the final code by RUNNING things (throwaway HOME, temp workspaces, no network, no live inference):\n1. Self-approval/self-installation through every agent tool route: file tools (write/edit/patch/delete/rename/move variants, `..`, symlinked parents, case variants, workspace = repo / HOME / `~/.config` / `~/.config/nexus`), `run_command` in review/auto/read-only modes (`nexus hooks approve` with no TTY, with `script`-faked TTY under `--sandbox` (is the store reachable under bwrap?), shell redirects into the store), the MCP and plugin tool paths, and `nexus trust`-adjacent files. State plainly what an unsandboxed command can do (documented limit) vs a sandboxed one.\n2. Pinning: every form of project hook command that names a workspace file or injects code: arguments, `--opt=value`, `-oVALUE`, `-c/-e/--eval/-p/-m`, loaders (`--require`, `--import`, `--loader`), `env`, `busybox`, `xargs`, `find -exec`, `awk`/`perl -e`/`ruby -e`/`php -r`/`lua -e`/`tclsh`/`sqlite3`, `make -f`, shell scripts sourcing others, `bash -c` behind `nice`/`timeout`/`nohup`/`stdbuf`/`ionice`/`time`/`chrt`/`taskset`/`setsid`/`flock`/`script`/`unshare`/`sudo`/`doas`/`su`/`runuser`/`systemd-run` (unknown prefixes!), `sh -- -c`, combined short flags (`-ec`), `--command=...`, symlink swaps, directory args, files created after approval, TOCTOU between the hash check and the spawn (how wide, can a model command win it?). Which refusal list gaps remain: the list is a blocklist; judge whether a generic (allowlist: only a pinned file as argv[0] plus pinned file args, never an interpreter with inline code) rule would be safer and what it would break. Operator hooks are trusted: confirm they get none of this.\n3. Gate ordering and fail-closed behaviour (`hooks_gate_bypass` claims): a hook answering approve in every dialect cannot admit a call a gate refused; a hook error/timeout/output flood/invalid JSON/BOM/duplicate keys/exit codes denies; hook processes cannot hold the turn forever (timeout enforced; cancel behaviour documented); environment scrubbed (no gateway key: check with a hook that dumps its env), redaction in post-tool payloads (secrets in tool output must not reach the hook), process-tree kill by pid with start-time checks, no `pkill`.\n4. Denial text framing: a hook's denial reason with prompt-injection text, newlines, 5k chars: arrives JSON-quoted and capped.\n5. Doctor/CLI: `nexus hooks list|test|approve|revoke` behave; an invalid operator hooks file denies every call with guidance; `serve-mcp` runs no hooks (documented).\nRun, one at a time, at the tip: every `cli/test/hook*.test.ts` and `hooks_*.test.ts`, `content_trust`, `strict_json`, `doctor_hooks`, `security`, `approval_policy`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): skip the pinned-file comparison; drop the wrapper check at non-zero argv index; drop the queue re-resolve; accept an operator file inside the workspace; drop a protected path: each must fail a test.\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev-hk1final origin/release/v1.3.0`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev-hk1final/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev-hk1final/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev-hk1final/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev-hk1final/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never touch the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`. No commits, pushes, or GitHub comments. Remove probes and the worktree at the end. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); prefix `rtk proxy` if output looks filtered; a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\nReport under 500 words: verdict on the merged code (SOUND / SOUND WITH FOLLOW-UPS / DEFECTS FOUND), each finding with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (a way for the agent's own sandboxed tools, or a hostile repository, to get a hook in force or to run unpinned code with the operator's rights is blocking; the documented unsandboxed-shell limit is not), the exact attack for each blocking one, test counts, mutation results, what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
