{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:20:38.006Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>af3a026529107ec07</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01MruFyB4ikcgiWrDrKoFA7e</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/af3a026529107ec07.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Hostile re-check of merged HK-1 hooks\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: DEFECTS FOUND.** The approval store and the operator hooks file hold up against every sandboxed route I tried. Project-hook pinning does not hold. I tested tip `d9b0f5a63` (`origin/release/v1.3.0`). I removed the worktree and all probes.\n\n## Blockers\n\nAll three were run, not just read.\n\n1. **Pinning is a blocklist, and unlisted launchers and interpreters run unpinned code.** The check is `coveredProjectFiles`, `cli/src/core/hooks.ts:342-449`. Only argv elements that are themselves workspace paths are pinned. Any program that takes a code string, or a name that it resolves itself, is not caught.\n   - Attack: the repository ships `[\"script\",\"-qec\",\"sh g.sh\",\"/dev/null\"]`, `[\"awk\",\"BEGIN{system(\\\"sh g.sh\\\")}\"]` or `[\"node\",\"--run\",\"x\"]`. The operator approves, and `nexus hooks approve` lists no covered files. The agent then edits `g.sh` or the `package.json` script.\n   - Result: the next tool call ran the edited code with no notice and no re-approval, and a marker file appeared outside the workspace.\n   - `npm run x` behaved the same way.\n   - Accepted but not executed: `ash`, `csh`, `tcsh`, `mksh`, `rbash`, `lua5.4`, `Rscript` with `-c`/`-e`, and `su -c`, `runuser -c`. They are missing from `INTERPRETERS`/`WRAPPERS` (lines 305 and 310).\n   - Held up: `setsid`, `flock`, `sudo`, `unshare` and `xargs` before `bash -c` (all refused), plus `sh -- -c`, `-ec`, `--command=`, and `nice`/`timeout`/`env`/`busybox` before `bash -c`. The index-N rule closed the nested-launcher class whenever a listed interpreter follows.\n   - docs/HOOKS.md names `awk`, `make` and `sqlite3` as unrecognised. It does not name `script`, `su`, `node --run` or `npm run`.\n   - Fix: switch to an allowlist. Accept argv[0] only as a pinned workspace file, or a known shell/interpreter whose first non-option argument is a pinned file, with options limited to a short list such as `-x`, `-u`, `--posix`. Refuse everything else in project files.\n   - What it breaks: project hooks that call `awk`, `make`, `npm`, `git` or `jq`. Those move to the operator file.\n\n2. **TOCTOU between the hash check and the spawn is winnable by a sandboxed process.** The code is `hookStillCurrent` plus `runHookProcess` (`hooks.ts:645-683`, `hook_runner.ts:102`).\n   - Attack: a background `run_command` loops `copy; rename` over the pinned `g.sh`, alternating benign and evil content. This needs only workspace writes, which the sandbox allows.\n   - Result: against `[\"sh\",\"g.sh\"]`, 150 calls gave 80 hook runs and 27 executions of the evil copy, about 34% per run. The other 70 calls dropped the hook because the file looked changed. Each call is a free retry.\n   - The docs call this limit \"not detected\", but it is exploitable from the sandbox, which makes it blocking under your rule.\n   - Fix: execute from the hashed bytes. Read the file once, check the hash, and exec it via `/proc/self/fd/N` or a private 0500 copy. For interpreter scripts, pass the verified copy as the script argument.\n\n3. **Sibling files a pinned script sources or imports are not pinned.** This is documented. `sh g.sh` with `. ./lib.sh`: editing `lib.sh` after approval ran with no notice. It has the same fix as #1, plus a documented rule that project hooks must be one self-contained file.\n\n## Non-blocking\n\n- `security.ts:164-176`: when the workspace is `~/.config/nexus`, `hooks.json` is not a protected path. The patterns match segments relative to the workspace root, and the absolute-pattern pass only covers absolute patterns. `write_file hooks.json` succeeded in a bridge probe that bypasses the hooks stage. The file then becomes an operator hooks file the next time Nexus starts elsewhere. Fix: add absolute `~/.config/nexus/hooks.json` and `extension_trust.json` to the protected list.\n- `delete_file` with `recursive:true` on `.config/nexus` or `.config` succeeded when the workspace was `~` or `~/.config` (bridge-level). The real agent denies every call in those workspaces because the in-workspace hooks file is invalid. Fix: have `forbiddenDeletion` (`tools.ts:59`) refuse a directory that contains a protected path.\n- `hook_runner.ts:197-200`: on `output_left_open` the runner denies but does not kill the process group. My `(sleep 20) &amp;` hook's child survived, orphaned at ppid 1. Fix: call `signalAll(\"SIGKILL\")` before `finish`.\n- A BOM before `{\"decision\":\"allow\"}` is trimmed and treated as an ignored allow. It admits nothing a gate refused.\n- Docs: add `script`, `su`, `node --run` and `npm run` to the \"not recognised\" list.\n\n## Confirmed sound\n\n- **Approval store, sandboxed:** with `--sandbox`, a shell cannot reach `extension_trust.json` or `hooks.json`. This held with the workspace set to the repo, `~` and `~/.config/nexus`, because the sandbox masks `~/.config`; the outside store stayed unchanged.\n- **Unsandboxed limit (documented):** `nexus hooks approve` with no TTY, or with `y` piped in, refuses. `script -qe` supplying the TTY approves, even with stdin piped. A direct write to the store works too, as documented.\n- **File tools:** write, edit, patch and delete across `..`, `//`, `./`, case variants, absolute paths and symlinked parents all hold, with the workspace at the repo, `~` and `~/.config`.\n- **Operator file:** inside the workspace, group-writable and symlinked files each deny every call with `HOOKS_CONFIG_INVALID`.\n- **Fail-closed behaviour:** each of these denies:\n  - exit 1\n  - a timeout (1.08 s for a 1 s limit)\n  - 64 KiB of stdout and 8 KiB of stderr\n  - bad JSON and duplicate keys, in either order\n  - an allow with `updatedInput` (ignored, admits nothing extra)\n- **Denial text:** an injection reason with newlines and 5k characters arrived as a JSON-quoted reason inside the framing, capped at 1149 characters.\n- **Environment:** the hook's environment contains no provider or gateway key, no `BASH_ENV`, `LD_PRELOAD` or `NODE_OPTIONS`.\n\n## Tests and mutations\n\n- 18 files run one at a time: 181 tests, 181 pass.\n- `tsc --noEmit` is clean.\n- All six mutations were killed:\n  - skipped pinned-file comparison: `hooks_project_trust`\n  - wrapper check only at argv[0]: `hooks_self_approval`\n  - dropped queue re-resolve: `hooks_project_trust`\n  - operator file accepted inside the workspace: `hooks_self_approval`\n  - dropped `extension_trust.json` protection: `hooks_self_approval`\n  - dropped `nexus/hooks.json` protection: `hooks_self_approval`\n\nGaps in the test suite: nothing covers `script`, `su`, `node --run` or `npm run`, and nothing exercises the TOCTOU.\n\n## Not checked\n\n- `run_command` in read-only mode and the MCP and plugin tool routes (read only).\n- Post-tool redaction beyond reading `hooks_post_tool.test.ts`.\n- Relative or empty PATH entries.\n- Hook processes holding a turn forever when the turn is cancelled.\n- Hooks in `serve-mcp` (read only).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>143080</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>46</tool_uses><duration_ms>509965</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
