{"parentUuid":"c7c81cc9-7033-48df-aabc-1b7985c3d5e7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"277fd3ce-a68e-4687-ae3a-40eea07eff69","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qr8UxnkmqE5g9MZ439KS7S","type":"tool_result","content":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/782#issuecomment-7147300113\nready","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ba6151a6-f96a-4006-88d5-d81048071c03","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:21:04.922Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/782#issuecomment-7147300113\nready","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"gitOperation":{"pr":{"number":782,"url":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/782","action":"commented"}}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c7c81cc9-7033-48df-aabc-1b7985c3d5e7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"ba6151a6-f96a-4006-88d5-d81048071c03","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNcNicMD3p7HtEZoJWLn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JXhnQiUb9pjiz6ZVvdKJFe","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"a317b41792fa50650","summary":"Fix HK-1 pinning: allowlist plus verified-bytes exec","message":"New HK-1 work in a NEW worktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/hk1b (branch `v1/hk1-pinning-allowlist`, created from origin/release/v1.3.0 tip d9b0f5a6; your old worktree ~/nexus-wt/hk1 is stale: do not use it). A hostile check of the merged HK-1 found defects; read the findings: `gh pr view 782 --comments` (last comment, \"Independent hostile check of the merged HK-1 code\"). Fix, on that branch, local commits, then push with a plain `git push origin v1/hk1-pinning-allowlist` (never force) and report; I open the PR. Same rules as before (throwaway HOME, `TMPDIR=/dev/shm`, leak guard, kill by pid, mutations from a copy and restored from it, never `git checkout`, complexity gate untouched, `git fetch origin` + merge origin/release/v1.3.0 often, never rebase). Files are claimed by me (HK1_PINNING_ALLOWLIST).\n\n1. BLOCKER: replace the blocklist (`coveredProjectFiles`, cli/src/core/hooks.ts ~342-449, INTERPRETERS/WRAPPERS ~305-310) by an ALLOWLIST for PROJECT hooks. A project hook command is accepted only if (a) argv[0] is itself an existing regular workspace file (pinned), run directly; or (b) argv[0] is a short fixed list of plain shells/interpreters (sh, bash, dash, zsh, node, python3 and versioned names, perl, ruby: decide a minimal list; `env`, wrappers, `script`, `su`, `awk`, `make`, `npm`, `npx`, `git`, `jq` etc. are NOT on it) AND its first non-option argument is a pinned regular workspace file AND every option before it is on a short per-interpreter list of harmless flags (e.g. `-e`/`-u`/`-x`/`--posix`/`--no-warnings`; NO inline-code flags, NO loader flags, NO `--run`, NO `--require/--import`), AND later arguments that name existing workspace paths are pinned or refused as before. Everything else is refused for project hooks with a message naming the rule and telling the operator to put that hook in their own operator hooks file. Operator hooks are unaffected. Tests: every attack in the finding must be refused at approve time and listed as invalid (`[\"script\",\"-qec\",\"sh g.sh\",\"/dev/null\"]`, `[\"awk\",\"BEGIN{system(\\\"sh g.sh\\\")}\"]`, `[\"node\",\"--run\",\"x\"]`, `[\"npm\",\"run\",\"x\"]`, `ash`, `csh`, `tcsh`, `mksh`, `rbash`, `lua5.4 -e`, `Rscript -e`, `su -c`, `runuser -c`, `env`, `busybox`, `xargs`, `nice bash -c`, `timeout 5 bash -c`, `sh -- -c`, combined `-ec`, `--command=`), and ordinary forms still work (`[\"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"bash\",\"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"node\",\"scripts/guard.js\",\"--flag\"]`, `[\"/usr/bin/true\"]` as an absolute path outside the workspace: decide: allow only if the operator pinned? say what you chose; the shipped examples/hooks must still pass hooks_examples). Mutation per rule.\n2. BLOCKER: TOCTOU between the hash check and the spawn is winnable from the sandbox (`hookStillCurrent` + `runHookProcess`, hooks.ts ~645-683, hook_runner.ts ~102: a background loop doing copy+rename over the pinned script won 27 of 80 hook runs). Fix: execute the verified bytes: read each pinned file ONCE, check the hash on those bytes, write them to a private per-run directory (mode 0700, files 0500, outside the workspace, removed after the run) and spawn with the copies substituted for the pinned paths (argv[0] file or the interpreter's script argument), so a later change of the workspace file cannot affect the running hook. Document that scripts referencing their own directory break and must be self-contained. For (a) direct execution use the verified copy as argv[0] (or `fexecve`-style via /proc/self/fd if you can do it simply and safely; the private copy is fine). Test: a test that swaps the workspace file between hash and spawn (inject a hook point/seam) and proves the ORIGINAL bytes run; and a stress test (bounded, ~20 iterations, no flakiness) with a concurrent swapper that must never execute the swapped bytes. Mutation: spawn the workspace path again → tests fail.\n3. Siblings (a pinned script sourcing `./lib.sh`) are not pinned: state in docs/HOOKS.md that project hooks must be one self-contained file and that sourced/imported files are NOT covered; consider a cheap defence: run interpreters with the private copy dir as the working directory? NO (cwd must stay the workspace as today): just document.\n4. Non-blocking, do them: (a) security.ts protected paths: add absolute `~/.config/nexus/hooks.json` and `extension_trust.json` (resolved from the real config dir via the same function the hooks loader uses) so a workspace of `~/.config/nexus` is covered, with a test; (b) `forbiddenDeletion` (tools.ts ~59): refuse `delete_file recursive` of a directory that CONTAINS a protected path, with a test; (c) hook_runner.ts ~197-200: on `output_left_open` kill the process group (signalAll SIGKILL) before finishing, test with `(sleep 20) &` child; (d) docs/HOOKS.md: list `script`, `su`, `node --run`, `npm run` among the refused forms and describe the new allowlist rule and the verified-copy execution; the unsandboxed limits stay.\nRun, one at a time: every cli/test/hook*.test.ts and hooks_*.test.ts, content_trust, strict_json, doctor_hooks, security, security_gates, approval_policy, managed_policy_enforcement, leak_guard, check_requirements; tsc --noEmit; the complexity gate; check_invariants; check_doc_versions; the hooks schema `--check` if the trust record changes (it should not). Changed/new test files three times. Commit `fix(hooks): <what>` + `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. Report under 350 words: the final allowlist as implemented (file:line), how verified-copy execution works and its limits, tests/mutations, anything it breaks for existing project hooks, commit hash.","type":"message","recipient":"a317b41792fa50650","recipient_kind":"agent","content":"New HK-1 work in a NEW worktree /home/mike/nexus-…"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3669,"cache_read_input_tokens":306731,"output_tokens":5070,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":489},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3669,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":5070,"cache_read_input_tokens":306731,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3669,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3669},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":15844,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01JXhnQiUb9pjiz6ZVvdKJFe":{"to":"a317b41792fa50650","summary":"Fix HK-1 pinning: allowlist plus verified-bytes exec","message":"New HK-1 work in a NEW worktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/hk1b (branch `v1/hk1-pinning-allowlist`, created from origin/release/v1.3.0 tip d9b0f5a6; your old worktree ~/nexus-wt/hk1 is stale: do not use it). A hostile check of the merged HK-1 found defects; read the findings: `gh pr view 782 --comments` (last comment, \"Independent hostile check of the merged HK-1 code\"). Fix, on that branch, local commits, then push with a plain `git push origin v1/hk1-pinning-allowlist` (never force) and report; I open the PR. Same rules as before (throwaway HOME, `TMPDIR=/dev/shm`, leak guard, kill by pid, mutations from a copy and restored from it, never `git checkout`, complexity gate untouched, `git fetch origin` + merge origin/release/v1.3.0 often, never rebase). Files are claimed by me (HK1_PINNING_ALLOWLIST).\n\n1. BLOCKER: replace the blocklist (`coveredProjectFiles`, cli/src/core/hooks.ts ~342-449, INTERPRETERS/WRAPPERS ~305-310) by an ALLOWLIST for PROJECT hooks. A project hook command is accepted only if (a) argv[0] is itself an existing regular workspace file (pinned), run directly; or (b) argv[0] is a short fixed list of plain shells/interpreters (sh, bash, dash, zsh, node, python3 and versioned names, perl, ruby: decide a minimal list; `env`, wrappers, `script`, `su`, `awk`, `make`, `npm`, `npx`, `git`, `jq` etc. are NOT on it) AND its first non-option argument is a pinned regular workspace file AND every option before it is on a short per-interpreter list of harmless flags (e.g. `-e`/`-u`/`-x`/`--posix`/`--no-warnings`; NO inline-code flags, NO loader flags, NO `--run`, NO `--require/--import`), AND later arguments that name existing workspace paths are pinned or refused as before. Everything else is refused for project hooks with a message naming the rule and telling the operator to put that hook in their own operator hooks file. Operator hooks are unaffected. Tests: every attack in the finding must be refused at approve time and listed as invalid (`[\"script\",\"-qec\",\"sh g.sh\",\"/dev/null\"]`, `[\"awk\",\"BEGIN{system(\\\"sh g.sh\\\")}\"]`, `[\"node\",\"--run\",\"x\"]`, `[\"npm\",\"run\",\"x\"]`, `ash`, `csh`, `tcsh`, `mksh`, `rbash`, `lua5.4 -e`, `Rscript -e`, `su -c`, `runuser -c`, `env`, `busybox`, `xargs`, `nice bash -c`, `timeout 5 bash -c`, `sh -- -c`, combined `-ec`, `--command=`), and ordinary forms still work (`[\"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"bash\",\"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"node\",\"scripts/guard.js\",\"--flag\"]`, `[\"/usr/bin/true\"]` as an absolute path outside the workspace: decide: allow only if the operator pinned? say what you chose; the shipped examples/hooks must still pass hooks_examples). Mutation per rule.\n2. BLOCKER: TOCTOU between the hash check and the spawn is winnable from the sandbox (`hookStillCurrent` + `runHookProcess`, hooks.ts ~645-683, hook_runner.ts ~102: a background loop doing copy+rename over the pinned script won 27 of 80 hook runs). Fix: execute the verified bytes: read each pinned file ONCE, check the hash on those bytes, write them to a private per-run directory (mode 0700, files 0500, outside the workspace, removed after the run) and spawn with the copies substituted for the pinned paths (argv[0] file or the interpreter's script argument), so a later change of the workspace file cannot affect the running hook. Document that scripts referencing their own directory break and must be self-contained. For (a) direct execution use the verified copy as argv[0] (or `fexecve`-style via /proc/self/fd if you can do it simply and safely; the private copy is fine). Test: a test that swaps the workspace file between hash and spawn (inject a hook point/seam) and proves the ORIGINAL bytes run; and a stress test (bounded, ~20 iterations, no flakiness) with a concurrent swapper that must never execute the swapped bytes. Mutation: spawn the workspace path again → tests fail.\n3. Siblings (a pinned script sourcing `./lib.sh`) are not pinned: state in docs/HOOKS.md that project hooks must be one self-contained file and that sourced/imported files are NOT covered; consider a cheap defence: run interpreters with the private copy dir as the working directory? NO (cwd must stay the workspace as today): just document.\n4. Non-blocking, do them: (a) security.ts protected paths: add absolute `~/.config/nexus/hooks.json` and `extension_trust.json` (resolved from the real config dir via the same function the hooks loader uses) so a workspace of `~/.config/nexus` is covered, with a test; (b) `forbiddenDeletion` (tools.ts ~59): refuse `delete_file recursive` of a directory that CONTAINS a protected path, with a test; (c) hook_runner.ts ~197-200: on `output_left_open` kill the process group (signalAll SIGKILL) before finishing, test with `(sleep 20) &` child; (d) docs/HOOKS.md: list `script`, `su`, `node --run`, `npm run` among the refused forms and describe the new allowlist rule and the verified-copy execution; the unsandboxed limits stay.\nRun, one at a time: every cli/test/hook*.test.ts and hooks_*.test.ts, content_trust, strict_json, doctor_hooks, security, security_gates, approval_policy, managed_policy_enforcement, leak_guard, check_requirements; tsc --noEmit; the complexity gate; check_invariants; check_doc_versions; the hooks schema `--check` if the trust record changes (it should not). Changed/new test files three times. Commit `fix(hooks): <what>` + `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`. Report under 350 words: the final allowlist as implemented (file:line), how verified-copy execution works and its limits, tests/mutations, anything it breaks for existing project hooks, commit hash."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011ChNcNiNDMgafjFo8F3CWF","requestRef":"ab906705-091d-4ec8-8f5a-69ef21323822","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"1de9403e-7590-4501-b92e-18a120103108","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:21:16.371Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
