{"parentUuid":"143e8ea2-d5c4-4841-a96f-b1d6f0bd6dc8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"dad53875-8d3c-48f3-8853-91a82ffe0b95","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a387832c0d2d19d84</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01Dwt938pUdMxK5mPm8nHPik</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a387832c0d2d19d84.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review PR #798 HK-3 plugin trust\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES** at `38c51b18823e1b5847f2935c72df9e4dfda8e9cf`. At the end of the review the PR was still OPEN, unmerged, and the head had not moved. The worktree and my probes are removed, and the throwaway-HOME runs never touched your real config.\n\n**Acceptance wording.** HK-3 in `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:103` is \"decision D18; migration note for existing plugins\". The stricter wording comes from `_plans/HK_PLAN.md` §4.1: per-file approval, a changed file asks again, and workspace trust alone no longer loads workspace plugins. You supplied the agent self-approval clause.\n\n| Clause | Met? | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| No import before approval | Yes, for the entry file | Run: trust-only, `NEXUS_TRUST_WORKSPACE=1`, a symlinked file, a symlinked plugin directory, an approved symlink whose target changed, read-only mode and the same bytes in another workspace or at another path all executed nothing. |\n| Changed file asks again | Yes for the entry file | Run: status `changed`, with a notice. |\n| Agent cannot approve itself | **No** | See blocker 2. |\n| Workspace trust alone no longer imports | Yes | Run. |\n\n**Blockers**\n1. `plugins.ts` `loadPluginFile`: workspace plugin code runs without its own approval.\n   - Only the entry file is hashed. An approved `.mjs` that imports `./lib/h.mjs`, or an approved `.js` that `require`s a `.cjs`, runs that sibling unchecked.\n   - I edited the sibling after approval and it executed (status stayed `trusted`).\n   - An unapproved sibling `.mjs` in the plugins directory was also imported by an approved plugin.\n   - The `files` field in the trust record (`content_trust.ts`) exists for this and is unused. The docs do not mention the gap.\n   - Fix: either record and recheck the covered files, or state \"single self-contained file\" and refuse any import that leaves it.\n2. `index.ts:1143`: the terminal-only approval is defeated by a pty. `echo y | script -qec \"nexus plugins trust ...\"` approved the plugin and wrote the record. `hooks approve` falls the same way, so this is inherited from HK-1.\n   - A `run_command` agent can write a plugin and then approve it.\n   - Fix: gate this in the shared approval path (for example, refuse inside agent-spawned process trees) and add a pty regression test for both commands.\n3. TOCTOU, which the PR discloses: `plugins.ts` hashes the file, then calls `import(path)`, which re-reads it.\n   - I ran a crude rename-flipper over 400 files. 216 passed the hash check and 92 of those loaded the attacker's bytes. The window is real, not theoretical.\n   - Fix: read the bytes once, hash them, and import those same bytes (for example from a `data:` URL for single-file plugins).\n\n**Non-blocking**\n- Policy is correct. Run: `disable_workspace` and `plugins.allow` refuse before any approval is read (`policy-refused`). An allow entry cannot approve an unapproved file.\n- The store is mode 0600 in a 0700 directory and holds no secrets. `extension_trust.json` is a protected path (`security.ts:171`); dropping it fails only `hooks_self_approval`.\n- Workspace equal to HOME makes global plugins read as `untrusted`. That fails closed but is surprising.\n- The plan's migration notice, doctor finding and shadowing test are absent.\n- The docs claim \"exact plugin bytes\" and say nothing about the sibling gap, the TOCTOU or pty approval.\n- Invalid UTF-8 and a plugin that registers tools showed no new grant path. I did not check large-file handling.\n\n**What \"partial\" leaves out:** subprocess execution (HK-3.2), doctor, the migration notice, and the CLI tests. It is not acceptable for an RC while the PR calls itself an approval-by-bytes gate with the blockers above open. Merging it with the limitations documented honestly would be acceptable.\n\n**Tests at head:** all green: `plugin_trust` 3, `workspace_trust` 5, `plugins_dirs` 1, `managed_policy_allowlists` 18, `content_trust` 7, `security` 14, `security_gates` 10, `leak_guard` 6, `check_requirements` 28, `hooks_schema` 5, `hooks_project_trust` 10, `hooks_self_approval` 10, `managed_policy_enforcement` 113, `harness` 90, and `doctor` 60 once `dist` was built. `tsc`, the complexity gate, the invariants check and the doc-versions check pass.\n\n**Mutations**\n- Skipping the hash comparison: caught.\n- Importing before the approval check: **survived**, because no test uses an import marker.\n- Dropping the terminal check: **survived**, because there is no CLI test for `plugins trust`.\n- Dropping the store's protected path: caught only by the hooks test.\n\n**Not checked:** large files, the `/mcp` add flow, Node 22 against 24, and the TUI.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>94435</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>920625</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"ff3bec17-f58f-4269-8674-12488d38010f","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:27:58.530Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"task-notification","producer":"session-task","runId":"0mwvb89lb-f01abf84"},"promptSource":"system","turnOrigin":"task_notification","turnPromptId":"dad53875-8d3c-48f3-8853-91a82ffe0b95","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":40,"turnIndex":292},"queueSkipAttachments":true,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
