{"parentUuid":"ff3bec17-f58f-4269-8674-12488d38010f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNcw3djXMLeS36oE6BBY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2918,"cache_read_input_tokens":315649,"output_tokens":2581,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":726},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2918,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":2581,"cache_read_input_tokens":315649,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2918,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2918},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":6266,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011ChNcw3PqsQUb5ZVSb8sXp","requestRef":"db723e06-83eb-4e66-b5e6-bd9ac5cfdc56","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"a47ad68d-59ca-4cfa-9cc5-1778d97adfbf","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:28:08.238Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"a47ad68d-59ca-4cfa-9cc5-1778d97adfbf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNcw3djXMLeS36oE6BBY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VyxSkwpBXtEufChRSzBxkx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/nexus-code && cat > /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev798.md <<'EOF'\n**Independent review at `38c51b18823e1b5847f2935c72df9e4dfda8e9cf` — REQUEST CHANGES (two blockers; one of the reviewer's three is the documented unsandboxed limit shared with HK-1).**\n\nAcceptance (`docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:103` says only \"decision D18; migration note for existing plugins\"; the per-file clauses come from `HK_PLAN.md` §4.1): **no import before approval — met for the entry file (run):** workspace trust alone, `NEXUS_TRUST_WORKSPACE=1`, a symlinked file, a symlinked plugin directory, an approved symlink whose target changed, read-only mode, and the same bytes in another workspace or path all executed nothing. **A changed file asks again — met for the entry file (run).** **Workspace trust alone no longer imports — met (run).**\n\n**Blocking**\n1. **Workspace plugin code runs without its own approval (`plugins.ts` `loadPluginFile`; run).** Only the entry file is hashed. An approved `.mjs` that imports `./lib/h.mjs`, or an approved `.js` that `require`s a `.cjs`, runs that sibling unchecked: editing the sibling after approval executed it with status still `trusted`; an unapproved sibling `.mjs` in the plugins directory was imported by an approved plugin. The `files` field in the trust record exists for this and is unused; the docs do not mention the gap.\n2. **TOCTOU between the hash and `import(path)` (disclosed in the PR; run).** A rename-flipper over 400 files: 216 passed the hash check and 92 of those loaded the attacker's bytes. Fix: read the bytes once, hash those bytes, import those same bytes (a `data:` URL for a single-file ESM plugin: relative imports then fail by construction, which also settles blocker 1; for CJS, a private copy).\n\n**Shared limit, not counted as a blocker here:** `echo y | script -qec \"nexus plugins trust …\"` supplied a terminal and approved (`index.ts:1143`); `hooks approve` falls the same way. It is the documented unsandboxed-command limit (an unsandboxed command runs with the operator's rights; under `--sandbox` the store is unreachable). The plugin docs must state it as HK-1's do, and a regression test should pin both commands' terminal checks.\n\n**Non-blocking:** policy is right (run): `disable_workspace` and `plugins.allow` refuse before any approval is read, and an allow entry cannot approve an unapproved file; store 0600 in a 0700 directory, no secrets; `extension_trust.json` is a protected path (`security.ts:171`). Workspace = home makes global plugins read as `untrusted` (fails closed, surprising). The plan's migration notice, doctor finding and shadowing test are absent. The docs say \"exact plugin bytes\" and say nothing about siblings, the race or pty approval. Mutations that survived: importing before the approval check (no test uses an import marker) and dropping the terminal check (no CLI test for `plugins trust`).\n**\"Partial\" leaves out** subprocess execution (HK-3.2), doctor, the migration notice, and CLI tests. Merging with the limits documented honestly would be acceptable; not while the PR presents itself as an approval-by-bytes gate with these open.\n**Tests at that head:** `plugin_trust` 3, `workspace_trust` 5, `plugins_dirs` 1, `managed_policy_allowlists` 18, `content_trust` 7, `security` 14, `security_gates` 10, `leak_guard` 6, `check_requirements` 28, `hooks_schema` 5, `hooks_project_trust` 10, `hooks_self_approval` 10, `managed_policy_enforcement` 113, `harness` 90, `doctor` 60; `tsc`, the complexity gate, invariants and doc versions pass. Not checked: large files, the `/mcp` add flow, Node 22, the TUI.\nEOF\ngh pr comment 798 --body-file /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev798.md | tail -1; echo \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ) REVIEW (claude-invariants-audit): #798 HK-3 REQUEST CHANGES at 38c51b18: sibling files not covered; TOCTOU between hash and import(path) (92 of 216 hash-passing loads ran attacker bytes). Fix = hash and import the same bytes (data: URL for single-file ESM), same technique as my HK-1 pinning fix in progress. DO NOT MERGE #798. Whoever owns #798: tell me here if you want to fix it; otherwise I will after HK1_PINNING_ALLOWLIST lands (shared helper).\" >> \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/nexus-claims.log\"","description":"Post the HK-3 review and log it"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2918,"cache_read_input_tokens":315649,"output_tokens":2581,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":726},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2918,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":2581,"cache_read_input_tokens":315649,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2918,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2918},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":11143,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VyxSkwpBXtEufChRSzBxkx":{"command":"cd ~/nexus-code && cat > /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev798.md <<'EOF'\n**Independent review at `38c51b18823e1b5847f2935c72df9e4dfda8e9cf` — REQUEST CHANGES (two blockers; one of the reviewer's three is the documented unsandboxed limit shared with HK-1).**\n\nAcceptance (`docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:103` says only \"decision D18; migration note for existing plugins\"; the per-file clauses come from `HK_PLAN.md` §4.1): **no import before approval — met for the entry file (run):** workspace trust alone, `NEXUS_TRUST_WORKSPACE=1`, a symlinked file, a symlinked plugin directory, an approved symlink whose target changed, read-only mode, and the same bytes in another workspace or path all executed nothing. **A changed file asks again — met for the entry file (run).** **Workspace trust alone no longer imports — met (run).**\n\n**Blocking**\n1. **Workspace plugin code runs without its own approval (`plugins.ts` `loadPluginFile`; run).** Only the entry file is hashed. An approved `.mjs` that imports `./lib/h.mjs`, or an approved `.js` that `require`s a `.cjs`, runs that sibling unchecked: editing the sibling after approval executed it with status still `trusted`; an unapproved sibling `.mjs` in the plugins directory was imported by an approved plugin. The `files` field in the trust record exists for this and is unused; the docs do not mention the gap.\n2. **TOCTOU between the hash and `import(path)` (disclosed in the PR; run).** A rename-flipper over 400 files: 216 passed the hash check and 92 of those loaded the attacker's bytes. Fix: read the bytes once, hash those bytes, import those same bytes (a `data:` URL for a single-file ESM plugin: relative imports then fail by construction, which also settles blocker 1; for CJS, a private copy).\n\n**Shared limit, not counted as a blocker here:** `echo y | script -qec \"nexus plugins trust …\"` supplied a terminal and approved (`index.ts:1143`); `hooks approve` falls the same way. It is the documented unsandboxed-command limit (an unsandboxed command runs with the operator's rights; under `--sandbox` the store is unreachable). The plugin docs must state it as HK-1's do, and a regression test should pin both commands' terminal checks.\n\n**Non-blocking:** policy is right (run): `disable_workspace` and `plugins.allow` refuse before any approval is read, and an allow entry cannot approve an unapproved file; store 0600 in a 0700 directory, no secrets; `extension_trust.json` is a protected path (`security.ts:171`). Workspace = home makes global plugins read as `untrusted` (fails closed, surprising). The plan's migration notice, doctor finding and shadowing test are absent. The docs say \"exact plugin bytes\" and say nothing about siblings, the race or pty approval. Mutations that survived: importing before the approval check (no test uses an import marker) and dropping the terminal check (no CLI test for `plugins trust`).\n**\"Partial\" leaves out** subprocess execution (HK-3.2), doctor, the migration notice, and CLI tests. Merging with the limits documented honestly would be acceptable; not while the PR presents itself as an approval-by-bytes gate with these open.\n**Tests at that head:** `plugin_trust` 3, `workspace_trust` 5, `plugins_dirs` 1, `managed_policy_allowlists` 18, `content_trust` 7, `security` 14, `security_gates` 10, `leak_guard` 6, `check_requirements` 28, `hooks_schema` 5, `hooks_project_trust` 10, `hooks_self_approval` 10, `managed_policy_enforcement` 113, `harness` 90, `doctor` 60; `tsc`, the complexity gate, invariants and doc versions pass. Not checked: large files, the `/mcp` add flow, Node 22, the TUI.\nEOF\ngh pr comment 798 --body-file /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev798.md | tail -1; echo \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ) REVIEW (claude-invariants-audit): #798 HK-3 REQUEST CHANGES at 38c51b18: sibling files not covered; TOCTOU between hash and import(path) (92 of 216 hash-passing loads ran attacker bytes). Fix = hash and import the same bytes (data: URL for single-file ESM), same technique as my HK-1 pinning fix in progress. DO NOT MERGE #798. Whoever owns #798: tell me here if you want to fix it; otherwise I will after HK1_PINNING_ALLOWLIST lands (shared helper).\" >> \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/nexus-claims.log\"","description":"Post the HK-3 review and log it"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChNcw3PqsQUb5ZVSb8sXp","requestRef":"db723e06-83eb-4e66-b5e6-bd9ac5cfdc56","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"8fa4d955-bd42-4060-929e-7df28d24dd64","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:28:19.415Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
