{"parentUuid":"321a054a-965e-43b9-a7a5-5cf7af71f138","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNczTKqao4GxvvLSZxJG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DDaBpby5YBMc5USsPiMBQE","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review PR #804 MT-2 registry","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent review of GitHub PR #804 (MT-2 slice 2: tool registry binding each spec to its handler) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 804 --json headRefOid` (expected `8b540fb9…`), review exactly that, and say whether it moved or merged. Read the PR body, `docs/TOOL_REGISTRY.md`, the plan section \"MT-2: tool registry and router\" in `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md`, `cli/src/core/tool_registry.ts`, the changes in `cli/src/core/spine.ts` (`resolveHandler`), `cli/src/agent.ts` (`effectiveTools` → `modelTools`, registrations), `cli/src/tools.ts` (`registerBridgeCapabilities`, `isBridgeBound`), `cli/src/core/capabilities.ts`, `cli/src/parser.ts`, `cli/src/mcp.ts`. Acceptance: \"focused dispatch and G-3 suites green; adding a tool is a registered spec+handler pair; no new grant path\". The slice must be behaviour-preserving: the slice-1 characterization (`cli/test/tool_surface_characterization.test.ts`, `tool_dispatch_characterization.test.ts`, fixtures `model_tools_v1.json`, `tool_bindings_v1.json`) must pass UNEDITED (check `git diff origin/release/v1.3.0...HEAD -- cli/test/fixtures cli/test/tool_*characterization* cli/test/helpers/tool_characterization.ts` is empty or justified).\n\nJudge, by running and reading: (1) Handler resolution: for EVERY shipped tool and for MCP, plugin, extension and unknown tools, is the handler that runs the same as on base (`origin/release/v1.3.0` before this PR: the PR's merge-base/first parent)? Build a table at HEAD and BASE (a second detached worktree for base) from the registry/spine resolution, plus an end-to-end run of a few representative tools through dispatch at both. Any difference in which of the five handler sources wins (spine handler, native binding handler, capability handler, context defaultHandler, bridge) is a finding. (2) The one removed fallback `|| capability.handler`: prove it is the same object `registry.getHandler` returns for every registered tool, including tools registered after startup (extensions added by MCP/plugins sync, `unregister` paths), and tools whose handler is set via `setHandler` later. (3) Grant paths: can anything newly make a tool run without the approval/decision path, lower an effect, register an extension with `read_workspace`/`internal`/write effect, or shadow a built-in tool name (an MCP server or plugin naming a tool `read_file`, `run_command`, `write_file`: what happens at register time and at resolve time vs. base: which wins?). `capabilities()` still returns the underlying `CapabilityRegistry` with `register`/`unregister`/`setHandler`: list the callers outside tests; is any reachable from model- or workspace-controlled input (MCP tool lists, plugin definitions, skills)? Compare against base. (4) `modelTools(ctx)` equals the old `effectiveTools` in every configuration: diff the model-visible list across a matrix at HEAD and BASE yourself (default, auto, read-only, subagent depth 1, each role, delegated allow-lists, objective on/off, requirements, skills active/constrained, output masking, MCP and plugin tools present, enable_tools groups) rather than trusting the slice-1 fixtures only; report any difference, including ordering and filtering by role deny lists and skill constraints. (5) Derived lists: `HARNESS_TOOLS`, parser `KNOWN_TOOLS`, serve-mcp list identical to base. Is the serve-mcp list still minus the same two tools (a tool newly exposed through MCP server mode is a security finding). (6) Fail-closed construction: does a registration error at startup now crash the CLI/sidecar for a user-visible reason that base tolerated (e.g. duplicate extension tool names from two MCP servers, a plugin tool colliding with another)? Try it: two MCP stub servers (or the test helpers) exposing the same tool name, a plugin tool named like a built-in. Compare base vs head behaviour and say which is safer. (7) Complexity gate unchanged for the five functions; `agent.ts` size/line growth.\n\nThen run at HEAD and BASE, one at a time, the failing sets of: slice-1 files, `tool_registry`, `capabilities`, `lsp_shape`, `tool_groups`, `role_deny_lists`, `acp_mapping`, `tool_grammar*`, `command_rules_dispatch`, `managed_policy_dispatch`, `vllm_nullable_dispatch`, `approval_policy`, `injection_gate`, `security_gates`, `sandbox_denial_events`, `agt111_subagent_parity`, `mcp_tool_through_spine`, `hooks_*`, `sidecar`, `acp_stdio`, `protocol_parity`, `critical_complexity`, `skills*`, `mcp`, `plugins*`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): resolve the wrong handler source; swap `modelTools` ordering; accept a duplicate; accept an extension with `read_workspace`: each must fail a test.\n\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev804 <full sha>` and for base `... worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev804-base <merge-base sha>` (`git merge-base origin/release/v1.3.0 <head>` gives the release commit the PR was last merged with; use `git log --first-parent` to pick the commit before the PR's changes); `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules <wt>/cli/` and `mkdir -p <wt>/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} <wt>/core/target/release/` in each. Tests: `cd <wt>/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never touch the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. No network, no inference. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. Remove probes and both worktrees at the end. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); prefix `rtk proxy` if output looks filtered; a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\n\nReport under 500 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; behaviour-preservation result per item (1)-(6) with the run that shows it; findings with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (any change in which handler runs, any new way a tool avoids the approval path or shadows a built-in, any newly exposed tool, or any crash on input base tolerated, is blocking); failing-set table HEAD vs BASE; mutation results; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2127,"cache_read_input_tokens":323459,"output_tokens":2722,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2127,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2722,"cache_read_input_tokens":323459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2127,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2127},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":16888,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01DDaBpby5YBMc5USsPiMBQE":{"description":"Review PR #804 MT-2 registry","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent review of GitHub PR #804 (MT-2 slice 2: tool registry binding each spec to its handler) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 804 --json headRefOid` (expected `8b540fb9…`), review exactly that, and 