{"parentUuid":"0356d69c-f2c0-4b97-9e46-fba2f89d5087","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"96fe070c-260d-42d9-bc1a-91e5848ca835","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TWDHiJCdE2pWuNbxpjFcXD","type":"tool_result","content":"BG3_TRUST_ABUSE_MODEL","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"77b1de55-652d-44a9-8c7f-60042c635ce3","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:44:40.514Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"BG3_TRUST_ABUSE_MODEL","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0356d69c-f2c0-4b97-9e46-fba2f89d5087","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"77b1de55-652d-44a9-8c7f-60042c635ce3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNeBgdDQUev6HS22uXLL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V18grc6q37SCBioWiKW7JJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Security review PR #803 BG-3 trust","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent hostile security review of GitHub PR #803 (BG-3 trust and abuse model for event triggers: spec, trust store, rate limiting, `nexus triggers` CLI; firing not built) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 803 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and say whether it moved or merged. NOTE: the PR says its branch was based on a local tip ahead of origin: check `git log origin/release/v1.3.0..<head>` and the diff really contains only this PR's 10 files and no foreign commits. Read the PR body, `docs/TRIGGERS.md`, `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` BG-3 row, `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md` (BG-3 and unattended-run rules), `cli/src/trigger_spec.ts`, `trigger_trust.ts`, `trigger_rate_limit.ts`, `triggers_cli.ts` (paths may be under cli/src/core/), the tests, and how HK-1/HK-3 handled the same classes (`gh pr view 782 --comments`, `gh pr view 798 --comments`): terminal-only approval, protected-path coverage for the trust store, hashing the exact bytes that will be used, no sibling/file indirection.\nThe owner has just put BG-3 in scope for v1.3.0, so this trust model will be the base for a firer that turns file changes and webhooks into unattended jobs. Judge it as the authority boundary: could a hostile repository, a model with sandboxed tools, or a remote webhook caller make a job run that the operator did not approve?\nAttack by RUNNING (throwaway HOME, temp workspaces, no network, no inference):\n1. Spec parsing: strict JSON (duplicate keys, BOM, unknown keys, huge files, deep nesting, numeric edge cases, type confusion), bounds and rate defaults (can a spec set its own rate limit to infinity/0/negative/NaN/huge, shrink the quarantine, widen globs to `/**`, point paths outside the workspace via `..`/symlinks/absolute/`~`, name another workspace), canonical SHA-256 (is it computed over canonical bytes that the firer will later use; is there a second parse that can differ: parser differentials, key order, unicode normalisation, `__proto__`).\n2. Trust store: content-hash trust is per exact spec bytes? what does 'changed spec asks again' do; store location/mode (0600/0700), atomic writes, symlink at the store path, other-uid, corrupted/truncated store (never fails open?), workspace-trust gating (untrusted workspace → no trust), revoke semantics, stale records for deleted triggers, the store as a protected path for the agent's file tools (write_file/edit_file/delete_file/patch across `..`, symlinks, workspace = HOME/`~/.config/nexus`: compare with `security.ts` DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS for extension_trust.json), and who can approve: the CLI requires a terminal: show the pty bypass (`echo y | script -qec \"nexus triggers approve ...\"`) and the sandboxed behaviour (is the store reachable under `--sandbox`), and state what is documented. Is there an env var, flag, or API path to approve without a terminal (`--yes`, library function exported for tests that production code can call)?\n3. Rate limiting and quarantine: sliding window correctness (clock step backwards/forwards, many triggers, huge event counts, memory growth: unbounded maps keyed by attacker-controlled names/paths), the 3-strike/15-minute quarantine (can an attacker trigger quarantine of another workspace's trigger: DoS; can quarantine be reset by editing the spec or restarting; is state persisted; persisted where; tamperable), workspace caps.\n4. Webhook design as far as it exists: is there a listener in this PR (it says not)? what does the spec promise for authentication (shared secret, HMAC, replay protection, bind address: localhost only, origin check, body size) and is it adequate for the firer to build on; what is MISSING that the firer must add (list concretely).\n5. Docs vs code: every claim in `docs/TRIGGERS.md` verified.\nRun at head, one at a time: the new test files (list from the diff), `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `hooks_self_approval`, `protocol_schema`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): approve without terminal; skip the hash comparison; fail open on a corrupt store; drop the workspace-trust gate; drop the quarantine: each must fail a test.\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev803 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev803/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev803/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev803/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev803/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never touch the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. Remove probes and the worktree at the end. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); prefix `rtk proxy` if output looks filtered; a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\nReport under 500 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; findings with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (any way for a hostile repo, a sandboxed model tool, or a remote caller to get a trigger approved, to change an approved spec without re-approval, to fail open, or to starve the rate limit is blocking; the documented unsandboxed-shell limit is not); the concrete list of what the firer MUST add to be safe; test counts; mutation results; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":636,"cache_read_input_tokens":338790,"output_tokens":3761,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":275},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":636},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":3761,"cache_read_input_tokens":338790,"cache_creation_input_tokens":636,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":636,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":15778,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01V18grc6q37SCBioWiKW7JJ":{"description":"Security review PR #803 BG-3 trust","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent hostile security review of GitHub PR #803 (BG-3 trust and abuse model for event triggers: spec, trust store, rate limiting, `nexus triggers` CLI; firing not built) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 803 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and say whether it moved or merged. NOTE: the PR says its branch was based on a local tip ahead of origin: check `git log origin/release/v1.3.0..<head>` and the diff really contains only this PR's 10 files and no foreign commits. Read the PR body, `docs/TRIGGERS.md`, `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` BG-3 row, `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md` (BG-3 and unattended-run rules), `cli/src/trigger_spec.ts`, `trigger_trust.ts`, `trigger_rate_limit.ts`, `triggers_cli.ts` (paths may be under cli/src/core/), the tests, and how HK-1/HK-3 handled the same classes (`gh pr view 782 --comments`, `gh pr view 798 --comments`): terminal-only approval, protected-path coverage for the trust store, hashing the exact bytes that will be used, no sibling/file indirection.\nThe owner has just put BG-3 in scope for v1.3.0, so this trust model will be the base for a firer that turns file changes and webhooks into unattended jobs. Judge it as the authority boundary: could a hostile repository, a model with sandboxed tools, or a remote webhook caller make a job run that the operator did not approve?\nAttack by RUNNING (throwaway HOME, temp workspaces, no network, no inference):\n1. Spec parsing: strict JSON (duplicate keys, BOM, unknown keys, huge files, deep nesting, numeric edge cases, type confusion), bounds and rate defaults (can a spec set its own rate limit to infinity/0/negative/NaN/huge, shrink the quarantine, widen globs to `/**`, point paths outside the workspace via `..`/symlinks/absolute/`~`, name another workspace), canonical SHA-256 (is it computed over canonical bytes that the firer will later use; is there a second parse that can differ: parser differentials, key order, unicode normalisation, `__proto__`).\n2. Trust store: content-hash trust is per exact spec bytes? what does 'changed spec asks again' do; store location/mode (0600/0700), atomic writes, symlink at the store path, other-uid, corrupted/truncated store (never fails open?), workspace-trust gating (untrusted workspace → no trust), revoke semantics, stale records for deleted triggers, the store as a protected path for the agent's file tools (write_file/edit_file/delete_file/patch across `..`, symlinks, workspace = HOME/`~/.config/nexus`: compare with `security.ts` DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS for extension_trust.json), and who can approve: the CLI requires a terminal: show the pty bypass (`echo y | script -qec \"nexus triggers approve ...\"`) and the sandboxed behaviour (is the store reachable under `--sandbox`), and state what is documented. Is there an env var, flag, or API path to approve without a terminal (`--yes`, library function exported for tests that production code can call)?\n3. Rate limiting and quarantine: sliding window correctness (clock step backwards/forwards, many triggers, huge event counts, memory growth: unbounded maps keyed by attacker-controlled names/paths), the 3-strike/15-minute quarantine (can an attacker trigger quarantine of another workspace's trigger: DoS; can quarantine be reset by editing the spec or restarting; is state persisted; persisted where; tamperable), workspace caps.\n4. Webhook design as far as it exists: is there a listener in this PR (it says not)? what does the spec promise for authentication (shared secret, HMAC, replay protection, bind address: localhost only, origin check, body size) and is it adequate for the firer to build on; what is MISSING that the firer must add (list concretely).\n5. Docs vs code: every claim in `docs/TRIGGERS.md` verified.\nRun at head, one at a time: the new test files (list from the diff), `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `hooks_self_approval`, `protocol_schema`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): approve without terminal; skip the hash comparison; fail open on a corrupt store; drop the workspace-trust gate; drop the quarantine: each must fail a test.\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev803 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev803/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev803/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev803/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev803/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never touch the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. Remove probes and the worktree at the end. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); prefix `rtk proxy` if output looks filtered; a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\nReport under 500 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; findings with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (any way for a hostile repo, a sandboxed model tool, or a remote caller to get a trigger approved, to change an approved spec without re-approval, to fail open, or to starve the rate limit is blocking; the documented unsandboxed-shell limit is not); the concrete list of what the firer MUST add to be safe; test counts; mutation results; what you did not check."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011ChNeBgTHcgK9ZvDT5h3Dn","requestRef":"7050e2ec-1edc-4533-ae6f-47b05d06113b","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"5m","type":"assistant","uuid":"343969cf-fabc-497c-8716-1956d6520ee3","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:44:52.398Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
