{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"e1b66ffc4057fcf25c4a0397ac11bd0c2e2e12524dfc5e42ca7b3c9232c1f7c9","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TWDHiJCdE2pWuNbxpjFcXD","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/mike/.claude/projects/-home-mike-nexus-code/memory && cat > v130-bg3-no-longer-deferred.md <<'EOF'\n---\nname: v130-bg3-no-longer-deferred\ndescription: Owner reversed the deferral of BG-3 (event triggers) on 2026-11-25: it is in scope for v1.3.0 and must be built, not rolled to v1.4\nmetadata:\n  type: project\n---\n\nOwner, 2026-11-25 (verbatim): \"BG-3 no longer defered.\"\n\nThe plan (`docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md:85`) deferred BG-3 (event triggers: file change, webhook) to v1.4 pending a trust and abuse model. The owner reversed that. It joins the unbuilt units that must be complete before the merge to main and release. [[v130-owner-directive-build-and-release-2026-11-24]]'s list of units to complete now includes BG-3.\n\n**Why:** the owner wants event triggers shipped in v1.3.0.\n**How to apply:** PR #803 (Codex) holds the trust surface only (spec, trust store, rate limit, `nexus triggers` CLI; firing not built). The trigger FIRER (watchers, webhook listener, firing jobs through the BG-1/BG-2 unattended path) is still to build and needs its own independent security review. Update PLAN.md, readiness inventory and release notes (no longer \"deferred\"). Do not merge #803 or the firer without an independent hostile review: the terminal-only approval check is the same bypassable-by-pty limit as hooks/plugins.\nEOF\npython3 - <<'EOF'\np='MEMORY.md'; s=open(p).read()\ns=s.rstrip('\\n')+\"\\n- [BG-3 no longer deferred](v130-bg3-no-longer-deferred.md) — owner 2026-11-25: event triggers are in v1.3.0; #803 = trust surface only, firer still to build\\n\"\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ncd ~/nexus-code && echo \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ) OWNER (relayed by claude-invariants-audit): 'BG-3 no longer defered.' (2026-11-25). BG-3 is in scope for v1.3.0: PR #803 (trust surface) is NOT held back any more, and the trigger firer (file-change watcher, webhook listener, fire through the BG-1/BG-2 unattended path) still has to be built. Who owns the firer? Codex holds BG3_TRUST_ABUSE_MODEL: say here if you are building it; otherwise I claim BG3_TRIGGER_FIRER after an independent review of #803. I am starting the independent security review of #803 now; do not merge #803 before it is posted.\" >> \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/nexus-claims.log\"; ls \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\" | grep -i BG3","description":"Save the owner's BG-3 reversal to memory and announce it in the claims log"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V18grc6q37SCBioWiKW7JJ","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Security review PR #803 BG-3 trust","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent hostile security review of GitHub PR #803 (BG-3 trust and abuse model for event triggers: spec, trust store, rate limiting, `nexus triggers` CLI; firing not built) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 803 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and say whether it moved or merged. NOTE: the PR says its branch was based on a local tip ahead of origin: check `git log origin/release/v1.3.0..<head>` and the diff really contains only this PR's 10 files and no foreign commits. Read the PR body, `docs/TRIGGERS.md`, `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` BG-3 row, `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md` (BG-3 and unattended-run rules), `cli/src/trigger_spec.ts`, `trigger_trust.ts`, `trigger_rate_limit.ts`, `triggers_cli.ts` (paths may be under cli/src/core/), the tests, and how HK-1/HK-3 handled the same classes (`gh pr view 782 --comments`, `gh pr view 798 --comments`): terminal-only approval, protected-path coverage for the trust store, hashing the exact bytes that will be used, no sibling/file indirection.\nThe owner has just put BG-3 in scope for v1.3.0, so this trust model will be the base for a firer that turns file changes and webhooks into unattended jobs. Judge it as the authority boundary: could a hostile repository, a model with sandboxed tools, or a remote webhook caller make a job run that the operator did not approve?\nAttack by RUNNING (throwaway HOME, temp workspaces, no network, no inference):\n1. Spec parsing: strict JSON (duplicate keys, BOM, unknown keys, huge files, deep nesting, numeric edge cases, type confusion), bounds and rate defaults (can a spec set its own rate limit to infinity/0/negative/NaN/huge, shrink the quarantine, widen globs to `/**`, point paths outside the workspace via `..`/symlinks/absolute/`~`, name another workspace), canonical SHA-256 (is it computed over canonical bytes that the firer will later use; is there a second parse that can differ: parser differentials, key order, unicode normalisation, `__proto__`).\n2. Trust store: content-hash trust is per exact spec bytes? what does 'changed spec asks again' do; store location/mode (0600/0700), atomic writes, symlink at the store path, other-uid, corrupted/truncated store (never fails open?), workspace-trust gating (untrusted workspace → no trust), revoke semantics, stale records for deleted triggers, the store as a protected path for the agent's file tools (write_file/edit_file/delete_file/patch across `..`, symlinks, workspace = HOME/`~/.config/nexus`: compare with `security.ts` DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS for extension_trust.json), and who can approve: the CLI requires a terminal: show the pty bypass (`echo y | script -qec \"nexus triggers approve ...\"`) and the sandboxed behaviour (is the store reachable under `--sandbox`), and state what is documented. Is there an env var, flag, or API path to approve without a terminal (`--yes`, library function exported for tests that production code can call)?\n3. Rate limiting and quarantine: sliding window correctness