{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:45:18.435Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a40cb1aaaefbcb27b</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01DDaBpby5YBMc5USsPiMBQE</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a40cb1aaaefbcb27b.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review PR #804 MT-2 registry\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS** at `8b540fb9440441fb13a73b195eed55852b2e8106`. The head did not move and the PR is still OPEN, unmerged. BASE is `d9b0f5a63`, the release tip, which is the merge-base and the first parent of the PR's three commits. There are no blockers.\n\n**Behaviour preservation, each item compared at HEAD and BASE**\n1. **Handler resolution.** A dispatch probe ran every registered tool plus `no_such_tool` in plain, MCP+plugin, and plugin-shadows-all-36-built-ins scenarios. It recorded the source, status, error, output and trace per tool. The JSON was byte-identical at HEAD and BASE. Winners were 25 capability handlers, 16 spine handlers, 1 MCP, 1 plugin, and 1 unregistered tool refused with FAIL-001. The slice-1 fixtures and test files have an empty diff against base.\n2. **Removed `|| capability.handler`.** It is dead code, by reading `capabilities.ts`. `capability` comes from `registry.getByToolName` (`spine.ts:379`). `getHandler` already returns `cap.handler` whenever that is truthy, so the removed fallback could only ever yield undefined. Handlers added later with `setHandler` mutate the same spec object. `unregisterExtensions` removes exactly the tools with category `extension`, which is what base removed. The new `native` binding handler is never set in production: no `register` call passes one.\n3. **Grant paths.** The 9 plugin tools named like built-ins (`read_file`, `run_command`, `write_file`, `git_status`, `update_requirements` and others) behaved identically at both. `add()` skips a name already in the capability registry, so the built-in wins at register and resolve time. Plugin tools carry an unprefixed name, so only plugins can collide; MCP names are prefixed `mcp__server__tool`. Extensions are always `exec`/`egress`. The only non-test caller of the mutating capability-registry APIs is `registerBridgeCapabilities` (`tools.ts:1839`). No MCP, plugin or skill input reaches `register`, `unregister` or `setHandler`, the same as base.\n4. **`modelTools` vs the old `effectiveTools`.** I built 500 seeded random configurations and got identical names, order and hash at both. They varied role, depth, groups, objective state, skills, mask, extensions, mode and delegated lists. Skill constraints are not applied in this list at base either.\n5. **Derived lists.** `HARNESS_TOOLS` (name list and sha256), the serve-mcp list, parser known names and `isWriteOrExecTool` are identical. serve-mcp is still minus `spawn_subagent` and `activate_skill`.\n6. **Fail-closed construction.** No crash on any input I tried. The cases were two plugins with the same tool name, two MCP servers with the same name, two with different names, a plugin named like an MCP tool, and 9 built-in names. Both sides gave identical results and neither threw. `add()` pre-guards duplicates, so the new `register` throws are unreachable from this path. `register` still throws on an empty tool name, as `CapabilityRegistry.register` did at base. Registering the shared module-level specs across 500 agents never tripped the `spec.handler` check.\n7. **Complexity and size.** `critical_complexity` passes (3 of 3) at both. `agent.ts` went from 4985 to 4974 lines.\n\n**Failing sets, HEAD vs BASE.** I ran 53 test files one at a time on each side: the requested list plus every `skill*` and `plugin*` file. Both have 0 failures, and per-file pass counts match. The one difference is `tool_registry`, which does not exist at base (17 of 17 pass at HEAD; the `tool_registry.ts` module itself is absent at base). Totals are 548 passing at HEAD and 531 at BASE. `tsc --noEmit`, `check_invariants` and `check_doc_versions` pass on both.\n\n**Mutations** (restored from copies, tree clean afterwards)\n\n| Mutation | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Wrong handler order (`registry.getHandler` before the spine handler) | `tool_registry` fails 1; both characterization files stay green |\n| Swap objective/requirements offer rank | `tool_registry` fails 1; `tool_surface_characterization` fails 2 |\n| Accept a duplicate | `tool_registry` fails 1 |\n| Accept an extension with `read_workspace` | `tool_registry` fails 1 |\n\n**Findings (all non-blocking)**\n- `tool_registry.ts:150` — `baselineTools` is an exported mutable `ToolRegistry`, and `serveMcpDefinitions()` is computed on every call. A later `baselineTools.register({baseline: true})` would expose a tool over `nexus serve-mcp` with no approval path. No such caller exists. Fix: export it as a read-only view, or freeze it after construction.\n- `spine.ts:346` — The native binding handler now outranks a handler added later with `setHandler`, and the slice-1 characterization cannot see precedence (mutation M1). A unit test catches it, but no production tool uses it. Fix: document or test the intended order, or drop `binding.handler` until slice 3.\n- Pre-existing and unchanged from base: plugin tools named `read_tool_output` or `update_requirements` register as `exec` extensions when the built-in is not registered. A plugin tool named like a built-in appears twice in the model list.\n\n**Not checked.** Rust core behaviour; real MCP servers or plugin loading from disk; the Node 22 vs 24 difference; the ACP kind table beyond `acp_mapping`; CI on the PR's merge commit.\n\nThe worktrees `rev804` and `rev804-base` and my probes are removed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>104047</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>37</tool_uses><duration_ms>971396</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
