{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T23:51:35.273Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>af9c758e5e74da609</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01UUrmuwABRrBpYkSY2aUW4c</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/af9c758e5e74da609.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Hostile review PR #805 hooks allowlist\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES** at `5408fab84cec54887e5c6b5c8106cde004106ee5`. The PR is OPEN, unmerged, and its head had not moved when I finished.\n\n**Earlier blockers**\n1. **Blocklist to allowlist: closed for argv-level forms, but there is a new bypass (B1 below).** I ran about 60 commands through `planProjectCommand`. These are all refused: `-ec`, `-xc`, `-uc`, `-ceu`, `-eC`, `--`, `-`, `-x.sh` as an option, `-Bx`, `-m`, `-Mstrict`, `-rfoo`, `--require=`, `--rcfile`, and the wrappers. A directory, a symlink out of the workspace, and `/dev/fd` or `/proc` script arguments are refused too. Spaces and newlines in names are fine, and `./-x.sh` is pinned.\n2. **Verified-copy execution and the swap race: closed.** The hook prints `$0` and `__filename`, and both are `…/run-XXXX/0/g.sh`; `/proc/self/cmdline` shows the copy. A concurrent copy-and-rename and overwrite writer ran against 150 spawns. That gave 70 runs that all printed GOOD, 80 refusals, 0 swapped runs, and no leftover directories. The private directory is 0700, files are 0500, and a symlinked or group-writable root is refused. Under `--sandbox`, `/tmp` is a fresh tmpfs and `$HOME` is masked, so the private directory is unreachable.\n3. **Non-blocking items: mostly closed, with one regression (S1).** Absolute protected paths held for workspaces `~/.config/nexus`, `~`, `~/.config` and the parent of `~`, using write, edit and delete spellings with `..`, `./` and case variants. The orphan-process test passes.\n\n**Blocking, found by running**\n- **B1: a shebang runs an unpinned workspace file.** `./shebang.sh` containing `#!./evil` (or `#!/proc/self/cwd/evil`) is approved with only `shebang.sh` pinned. The copy executes with cwd = workspace, so it runs the workspace's `evil` and prints `EVIL-WORKSPACE-INTERP-RAN`. After approval the agent can edit `evil`, and the next call runs it with no re-approval. This is the exact case the task listed. Fix: refuse any pinned script whose `#!` line is not an absolute path outside the workspace, or run the copy through an explicit interpreter. `planProjectCommand` does not read the first line.\n- **B2: an interpreter that resolves into the workspace is accepted.** `[\"/proc/self/cwd/bash\",\"g.sh\"]` passes `planProjectCommand` and runs the workspace `bash` (`WORKSPACE-bash-ran`). The plan's `realpath` resolves in nexus's cwd, but the child's cwd is the workspace. This happens when nexus is started with a workspace different from its cwd. The same gap exists with a PATH containing `.`, a relative entry or an empty entry, when the interpreter is not found earlier. I ran `python3.99` through PATH `\"/usr/bin::/bin\"`, `\".:/usr/bin\"` and `\"/usr/bin:.\"`, and the workspace file ran each time. `hooks.ts:371-384` (`onPath` skips empty entries, and `real === undefined` is accepted). Fix: resolve `argv[0]` to an absolute path from absolute trusted PATH entries only, refuse `/proc` and `/dev`, spawn that absolute path, and refuse when it is not found.\n\n**Non-blocking**\n- **S1: refusing directories over 5000 entries is a regression** (`tools.ts` `protectedBelow`). The base had no limit. I ran it against a 5200-file `node_modules`, and it refuses with \"delete it in parts\". Raise the limit well above normal dependency trees or walk without a cap. The synchronous walk also blocks the event loop.\n- **S2: `/tmp/bash` or `/dev/shm/bash` as argv[0] is accepted.** Any absolute path outside the workspace passes, whatever its owner or writability. Under `--sandbox` this is unreachable, but an unsandboxed shell can plant one. Fix together with B2 by using trusted directories.\n- **S3 (read only): `openSync` on the pin target can block on a FIFO** swapped in after the plan (`hooks.ts:~478`), which would freeze nexus. Open with `O_NONBLOCK` and `fstat`.\n- **S4 (read only): there is no sweep of stale `run-*` directories** after a SIGKILL or `process.exit`; cleanup only runs in `.finally`.\n\n**Tests at head, one at a time, 0 failures**\n- Hook tests: `hook_output_reduce` 13, `hook_runner` 8, `hooks_cli` 14, `hooks_config` 6, `hooks_deny` 9, `hooks_examples` 6, `hooks_gate_bypass` 11, `hooks_post_tool` 5, `hooks_project_trust` 10, `hooks_schema` 5, `hooks_self_approval` 16.\n- Others: `content_trust` 7, `strict_json` 9, `doctor_hooks` 5, `security` 14, `security_gates` 10, `deletion_guard` 2, `approval_policy` 16, `leak_guard` 6, `check_requirements` 28, `sandbox_confine` 19, `managed_policy_enforcement` 113.\n- `tsc` is clean, the complexity gate ran and printed its table (I did not read its exit code), and `check_invariants` and `check_doc_versions` pass.\n\n**Mutations, each caught**\n- Spawning the workspace path: 4 failures.\n- One extra option (`-c` on sh): 1 failure in `hooks_self_approval` and 1 in `hooks_cli`.\n- Dropping the interpreter-in-workspace check: 1 failure, in `hooks_cli` only.\n- Dropping the absolute protected path: 1 failure.\n- Dropping `protectedBelow`: 1 failure.\n\nNo test covers B1 or B2.\n\n**Not checked**\n- Operator-hooks regression beyond what the tests cover.\n- `nexus hooks list/approve/test` output by hand.\n- Winning the FIFO race (S3).\n- A real `--sandbox` run.\n- Kill and crash cleanup beyond reading the code.\n\nThe worktree and probe files are removed, and nothing was committed, pushed or commented.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>90208</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>30</tool_uses><duration_ms>526406</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
