{"parentUuid":"c33837a5-ed7d-4e39-b92e-fa6e0fd54485","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNfrS2pfxJDsKieDbrJm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015gLzGqgomZtnXaRaJAw82x","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Build BG-3 trigger firer","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Build the BG-3 trigger firer (event triggers: file-change watcher and webhook listener that start unattended jobs) in the worktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3 (Nexus Code, TypeScript CLI in `cli/`). The owner put BG-3 in scope for v1.3.0. The trust model is PR #803, already pushed to `origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` (head 3e5ab8e95) and under re-review: your branch must STACK on it. Start: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3 && git fetch origin && git status && git checkout -b v1/bg3-firer origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` (the worktree currently has local branch `v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model-cl` at the same commit). Commit locally on `v1/bg3-firer`; do NOT push, no PR (I review first). If #803 changes under review, `git fetch origin` and merge `origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` into your branch (never rebase). Release branch `origin/release/v1.3.0` is merged in often too.\nRead first, fully: `docs/TRIGGERS.md`; `cli/src/trigger_spec.ts`, `trigger_trust.ts`, `core/trigger_rate_limit.ts`, `triggers_cli.ts` (find them: `git ls-files | grep -i trigger`); the independent security review on #803 (`gh pr view 803 --comments`: first comment \"Independent hostile security review at 4dd93530…\" has the list \"What the firer must add\"); the BG-1/BG-2 unattended machinery: `docs/RUNNER.md`, `docs/SCHEDULES.md`, `cli/src/core/job_runner.ts`, `job_submission.ts`, `schedule_engine.ts`, `verifier_check.ts`, `unattended.ts`, `job_receipt.ts`, and the plan `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md` (BG-3 notes, unattended rules); `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` BG-3 row; and AGENTS.md. Acceptance to meet (write it into `docs/TRIGGERS.md` and tests): a trusted, approved trigger fires an unattended job through the SAME submission path as `nexus job`/schedules (no second execution path; read-only default, fresh worktree, no auto, no allow-always, bounded auto-resumes, receipts carry the trigger id/event); an unapproved, changed, revoked, rate-limited, quarantined, or untrusted-workspace trigger fires nothing and says why (visible in `nexus triggers status`/receipts, not silent); a webhook with a wrong/missing/replayed signature is refused before any body parsing beyond the size cap and counts toward the limiter only after authentication (design below); kill-and-resume of a triggered job passes; event floods are bounded and reported, never replayed as a burst.\nMUST-HAVE security properties (from the review; each needs a test that fails when the guard is removed):\n1. Fire exactly what was approved: re-read and re-parse the spec bytes once at fire time, hash, compare with the trust record, fire from THAT parse; re-check workspace trust and approval at every fire.\n2. Spec paths and globs: relative to the workspace, resolved by realpath, must stay inside the workspace (symlink escapes refused); webhook paths relative only; refuse duplicate ids; exclude `.git/`, `.nexus/`, and the trust/spec files from file-change events (a triggered job's own writes must not re-trigger itself: define and test a loop guard, e.g. events caused while a job of the same trigger runs, or paths under the job's worktree, are dropped and counted).\n3. File-change watcher: bounded (max watched directories/files, max pending events, debounce/coalesce window), no unbounded memory; event storms produce at most the rate limit of fires, the rest recorded as `dropped` counts in status; watcher errors (inotify limits) reported not swallowed; the event payload given to the job is data, never instructions: the job prompt comes only from the APPROVED spec; event details (changed paths) are passed in a clearly delimited, size-capped, escaped data block inside the prompt or as a file the spec names, and stated in the docs as untrusted.\n4. Webhook listener: OFF unless an approved webhook trigger exists and the operator runs the runner with it enabled; binds loopback only by default (127.0.0.1; a non-loopback bind requires an explicit flag AND is refused unless authentication is configured); a per-request body size cap (e.g. 64 KiB) and header limits and read timeouts (slow-loris), one request at a time per connection limits, a global concurrent-connection cap; HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body plus a timestamp header (reject skew > 5 minutes) plus a nonce/delivery-id replay cache (bounded, persisted privately or in-memory with restart caveat documented: choose and say), constant-time compare; secret from `secretEnv` (env) (inline secrets as #803 allows are accepted only if >=16 chars; document that env is preferred); Origin/Host are NOT authentication (reject browser-style requests: require a custom header and refuse `Origin` present with a mismatching/unknown value only as defence in depth; DNS-rebinding: validate the Host header against loopback names); constant generic error body so a caller cannot tell which check failed; authentication BEFORE rate limiter recording (use `WorkspaceRateLimiter.checkAndRecord(realWorkspace, id, rate, now)` AFTER auth and before parsing the body into an event); persistent limiter snapshot written privately (0600 in a 0700 dir) and the file added to `DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS` in `cli/src/security.ts` (relative style like `trigger_trust.json`, see how it was added), restored with the validating `restore` (a `false` return = locked/quarantined).\n5. Run mode: where does the firer run? Put it in the existing detached runner (`nexus runner serve`, BG-1) as an additional loop next to the schedule engine, reusing `job_submission.ts`; it needs no new daemon. The runner must also work without any triggers exactly as before (no new listener unless a webhook trigger is approved and enabled: test that a runner with no triggers opens no socket and no watcher).\n6. `nexus triggers` additions: `status` shows per-trigger state (approved/changed/revoked/quarantined/rate/dropped/last fire/last refusal reason); implement `release` (clear a quarantine; terminal-only like approve; documented in docs) now that quarantine is persisted; `fire-test <id>` for the operator to dry-run an event against a spec without starting a job (prints what would fire and what would be refused).\n7. Docs: `docs/TRIGGERS.md` (full lifecycle, threat model, what an event can and cannot do, the limits: unsandboxed shell with faked terminal can approve; same-uid process can read the webhook secret env), `docs/RUNNER.md` cross-reference, `docs/USER_MANUAL.md` section, CHANGELOG Unreleased entry, a runnable example under `examples/triggers/` run in CI by a test (file-change trigger end to end with the scripted gateway; webhook trigger with a signed request to the loopback listener on an ephemeral port), release-note text for the behaviour changes.\nHostile tests (each fails when its guard is removed; mutate a copy of the source and restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): wrong/missing/expired/replayed signature, oversized body, slow body, bad Host, Origin present, non-loopback bind refused, changed spec after approval (not fired), revoked, quarantined, rate-limited, untrusted workspace, symlink escape in a path, `.git` events ignored, job-writes loop guard, event flood bounded (memory and fires), listener closed on shutdown (no leaked sockets: register with `trackResource`), watcher closed, runner with no triggers has no listener, event data in the prompt is delimited/capped, the job inherits unattended rules (no allow-always, read-only default, receipt has trigger fields), kill -9 of a triggered job's worker resumes without a duplicate fire for the same event id.\nRules: new test files start with `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\";`. Run one test file at a time: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (throwaway HOME mandatory; never read the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus; no network except loopback ephemeral ports in tests; no live inference: scripted gateway `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`/`makeHome`). Stop processes only by their own pid, never `pkill -f`; leave no runner/worker/listener behind. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` if cut); a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; Rust binaries are in `core/target/release/`. The complexity gate (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`) must not grow; keep `cli/src/index.ts` changes to command registration only. Schemas: follow the ledger rules (`docs/protocol/…VERSIONS.json`, generators with `--check`) for any receipt or status JSON you change, additive, version stays unless nothing shipped the old shape. Commit in 3-4 coherent commits `feat(triggers): …` each ending with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`; `git fetch origin` and check foreign commits before each commit.\nAt the end run: `npx tsc --noEmit -p .` in `cli/`, the complexity gate (check the exit code), `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`, schema `--check` generators, all new test files three times, and `leak_guard`, the BG-1/BG-2 files (`ls cli/test | grep -iE \"runner|job|schedule\"`), `fault_kill_mid_tool_call`, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `examples`, `protocol_schema`, `check_requirements`. The host is loaded: rerun timing failures alone before concluding. Report under 500 words: commits (hashes), design choices (limits, replay cache, loop guard, where it runs), tests with counts and the mutation that fails each, what the firer cannot protect against, schema changes, anything left open or any #803 trust-model issue you hit (report, do not fix #803's files except to adapt call sites)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":346539,"cache_read_input_tokens":32782,"output_tokens":6035,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":202},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":346539},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6035,"cache_read_input_tokens":32782,"cache_creation_input_tokens":346539,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":346539,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":26914,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015gLzGqgomZtnXaRaJAw82x":{"description":"Build BG-3 trigger firer","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Build the BG-3 trigger firer (event triggers: file-change watcher and webhook listener that start unattended jobs) in the worktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3 (Nexus Code, TypeScript CLI in `cli/`). The owner put BG-3 in scope for v1.3.0. The trust model is PR #803, already pushed to `origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` (head 3e5ab8e95) and under re-review: your branch must STACK on it. Start: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3 && git fetch origin && git status && git checkout -b v1/bg3-firer origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` (the worktree currently has local branch `v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model-cl` at the same commit). Commit locally on `v1/bg3-firer`; do NOT push, no PR (I review first). If #803 changes under review, `git fetch origin` and merge `origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` into your branch (never rebase). Release branch `origin/release/v1.3.0` is merged in often too.\nRead first, fully: `docs/TRIGGERS.md`; `cli/src/trigger_spec.ts`, `trigger_trust.ts`, `core/trigger_rate_limit.ts`, `triggers_cli.ts`