{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-25T00:15:37.879Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a806cb8075ea3a909</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01WwUwewVqCV1YMBBFUcN5Mq</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a806cb8075ea3a909.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Re-review PR #803 trust fixes\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS** at `3e5ab8e95dac0943003e46b88d5d968612f2b1fc`. The PR is still OPEN and the head has not moved. I found no blocker by your criteria.\n\n**Earlier blockers: all five closed, each by running the original attack**\n1. **Trust store protected.** I ran `write_file`, `edit_file`, `patch_file` and `delete_file` against it with the workspace set to HOME, `~/.config/nexus` and a repo. I tried `..`, an absolute path, a symlink to the file, a symlinked directory and case variants. All were refused and the store stayed intact.\n   - A hardlink to the store inside a workspace that is HOME bypasses `isPathProtected`, so `edit_file` and `delete_file` succeeded. Creating the link needs `run_command`, which can write the store directly anyway, so this is the documented shell limit. It is not blocking.\n2. **`approve` display.** It now prints verifier, paths, origins, rate, bounds and secret status, and the prompt in full. Control characters, ESC, bidi overrides, Unicode and surrogate pairs are all `\\u`-escaped. Without a pty, `echo yes |` refuses (exit 2). Under `script -qec` the approval works, and the docs now say so. Over-long fields (3,999 characters and up) are refused with a marker, and a 5,000-character prompt fails parsing. `--sha256` mismatch refuses.\n3. **Rate limiter.** Denials are no longer recorded: probes every 30 minutes on a 1/day trigger admitted again at minute 1470. 6,000 attempts took 34 ms. With 100k ids or 100k workspaces it held 1,024 limiters and 256 workspace windows, about 7 MB. Quarantine survives eviction. When the quarantine map is full, new triggers are denied (`limiter state full`) and recover after the cooldown. As a DoS lever it needs 4,096 live quarantines, i.e. thousands of approved triggers, so I judge it acceptable. Two workspaces with the same id get separate limits. Changing the rate applies in both directions.\n4. **`restore()`.** 47 tamper cases never admitted, including `__proto__`, 1e6-entry arrays, wrong types, Infinity, NaN, negatives, 1e308, a missing `until` and extra keys. A restored invalid limiter stayed denied 1,000 days later and cleared only by release. The 7 whole-limiter cases locked it. Truncated JSON is refused earlier by `parseStrictJson`.\n5. **Workspace cap.** It is exact: 5 admitted at 5/min. A cap denial does not quarantine the trigger. A trigger with a high rate can use the shared cap and starve its siblings, which is by design.\n\n**New findings (none blocking)**\n- `triggers_cli.ts:55`: the confirmation regex `/^yes$/i` plus `.trim()` approved `YES` and ` yes `. Run. You asked for literal `yes`, and the docs say type `yes`. Fix: `answer === \"yes\"`.\n- `trigger_rate_limit.ts:344-348`: `recordFailure` skips the quarantine cap. 1,000 distinct ids gave 1,000 quarantines against a cap of 3, so the \"at most 4,096\" claim is false. Run. Fix: apply the same full-map guard.\n- `trigger_rate_limit.ts:443,474`: LRU eviction resets the evicted trigger's or workspace's window, so the rate cap resets. Run. Per-trigger it is bounded by the workspace cap; evicting a workspace window clears that workspace's own cap, which needs 256 other workspaces firing. The header says this fails closed, which is true only for quarantines. Also `limiterFor` creates limiters for denied and unknown ids. Fix: create limiters only for approved ids, or keep entries with a live window.\n- `limiterKey` checks only that the path is absolute. A symlinked workspace path does not share a limit with its real path; the caller must pass the real path. Fix: `realpath` inside.\n- A rate-limit-triggered quarantine (3 denials, 15 minutes) can be re-forced by whoever can fire the trigger. The design accepts this.\n- The terminal prompt crashes with an unhandled `AbortError` on EOF. It fails closed, but the stack trace is ugly.\n- Docs: `worktree-write` is described as opt-in, but the CLI has no flag, so such specs are refused. The field cap counts JSON quotes and escapes.\n- The path printed on the first line of `approve` is unescaped.\n- A sparse `admittedAt` array is accepted, but JSON cannot produce one.\n\n**Other checks**\n- A 0644 spec is accepted. 0664 and 0666 are refused with a `chmod go-w` hint, and a symlinked spec is refused. A file owned by another non-root uid is refused in code; I could not test it.\n- Duplicate ids and a bad third spec approve nothing.\n- `NEXUS_TRUST_WORKSPACE=1` does not count for `approve`, `list` or `status`.\n- `secretEnv` works. A 1-character inline secret is refused and a 16-character one is accepted.\n- The docs example parses.\n\n**Tests at head**\nAll pass: rate_limit 55, self_approval 2, spec 24, trust 18, triggers_cli 21, security 14, workspace_trust 5, hooks_self_approval 10, leak_guard 6, check_requirements 28, protocol_schema 8. `tsc`, the complexity gate (exit 0), `check_invariants` and `check_doc_versions` are clean.\n\n**Mutations**\nAll six failed tests, restored from copies with a clean tree afterwards:\n- Dropping the protected path: 2 failures.\n- Truncating the verifier in the display: 1 failure.\n- Skipping the terminal check: 1 failure.\n- Skipping the workspace cap: 2 failures.\n- Skipping validation in `restore`: 31 failures.\n- Recording denials: 2 failures.\n\n**Not checked**\n- Node 22.\n- `--sandbox` reachability.\n- A root-owned or other-uid store or spec.\n- The firer, which does not exist.\n\nI removed the probes and the worktree, and I did not touch the real HOME.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>97913</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>31</tool_uses><duration_ms>536543</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
