kspace file (output `EVILSH`).\n  - Cause: `hooks.ts` `shebangProblem` calls `.trim()`, which strips form feed, CR, VT and NBSP. The kernel skips only space and tab. It reads `\\f/bin/sh` as a path relative to the cwd, which is the workspace. A hostile repo ships a directory named `\\f` containing `bin/sh`.\n  - Fix: take the interpreter with the kernel's rule, `first.slice(2).replace(/^[ \\t]+/, \"\")` and split on `[ \\t]` only. Also refuse the line if it contains any control character or a byte above 0x7f. Add tests for `\\f`, `\\r`, `\\v` and `\\xa0`.\n  - A shebang ending in CRLF fails with ENOENT. That is safe.\n- **B2 (interpreter location): closed.** `trustedProgram` refused `/proc/self/cwd/bash`, `/proc/self/root/...`, `/dev/shm`, `/tmp`, `/var/tmp`, a symlink into the workspace and `python3.99`. PATH entries `.`, `:`, `/usr/bin::/bin`, relative `bin` and a workspace symlink all fell through to `/usr/bin/bash`. The spawned argv[0] is the absolute path. I did not run the case of a workspace that sits inside a trusted directory.\n- **S1 closed.** Deleting a 20,000-file tree passed in 97 ms with a longest event-loop gap of 13 ms. A nested `hooks.json` is refused. A symlink loop and a symlink to outside are not followed, and a symlink at the root returns null.\n- **S2 closed.** `/tmp/bash` is refused.\n- **S3 closed.** A FIFO is refused with ENOTREG in 1 ms, and `/dev/zero` is refused too.\n- **S4 closed.** Old `run-*` directories were swept. A young one, `other-old`, a `run-file` file and the `run-x` symlink were left alone, and the symlink target stayed intact. A second sweep within a minute did nothing.\n\n**New findings**\n- **Non-blocking:** `hook_runner.ts` `hookEnvironment` passes the parent's `NODE_PATH` through. The workspace cannot set it, but you asked that it not leak. Drop it from the allowlist.\n- **Non-blocking:** the interpreter and shebang are checked on the verified bytes, but the interpreter file itself is checked and then spawned later (TOCTOU). That is covered by the documented trust-base rule.\n- **Not a finding:** a script that runs `source ./x` loads an unpinned workspace file. `docs/HOOKS.md` documents this as the self-contained-file rule.\n- **Implicit loading:** none of the interpreters I could test loaded workspace code at startup. Python (`import x`, `sitecustomize`, a stdlib-shadowing `json.py`), node (`require`, `node_modules`, `.noderc`), perl (`use x`), bash (`.bashrc`) and sh (`.profile`) all ignored the marker files, and python's `sys.path` held only the private run dir. A second pass on explicit `source` and node `require` showed they fail or load as documented. ruby is not installed here, so it was refused as not found.\n- **Environment scrub:** the hook environment is an allowlist, and `BASH_ENV`, `ENV`, `PYTHONPATH`, `PERL5LIB`, `RUBYLIB`, `PYTHONSTARTUP`, `PERL5OPT`, `RUBYOPT` and `LD_*` did not appear. `NODE_OPTIONS` was rejected by node when I set it, so I did not confirm its scrubbing.\n\n**Tests**\n- All 23 requested files pass with 0 failures. `managed_policy_enforcement` ran 113 tests and `hooks_self_approval` ran 19.\n- `tsc` exited 0, the complexity gate exited 0, and `check_invariants` and `check_doc_versions` exited 0.\n\n**Mutations**\nEach was restored from a copy and each broke at least one test: dropping the shebang check (2 failures), PATH with relative entries (3), `/tmp` trusted (1), spawning the workspace path (2) and dropping `O_NONBLOCK` (1). No test covers the whitespace bypass, which is why B1 slipped through.\n\n**Not checked**\nThe operator-hook regression beyond the existing tests, a ruby run, a workspace under `/usr/local/bin`, and hard links. I also did not test the `#!perl -T -x` variants live.\n\nThe worktree and my probe files are removed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>65701</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>25</tool_uses><duration_ms>681249</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>\n</system-reminder>"}]}}
