m` experimental\n- `a8a8c64ed` merge of `origin/release/v1.3.0` (BG-3 and HK-3 landed meanwhile). The goal, security and operation tests still pass after it.\n\nThe read path (`nexus operation status`) lands in commit 2, not commit 1.\n\n**Findings**\n1. **Stale check, identity.** The new `cli/src/core/driver_identity.ts` records {pid, host, start ticks, boot id}. A goal counts as driven only when all four are present and match a live process on this host. Otherwise it pauses with one of these reasons: process gone, pid recycled, other host, other boot, identity unknown. Drive, `/goal` status and list, and `nexus operation status` and `--dag` now share this verdict. `/goal status` while another process drives reports \"Driven by pid N\" instead of throwing `OperationLockedError`. Tests are in `goal_driver_identity.test.ts`. Mutations that each fail it: dropping the host, ticks or boot requirement, the recycled-pid check, the other-host and other-boot checks, the derived read view, surfacing the lock error, and accepting a platform that cannot prove identity.\n2. **State tamper.** `defaultTaskStateDir()` is now a protected absolute path in `security.ts`. The test checks `isPathProtected` and that write, edit, read and delete are refused. Removing the entry fails it. Docs say plainly that an unsandboxed command can rewrite goal state, that integrity is file mode 0600, and that there is no signature.\n3. **Receipt gate.** Test added in `goal_pa3_completion.test.ts`; deleting the gate fails it.\n4. **Fingerprint.** `scheduler.ts` now builds the fingerprint from the redacted reason. A secret-shaped reason repeated 3 times now stalls as `NOT_CONVERGING`; plain reasons still accumulate and different reasons do not trip. Reverting the fix fails the secret test.\n5. **Token cap.** New `nexus operation budget &lt;id&gt; --max-total-tokens N|none` in `core/operation_budget.ts`. It takes the driver lock, writes an `OPERATION_BUDGET_CHANGED` journal event (who, when, old, new), applies from the next dispatch, and does not resume a paused goal. Removing the journal write fails its test. The cap is checked before every dispatch. The overshoot bound is one dispatched batch, not exact, and the docs say so. No marker exists for an agent-spawned process, so the command cannot refuse an agent that shells out; it is documented as an operator command.\n6. **Docs and labels.** `PA3_GOAL_LIFECYCLE.md`, `PA3_ACCEPTANCE.md`, `PLAN.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` corrected. `/team` and `nexus operation` lose \"[experimental]\" in `EXECUTION_CONVERGENCE.md` and `operation_cli.ts`. I lifted the label from `nexus operation` as well as `/team`, because the line you pointed at labels `nexus operation`. A `/team` and `/goal` section is added to `USER_MANUAL.md`. The stale-reason wording is fixed and both the pause and stale reasons are shown.\n\n**State schema.** v7 is unchanged. `activeStartTicks` is an optional additive field, with a note in the `operation_schema.ts` header. Records from the previous build have no ticks, read as unknown identity, and pause once.\n\n**Behaviour changes you may not expect**\n- A fresh plan with no identity at all and no started units may still start; `loop.ts` relies on that.\n- A goal sitting at an operator decision (BLOCKED) stays BLOCKED. Before, `/goal` showed it as PAUSED while drive never paused it.\n- Off Linux, an interrupted goal always pauses, because identity comes from `/proc`.\n\n**What an unsandboxed command can still do.** It runs with your rights and can rewrite goal state, caps, stall counts and driver identity, and it can run `nexus operation budget`. `--sandbox` blocks the shell writes.\n\n**Open.** Interrupted-effect reconciliation on restart and live-gateway qualification are still not claimed. I did not run the Node 22 and 24 pair.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>223152</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>84</tool_uses><duration_ms>1525492</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>\n</system-reminder>"}]}}
