{"parentUuid":"fec527e5-529e-48a1-b035-33650fdd4067","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNkYWmfAr3VnmHtZmxeP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TbfwwHAZ82vQ4jCouHbReM","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Hostile review PR #808 HK-3 completion","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent hostile review of GitHub PR #808 (HK-3 completion: workspace plugin loader fixes, migration notice and doctor, workspace manifest plugins) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 808 --json headRefOid` (expected `622c07fb…`), review exactly that, and say whether it moved or merged. Read the PR body, my earlier hostile review on #798 (`gh pr view 798 --comments`, \"Independent hostile re-review at `605e3d5c…`\") and the plan `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/HK_PLAN.md` section 4 (HK-3.1/3.2), `docs/PLUGINS.md`, `docs/SKILLS_AND_PLUGINS.md`, `cli/src/plugins.ts`, `plugins_cli.ts`, `cli/src/core/workspace_plugins.ts`, `content_trust.ts`, `plugin_store.ts`/`plugin_manifest.ts` (HK-2), `doctor_plugins.ts`, and how HK-1's merged code works (`cli/src/core/hooks.ts` `planProjectCommand`, shebang rules, verified-copy execution, trusted-program resolution; `security.ts` protected paths) because manifest hooks must inherit all of it. The PR makes three groups of changes (commits `e8f36f85a`, `3bd401b20`, `622c07fba`).\nVerify by RUNNING (throwaway HOME, temp workspaces, no network, no inference; scripted gateway `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts`):\nA. My earlier findings closed: (1) workspace = HOME: global/user plugin dirs load as global, not skipped; workspace dir `/ws` vs `/ws2` prefix confusion; a symlinked plugin dir; the PR notes that with workspace = HOME operator plugin directories load as the operator's WITHOUT approval although the agent's file tools can write them: assess how bad (can the agent write `~/.nexus/plugins/x.mjs` or `~/.config/nexus/plugins/x.mjs` with `write_file` in a HOME workspace and have it load next start? compare HK-1, which refuses the equivalent hooks file; recommend: refuse operator plugin dirs inside the workspace, or protect them): this is likely BLOCKING: run it. (2) approved plugin with failing imports: `load-failed` record, one notice per plugin, shown in `plugins list` and doctor, reason redacted/capped (put a secret-shaped string in the error message); the `loadFailed` field in the trust record: can a repo-shipped or agent-written trust record forge/clear it, and does a malformed `loadFailed` value break loading of other plugins or fail open? (3) hardened read: 50 MB file, symlink, hard link, FIFO, invalid UTF-8, BOM, `.ts`/`.mjs`/`.cjs`/`.js`; trust hashing through the same reader; TOCTOU re-hash after prompt (injected answer test). (4) the earlier blockers stay closed (race over several hundred loads: only verified bytes run from the private copy; siblings fail).\nB. Migration: first-session notice (capped, exact commands, no ANSI/escape injection from plugin file names: use names with control chars/ESC/newlines/bidi), doctor `plugins.workspace` warn/PASS semantics and remedy text, `docs/PLUGINS.md` claims vs code, CHANGELOG.\nC. Manifest plugins (`.nexus/plugins/<name>/nexus-plugin.json`): unapproved not run; approval by tree digest: every file accounted for; change ANY byte, add an unlisted file, add a symlink, swap a file for a symlink/FIFO/hard link/directory, rename, case-variant names, `..`/absolute paths in the manifest, duplicate keys/BOM/huge/deep JSON (strict reader), unknown keys, hook `command` shapes (the allowlist rules apply with the plugin dir as base: `[\"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"bash\",\"guard.sh\"]`, `[\"node\",\"--run\",\"x\"]`, `[\"npm\",\"run\",\"x\"]`, `env`, `-c`, absolute `/usr/bin/true`, a script outside the plugin dir, a script that is a symlink into the workspace); verified-copy execution (swap race with a concurrent writer: never runs swapped bytes), `#!` rule; hook ordering operator → manifest plugins → project; no source can remove/disable another's hooks; a plugin hook answering `allow` for a denied action has no effect (use the `hooks_gate_bypass` shape); workspace trust gate, read-only mode, managed policy (`plugins.disable_workspace`, `plugins.allow`) above approval; `plugins trust <dir>` terminal-only (non-TTY refuses; `--sha256` only pins; faked-terminal limit documented?), re-reads the tree after the prompt, records nothing for a policy-refused plugin; the trust store and the plugin directories as protected paths for the agent's file tools (`.nexus/plugins/<name>/` is inside the workspace so the agent CAN edit it: a change must make the plugin inactive and notify: verify), re-read on every tool call: performance impact (a 10k-file tree?) and DoS limits (size caps, file-count caps, depth caps on the tree walk). Manifest skills: validated and covered by approval but add nothing to any request: verify nothing is injected into the request (request_shape test) and the docs say so.\nRun at head, one at a time: the three new test files (`plugin_loader_hardening`, `plugin_migration`, `workspace_manifest_plugin`), `plugin_trust`, `plugins*`, `hooks_*` and `hook*` test files, `content_trust`, `strict_json`, `doctor_hooks`, `doctor_plugins` if present, `request_shape`, `skills*`, `workspace_trust`, `managed_policy_allowlists`, `managed_policy_enforcement`, `security`, `security_gates`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`, `doctor` (11 failures are known host failures on base: confirm the same set at base); `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate (check the exit code); `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the hooks/trust schema `--check` generators. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): skip the tree-digest comparison; accept an unlisted file; skip planProjectCommand for manifest hooks; spawn the workspace path instead of the verified copy; drop the terminal check; skip policy-above-approval; drop the `load-failed` write: each must fail a test.\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev808 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev808/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev808/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev808/core/target/release/`; for the doctor tests `npm --prefix cli run build` inside the review worktree. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev808/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never touch the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`; never delete anything outside your own temp dirs (no `rm -rf /dev/shm/nexus-*` globs). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. Remove probes and the worktree at the end. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); prefix `rtk proxy` if output looks filtered; a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\nReport under 550 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; A/B/C each with the attacks run; findings with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (workspace-controlled code running without that exact file/tree's approval, running unpinned or swapped bytes, a plugin hook admitting a denied action, an agent-written file loading as operator code, a policy refusal overridden by an approval, silent loss of existing plugins without a notice, are blocking; the documented unsandboxed-shell and same-uid limits are not); test counts; mutation results; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2380,"cache_read_input_tokens":472606,"output_tokens":3136,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_