e walk is capped at 64 files, 256 directories, 8 levels and 8 MiB with a stat-stamp cache; a louder \"NO LONGER IN FORCE\" notice once per session; approve/revoke/`noteExtensionLoad` run under an `O_EXCL` lock file naming pid and start time.\nRe-run the ORIGINAL attacks and try to get past each fix by RUNNING (throwaway HOME, temp workspaces, no network, no inference): (1) workspace = HOME, = parent of HOME, = `~/.config`, = `~/.nexus`, = a repo: agent-written files in both operator plugin dirs: not loaded as operator code with no approval (marker file check), the skip notice appears once; what does the agent's `write_file` do against both dirs in each workspace (protected paths, incl. symlinked HOME real-path spelling, `..`, case variants, hard links); with workspace = HOME the operator dir is treated as a workspace plugin dir needing approval: does the operator's own legitimately installed global plugin (e.g. from `nexus plugins install` HK-2 or hand-placed) silently STOP loading for users who start Nexus in HOME — is that a regression with a notice (is the notice clear and does `nexus plugins list`/doctor explain how to approve/fix), judge severity; `NEXUS_STATE_DIR`/`XDG_CONFIG_HOME` relocations, containment logic edge cases (`/ws` vs `/ws2`, trailing slashes, symlinked dirs, `..hid` named dirs: compare with the `..` prefix bug class of issue #800: is `startsWith(\"..\")` used anywhere in the new code?). (2) Escape injection: plugin dir names, file names, skill names, manifest `name` fields, hook names, error messages from failing plugins, `plugins trust <dir>` file list, doctor output, `plugins list`, JSON output modes (are `--json` outputs still valid JSON and carry raw names escaped as JSON strings: ok), names with ESC, CSI, OSC (`\\x1b]`), newline, CR, bidi (U+202E, U+2066), zero-width, U+0085, DEL, C1 controls (0x80-0x9f) and invalid UTF-8; assert on the exact bytes written; a name that is 10k characters; is every print site covered (grep for `console.`/`process.stderr.write` in the plugin/doctor code paths and list any site not using the helper). (3) Tree walk: caps enforced with the stated reasons (dirs 256, depth 8, files 64, bytes 8 MiB); the cache never serves stale content: change one byte keeping size and mtime (utimes) — ctime changes: detected?; replace a file by a hard link/symlink; add/remove a directory; touch only a directory; clock skew; two plugins sharing files; is the stamp-based cache a TOCTOU risk for the approval (approval digest computed from bytes actually hashed, and the execution from the verified copy: unchanged guarantee: re-run the swap race against manifest hooks with a concurrent writer). 100-inspection benchmark 100–130 ms: acceptable? (4) Lock: four concurrent writer processes (approve/revoke/noteExtensionLoad) lose no entry and never corrupt the store; a stale lock from a dead pid and from a recycled pid (start-time check) is broken safely; a lock file symlink or planted by the repo (is the lock under the operator config dir, not the workspace?); lock held forever = bounded wait and clear error; does the lock make any path fail open. (5) Regression: earlier fixes stay closed (race, siblings, load-failed record, hardened read, policy above approval, hook allowlist for manifest hooks, `ignored_allow`), docs claims vs code (`docs/PLUGINS.md`, `HOOKS.md`, CHANGELOG).\nRun at head, one at a time: `plugin_review_fixes`, `plugin_loader_hardening`, `plugin_migration`, `workspace_manifest_plugin`, `plugin_trust`, `plugins_dirs`, `workspace_trust`, every hook test (`ls cli/test | grep -iE hook`), `content_trust`, `strict_json`, `doctor_hooks`, `doctor` (build cli/dist first: `npm --prefix cli run build`), `request_shape`, `skills*`, `managed_policy_allowlists`, `managed_policy_enforcement`, `security`, `security_gates`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate (check the exit code); `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the schema `--check` generators. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): drop `operatorDirInWorkspace`; drop one protected path; skip the printable helper at one notice; raise a cap; make the cache ignore ctime; drop the lock: each must fail a test.\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev808b <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev808b/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev808b/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev808b/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev808b/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never touch the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`; never delete anything outside your own temp dirs (no glob deletes). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. Remove probes and the worktree at the end. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); prefix `rtk proxy` if output looks filtered; a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\nReport under 500 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; the blocker closed or not with the attack run; each non-blocking item closed or not; new findings with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (agent-written or workspace-controlled code running as operator code or without that exact file/tree's approval, running unpinned or swapped bytes, a plugin hook admitting a denied action, silent loss of existing operator plugins without a clear notice, a policy refusal overridden, a lock that fails open or corrupts the trust store, are blocking; the documented unsandboxed-shell and same-uid limits are not); test counts; mutation results; what you did not check."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChNnzHPTxhgJEAZhz6PN4","requestRef":"61b095a3