{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-25T01:44:32.431Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a83e289b881d1a539</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01Bhe6gFYgZNMm39P1h5kF16</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a83e289b881d1a539.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Delta review PA-3 fixes 4cd35eb92\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES** on the code at the release tip (`6598f1f69`). B1 is only closed for bwrap. Probes: `/var/tmp` temp dirs, real bwrap 0.13.1 and a real docker daemon. #809 closes the docker case. The probes did not check cancellation or reuse.\n\n**B1, bwrap, partly closed.**\n- Run: workspace = HOME, parent of HOME, elsewhere, and under HOME. `NEXUS_STATE_DIR` under HOME, in `/var/tmp`, symlinked, and relative. Both bwrap profiles, plus the worker launch.\n- In every case the command could not list, read, write or delete `tasks/*.json`. The general profile showed an empty tmpfs.\n- The read-only profile was already blocked by its read-only mounts, and the worker args carry the tmpfs mask.\n- **Blocking (run), `cli/src/sandbox.ts:36-43`:** the mask is only the `tasks` directory.\n  - With workspace = HOME, a sandboxed `mv ~/.nexus ~/.nexus.old` succeeded (rc=0).\n  - `mkdir -p ~/.nexus/tasks &amp;&amp; echo FORGED &gt; ~/.nexus/tasks/goal-forged.json` then created a forged goal file on the host.\n  - Fix: also mask or bind-protect the parent chain, or refuse a workspace that contains the state dir under `--sandbox`.\n- **Blocking (run), same function:** a state dir that does not exist at wrap time is skipped (`realpathSync` throws).\n  - With workspace = HOME, a sandboxed command created it on the host and planted `planted.json`.\n  - Fix: `mkdir` the directory (mode 0700) before wrapping.\n\n**B1, docker, not closed at the tip (blocking, run).**\n- At the tip the docker argv has no mask. With workspace = HOME, a sandboxed `cat` of `~/.nexus/tasks/goal-x.json` returned `ORIG`, and `echo PWN &gt;` left the host file reading `PWN`.\n- #809 adds the docker `--tmpfs` mask. With it applied locally, the read was blocked, the write failed read-only, and the host file stayed `ORIG`.\n- #809 has the same rename and not-yet-created gaps as bwrap.\n\n**S1 closed.** `watch`, `status --replay --json`, `--dag` and `status` all show PAUSED for a killed driver (that test passes). I did not run `/goal status` separately; the zombie test covers `list()`.\n\n**S2 closed.** A real zombie reads gone in both `processStartTicks` and `processRunning`. A live parent reads running. I did not run a D or T state process. Only Z and X are excluded in the code, so both stay alive by construction.\n\n**S3 closed.** A live foreign driver with no lock is refused, the error names the pid, and no unit runs. The refusal is only on `entry === \"existing\"`. A recycled pid, other host or other boot pauses rather than refuses. A driver that is this process is allowed.\n\n**S4 closed with gaps.**\n- A symlinked-HOME real path is refused, including for a new file.\n- A hard link to a state file is refused, and a symlink to one is too.\n- A legitimate multi-link file outside the state dir is unaffected.\n- **Suggestion (run):** the scan is not recursive, so a hard link to a file in a state subdirectory passed as unprotected.\n- **Suggestion (run):** with 5,000 state files, each file-tool check on any file with more than one link costs about 100 ms. It was 70 ms when the match was found and 1 ms for a plain file. Delete recursion (`protectedBelow`) calls this per entry. pnpm trees, which are heavily hard-linked, would crawl. Fix: cache the state dir's inode set for a short time.\n- The TOCTOU (a link created after the check) is inherent and unmitigated.\n\n**Tests.**\n- All runs passed except one flake: `goal_driver_identity` failed once, then passed 3 of 3 full runs and 15 of 15 filtered runs. The zombie setup raced with `ENOENT` on `/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/stat` at line 276.\n- Counts: `goal_driver_identity` 15, `goal_pa3_completion` 7, `goal_lifecycle` 10, `goal_ledger_propagation` 2, `sandbox_confine` 21, `sandbox_profiles` 16, `sandbox_cancel` 2.\n- Counts: `check_requirements` 28, `durable_continuity` 42, `leak_guard` 6, `security` 14, `runner_examples` 1, `schedule_examples` 1.\n- All 19 `operation_*` files passed (about 250 tests).\n- `tsc --noEmit` exit 0, complexity gate exit 0, invariants OK, doc versions OK.\n\n**Mutations (all caught).**\n\n| Mutation | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Drop general-branch mask | 7 `sandbox_confine` failures |\n| Drop read-only-branch mask | 2 failures |\n| Drop state dir from the mask list | 2 failures |\n| Drop realpath protected entry | 1 failure in `goal_pa3_completion` |\n| Drop dev+ino check | 1 failure in `goal_pa3_completion` |\n| Drop Z/X handling | 1 failure |\n| Drop live-driver refusal | 1 failure |\n\n**False doc sentences.**\n- At the tip, `PA3_GOAL_LIFECYCLE.md` says \"A sandboxed command can neither read nor write it\", and the CHANGELOG says the dir is \"masked inside `--sandbox`\". Both are false for docker, and for the rename and not-yet-created cases.\n- At the tip, `PA3_GOAL_LIFECYCLE.md` says Docker \"needs no mask unless the workspace contains the state directory\". That is false with workspace = HOME, which is the B1 case.\n- #809's manual line \"`--sandbox` hides the directory… even when the workspace is your home directory\" is false for the rename bypass.\n- \"Hard link refused\" omits state subdirectories.\n\n**Not checked.**\n- A real drive end to end.\n- Docker with a not-yet-created dir.\n- Cancel and kill behaviour inside the mask.\n- Node 22.\n\nThe probes and the worktree are removed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>94149</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>43</tool_uses><duration_ms>984157</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
