{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-25T02:12:56.494Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a3625e888ee2d5710</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_014VZCMq6FVmx7SjDCDdzouA</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a3625e888ee2d5710.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Re-review PR #808 HK-3 fixes\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS for `f375865edc2da5b1e646e3cc909b5fcb8e3b9ca1`. But PR #808 did not carry the fix, so the blocker is still open on `release/v1.3.0`.**\n\n**Head and merge state.** `gh pr view 808` reports head `ff6bdddb3c6a34d9508b32655334831ad48f8779`, not `f375865e`. The PR was merged at 2026-11-25T01:29:43Z (merge commit `39d796fad`). `7ca90de26` and `f375865ed` were committed at 01:31 and 01:32, after the merge. They exist only on `origin/v1/hk3-followups`, which is the current release tip plus the fix. At `ff6bddd`, `git grep operatorDirInWorkspace` finds nothing, so the original attack still works on the release branch. The fix needs a new PR into `release/v1.3.0`. I reviewed `f375865e`, and the worktree is removed.\n\n**Blocker (workspace = HOME): closed by run.**\n- No operator code ran in any workspace I tried: HOME, its parent, HOME through a symlink, trailing-slash HOME, `~/.config`, `~/.nexus`, and `~/.config/nexus` (each skips only the operator dir inside it). In a plain repo workspace both operator dirs still load.\n- `write_file`, `edit_file`, `delete_file`, `read_file` and `list_files` on both dirs were refused in repo and HOME workspaces. The exception is a symlinked HOME with the dirs absent, where the real-path spelling wrote both files (the protected paths list only the symlinked spelling). The loader's real-path containment still skips them afterwards, so this is defense-in-depth only.\n- Containment edge cases are right: `/ws` vs `/ws2`, `..hid`, trailing slash. The new code never uses `startsWith(\"..\")`. The #800 pattern remains in the old `isWorkspaceFile` (`workspace_trust.ts:39`).\n- A user who starts in HOME gets a stderr notice, once per text. `~/.nexus/plugins` files can then be re-approved as workspace plugins. `~/.config/nexus/plugins` cannot be approved at all, so those plugins stay dark. I rate this low severity and non-blocking. `plugins list` and doctor do not mention the skipped dirs. Suggest a doctor line.\n- HK-2 installed packages are not loaded by anything yet, so nothing regresses there. The plugin dirs ignore `NEXUS_STATE_DIR` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` in both loader and protection.\n\n**Non-blocking items from your review**\n- **Escape injection:** mostly closed. The loader notices, trust lists, `plugins list` and `plugins list --json` (valid JSON) showed no raw control bytes across ESC, OSC, CSI, newline, CR, bidi, zero-width, U+0085, DEL, C1, a 10k name and an invalid-UTF-8 filename. Manifest names and hook names are charset-validated anyway.\n- **Missed print sites:**\n  - `doctor.ts` `plugins.config` (~1243–1262) prints raw plugin file paths. ESC, CR, bidi and C1 reached doctor text output.\n  - `index.ts:1168` (`plugins revoke`) is unescaped.\n  - The HK-2 install/verify/list-installed output (`plugins_cli.ts:153–220`) is unescaped.\n  - `printableRefusal` handles C0/C1 only, not bidi (read, not run).\n  - `printable` also omits U+FFF9–FFFB and the U+E0000 tag characters.\n- **Tree walk:** closed. All four caps are enforced with their stated reasons. The 50,000-dir case now takes 31–38 ms, and 100 cached inspections took 47 ms.\n- **Cache:** it never served stale content in my tests. Changing a byte and restoring mtime, a hard link, a symlink, a shared hard-linked file and a chmod were all handled correctly. The kernel's timestamp granularity here is about 1 ms, so a same-tick write could in theory go unnoticed. Execution re-hashes at copy time (`hooks.ts:637–640`), so unpinned bytes cannot run. A concurrent-writer swap race over 514 tool calls never ran the evil script.\n- **Lock:** closed. Four writers lost nothing, and the store was never corrupted. Dead-pid and recycled-pid locks were broken safely. A symlink or live-holder lock fails closed after 10 s with a clear error. The lock sits under `~/.config/nexus`, not the workspace.\n  - Residual: stale takeover (`lockIsStale` then `unlink`) is not atomic. It did not reproduce in 12 rounds of 8 writers.\n  - Residual: if `/proc` is unavailable the pid check always reads as stale.\n- **Louder \"NO LONGER IN FORCE\" notice:** it covers hooks and manifest plugins only. A changed JS plugin file gets only the generic pending notice.\n\n**Tests:** 34 files, 452 tests, 451 pass, 0 fail, 1 skipped (`retention_git_hook`). `tsc` is clean. The complexity gate exits 0, as do `check_invariants`, `check_doc_versions` and the 10 `gen_*schema --check` runs.\n\n**Mutations:** 17 run, 14 killed. Dropping `operatorDirInWorkspace`, either protected path, any one cap, the ctime stamp and the lock were all killed. Three survived:\n- the unescaped `plugins list` in `index.ts`\n- the unescaped project-hooks notice\n- the root-directory-only ctime stamp\n\n**Not checked:**\n- the docs claim that sandboxed commands cannot reach the dirs\n- hard-link writes (they need a shell)\n- Node 22 vs 24\n- the TUI rendering of notices\n- macOS\n- `gen_protocol_samples`</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>190946</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>101</tool_uses><duration_ms>1960796</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
