{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-25T03:43:33.776Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a88f34e8c89df42f5</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_019jNPievvCek8EkpiR6bFn9</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a88f34e8c89df42f5.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Build BG-3 trigger firer\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>`v1/bg3-firer` is pushed (plain push, no force). It is a first push, so there is no review state yet.\n\n- **Gaps in the checks:**\n  - `release/v1.3.0` has moved again since my merge (#811 PA-3, sandbox tasks mask). I did not re-merge it or re-run on top of it.\n  - I did not run `nexus triggers` on Node 22 (only the host's Node 24.11).\n  - `--sandbox` reachability is untested.\n- **Failure I fixed:** `runner_store` failed once because my new `trigger` origin changed an error message its test asserted. I updated the test, and it passes.\n- **Final results on the merged state:**\n  - `tsc` and the schema `--check` generators are clean.\n  - The complexity gate exited 0.\n  - `check_invariants` and `check_doc_versions` are clean.\n  - Every other file on your list passes (BG-1/BG-2/runner/schedule, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `fault_kill_mid_tool_call`, `examples`, `protocol_schema`, `leak_guard`).\n  - The 8 new test files pass three times each, after `npm run build`.\n\n**Commits:**\n- `26b6c1237`: the #803 follow-ups (the six you listed; each has a test).\n- `34b7931c7`: trigger origin, receipt fields, schemas and ledgers, path rules, run planning, state, `nexus-triggers*` protected path.\n- `c283765c2`: firer, watcher, webhook listener, runner integration, `nexus triggers status|release|fire-test`.\n- `66f2ec4d1`: docs, `examples/triggers`, real-runner tests.\n- `791bec9ae`: mutation-driven tests.\n- `164d0e511`: merge of the release branch.\n- `0d625cfff`: the `runner_store` test fix.\n\n**Design:**\n- **Where it runs:** the firer is a loop inside `nexus runner serve`, next to the schedule engine. It submits through `buildJobSubmission` and the runner's own acceptance, so there is no second execution path.\n- **Flags:** `--webhook-port`, `--webhook-host`, `--webhook-allow-remote`, `--no-triggers`.\n- **No-trigger runner:** with no approved trigger the runner opens no watcher, socket or state, and no worker inherits the webhook secret variable.\n- **Re-check at every fire:** the approval and the spec file are re-read and re-hashed once per fire; it fires only from that parse.\n- **Fire order:** a repeated event id and an overlapping job are dropped first, then approval and trust, then the limiter, then submit.\n- **Loop guard:** `.git`, `.nexus` and `nexus-triggers` paths never count, and events are dropped and counted while the trigger's previous job runs.\n- **Watcher limits:** 256 directories per trigger, 1,024 in total, 1,000 pending paths, a 2-second window; a symlink out of the workspace is dropped and counted.\n- **Webhook limits:**\n  - 64 KiB body, 8 KiB headers, 10 s request timeout, 32 connections, 1 request per socket.\n  - HMAC-SHA256 over `timestamp.delivery.body` with a 5-minute skew; every pre-auth refusal returns an identical 401.\n  - The limiter runs only after authentication.\n- **Replay cache:** in memory, 4,096 entries for 10 minutes, and it fails closed when full. Restart replays are closed by refusing any timestamp earlier than the listener's start, plus an existing-job check on the event id.\n- **Limiter state:** private (0600 file in a 0700 dir) at `runner/nexus-triggers/state.json`, restored with the validating `restore`; a refused state denies all firings until `release --limiter`.\n\n**Tests and mutations:**\n- 8 new files with 78 tests. The #803 follow-ups sit in `trigger_followups` (7), the CLI additions in `trigger_status_cli` (5), and the full list of files is in the commit history.\n- About 60 mutations, each fails its guard when removed.\n- Equivalent mutants (a removal that cannot change behaviour):\n  - the declared `Content-Length` check (the streamed cap does the same job)\n  - the dummy-key HMAC\n  - the fire-time `specPathProblem`\n  - the symlinked-directory check\n\n**What the firer cannot protect against:**\n- A faked terminal can approve or release.\n- A process under your account can read the secret from `/proc`, rewrite state, and send signed deliveries.\n- Event text can still steer the model; the job's read-only, no-approval rules are the limit.\n- Events are not durable: those dropped by rate, overlap or quarantine are never replayed.\n- The webhook is plain HTTP, and a clock set back reopens a rate window.\n\n**Schema changes:** `job-v1`, `runner-files-v1`, `job-receipt-v1` and `job-status-v1` change additively (version stays 1, ledger entries added). A job's `origin` gains a `trigger` variant, and the receipt gains optional `trigger_id` and `trigger_event`.\n\n**Open items:**\n- **#803 limiter issue:** `consecutiveFailures` resets on every admission, so job failures reported through `recordFailure` never reach 3 in a row. Only rate denials quarantine. I left `trigger_rate_limit.ts` alone and documented it.\n- `bounds.maxRounds` maps directly to `maxTotalSteps`, so a default of 5 is tight; the docs say to use 10 or more.\n- `nexus-trigger-state` has no published schema.\n- The example tests need a fresh `npm run build`, or the shim fails on `tsx`; this already affects `schedule_examples`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>433805</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>3</tool_uses><duration_ms>74660</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
