{"parentUuid":"3f25baae-88bd-494e-b156-64208bf4add2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"5a1afd13-5f5c-4f12-8664-a0e6557e0d7b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TuWTuLGSM1AddFdEp26wLC","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: a42201ac5976186d5 (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'a42201ac5976186d5', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a42201ac5976186d5.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"5135b5aa-cec7-4121-9774-d9c5899c9738","timestamp":"2026-11-25T03:44:31.646Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"a42201ac5976186d5","description":"Hostile review PR #812 BG-3 firer","resolvedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","prompt":"Independent hostile security review of GitHub PR #812 (DRAFT: BG-3 trigger firer: file-change watcher and signed webhook listener that start unattended jobs through the runner) in ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 812 --json headRefOid` (expected to start `71dcc9b8`), review exactly that, and say whether it moved. This is the authority boundary for the whole BG-3 track: judge whether a hostile repository, a model with sandboxed tools, a local process, or a remote webhook caller can make a job run that the operator did not approve, escalate what a triggered job may do, exhaust the host, or learn the secret. Read the PR body, `docs/TRIGGERS.md`, `docs/RUNNER.md`, `docs/SCHEDULES.md`, the earlier hostile reviews of the trust model on #803 (`gh pr view 803 --comments`: the list \"What the firer must add\"), `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` BG-3 row, and the code: `cli/src/core/trigger_*.ts` (firer, watch, webhook, state, paths, run, rate limit, spec, trust, load), the runner integration in `cli/src/core/job_runner.ts`, `job_submission.ts`, `job_receipt.ts`, `runner_store.ts`, `background_cli.ts`, `triggers_cli.ts`, `security.ts` protected paths, the schema ledgers. Attack by RUNNING (throwaway HOME, temp workspaces, loopback ephemeral ports only, no live inference: scripted gateway `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`/`makeHome`):\n1. Webhook: wrong/missing/malformed signature, signature over a different body/timestamp/delivery id, timestamp skew ±5 min and exactly at the edge, far future, negative, non-numeric; replay of a valid delivery (same delivery id within and after 10 minutes; replay cache full: fails closed: can an attacker fill 4,096 entries with VALID-signature deliveries only: requires the secret; can unauthenticated requests fill it (must not)); restart replay (timestamp before listener start); oversized body (declared Content-Length vs streamed; chunked), slow-loris (headers, body), 8 KiB header cap, many connections (cap 32), pipelining/keep-alive abuse (1 request per socket), HTTP request smuggling shapes (CL+TE, duplicate CL, obs-fold headers, absolute-form URI, `Expect: 100-continue`), method confusion (GET/HEAD/OPTIONS/CONNECT), path confusion (`//`, `..`, encoded `%2e%2e`, trailing slash, case, query strings, unknown trigger ids, ids with unusual characters), every pre-auth refusal must be byte-identical (status, headers, body, and timing: measure timing for unknown trigger vs known trigger with a bad signature: constant-time compare, a dummy key for unknown: statistically indistinguishable?), response does not reveal whether a trigger exists, DNS-rebinding/Host header and `Origin` handling (browser-style request to loopback: refused), non-loopback bind refused without `--webhook-allow-remote` and `--webhook-allow-remote` requires authentication configured; IPv6/`::1`, `localhost` resolution quirks, binding to `0.0.0.0` via `--webhook-host`; port already in use, port 0; the listener closes on runner shutdown, no leaked sockets; secrets: read from the environment variable only, not logged, not in `state.json`, not in receipts/flight/telemetry/error messages, not inherited by workers or by the model's commands (check the worker environment of a triggered job: the variable absent; and with `--sandbox` the `/proc` reachability caveat is documented), inline secrets in the spec (>=16 chars) are exposed in the repository file: documented? The event payload (body) given to the job: delimited, size-capped, escaped, never the job instruction; try body text that imitates the delimiter or an instruction (\"ignore previous instructions\", fake tool results) and say what the job sees.\n2. File watcher: events from `.git`, `.nexus`, `nexus-triggers`, the spec file itself, the job's own worktree (loop guard: can a triggered job's own writes re-trigger it indefinitely, directly or through another trigger on the same paths (trigger A's job writes the files trigger B watches and B's job writes A's: a cycle across triggers!)? is there a global/chain guard or only per-trigger? measure fires over time: bounded by rate limit? say the worst-case rate), symlink out of the workspace (dropped and counted), a symlink swapped in after approval, rename storms, 100k file creates (memory bound, pending cap 1,000, 2 s window), huge directory trees (256 per trigger / 1,024 total), inotify limit errors reported not swallowed, files with newline/ESC/bidi in names appearing in event text and receipts and `nexus triggers status` (escaped?), paths outside the approved globs, glob edge cases (`**`, leading `/`, `..`, `~`, Windows separators, brace, negation).\n3. Fire path authority: a trigger spec changed after approval (byte, whitespace, key order, duplicate keys): not fired, notice; revoked; quarantined; rate-limited; workspace untrusted (`NEXUS_TRUST_WORKSPACE=1` must not count); managed policy (`approval.*`, `sandbox.forbid_network`, `plugins.*`) applies to the triggered job exactly as to `nexus job`; the triggered job is read-only by default, fresh worktree, no auto, no allow-always, receipts carry `trigger_id`/`trigger_event`; can the spec request `worktree-write` (is it refused as the docs say) and can an event or a spec field widen the job's level, verifier, budgets (`maxRounds`/`maxTotalSteps` caps, token cap), tools, model, workspace (another workspace path!), env, or the operator verifier (string validated by `verifierProblem`)? Can the model (file tools) edit the spec, state file (`runner/nexus-triggers/state.json`), or the trust store (protected paths, symlinked HOME, workspace = HOME) to approve/unquarantine/reset a limiter; `nexus triggers release --limiter` and `approve` terminal-only (non-TTY refuses; faked-terminal limit documented); `fire-test` really fires nothing and starts no job and opens no listener; `triggers status` on a corrupt state fails closed.\n4. Limiter and state: `consecutiveFailures` resets on every admission (documented open item: judge if job failures can never quarantine and what that means for a flood of failing triggered jobs: cost/DoS bound); state file corruption/tamper (restore validates: refused state denies all firings until release), concurrent runner instances (two `nexus runner serve` processes: is there a single-runner lock; do two firers double-fire the same event?), clock stepping, persistence across restart (cooldowns survive?), per-workspace cap, memory growth with attacker-chosen event ids/paths/delivery ids (bounded?).\n5. Resource and DoS: a flood of authenticated webhooks (rate limiter after auth: what does an authenticated attacker cost per request: CPU, disk (job records!), queue (runner queue 32): can it fill the disk with job directories (retention?), can it starve schedules/other jobs; per-trigger and global bounds). A flood of unauthenticated requests (cost per request).\n6. No-trigger runner: with no approved trigger the runner opens no watcher, socket or state file: verify (listening sockets via `ss -ltnp` filtered to the runner pid, open inotify fds, files created); the runner with `--no-triggers`.\n7. Docs and claims: every sentence in `docs/TRIGGERS.md` and the PR body vs code; the schema ledger additions are additive and the generators' `--check` pass; old receipts/jobs still parse.\nRun at head, one at a time: all new/changed trigger test files (`git diff --stat origin/release/v1.3.0...HEAD -- cli/test`), `trigger_*` and `triggers_*` tests, the runner/job/schedule files (`ls cli/test | grep -iE \"runner|job|schedule\"`), `fault_kill_mid_tool_call`, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `protocol_schema`, `run_json_contract`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`, `runner_examples`/`schedule_examples`/the triggers example test (after `npm --prefix cli run build` inside the review worktree); `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate (check the exit code); `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the schema `--check` generators. Mutations (copy aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): skip signature check; skip timestamp skew; skip replay cache; accept bigger bodies; non-loopback bind allowed; limiter after parse instead of after auth; re-hash at fire skipped; loop guard off; symlink-out check off; protected path for state off: each must fail a test.\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev812 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev812/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev812/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev812/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev812/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`. Never touch the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus, /etc/nexus; loopback only; no outbound network. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`; never delete anything outside your own temp dirs (no glob deletes). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. Remove probes and the worktree at the end. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); prefix `rtk proxy` if output looks filtered; a safety check blocks scripts containing `rm`.\nReport under 600 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; findings with file:line, run-or-read, blocking or not (a trigger firing that was not approved or whose spec changed, a forged or replayed webhook starting a job, any widening of a triggered job's authority, a secret exposure, an unbounded resource consumption by an unauthenticated peer, or a trigger-to-trigger infinite cycle without a global bound, are blocking; the documented unsandboxed-shell and same-uid limits are not); test counts; mutation results; what you did not check.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a42201ac5976186d5.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3f25baae-88bd-494e-b156-64208bf4add2","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"v1/bg3-trust-abuse-model","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
